  'lower-bound', 'problem', 'Lower Bound (Binary Search)', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/implement-lower-bound/1', 'medium', 'Binary Search', 'Lower Bound / First index with a[i] ≥ x',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given a **sorted** array `a` and a value `x`, return the **first index** `i` such that `a[i] >= x`.\nIf no such index exists, return `n` (the array length).","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\n- `a`: sorted integer array\n- `x`: integer target\n\n### Output\n- First index `i` with `a[i] >= x`, else `n`","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"```txt\na = [1, 3, 3, 5, 8], x = 4\noutput = 3\n```","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"`a[3] = 5` is the first value `>= 4`.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Just find the first position where the condition becomes true.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"Scan from left to right; return the first index with `a[i] >= x`. If none, return `n`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"public class LowerBoundLinear {\n    // Returns the first index i such that a[i] >= x, or n if it doesn''t exist.\n    public static int lowerBound(int[] a, int x) {\n        for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {\n            if (a[i] >= x) return i;\n        }\n        return a.length;\n    }\n}\n"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"- **Time `O(N)`**: in the worst case, you may scan all `n` elements.\n- **Space `O(1)`**: only a few variables are used.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Think of **“first true”** in a monotonic boolean array.\n\nBecause the array is sorted, the predicate `a[i] >= x` becomes:\n\n`false false false ... true true true`\n\nThe lower bound is the first index where it becomes `true`.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"Maintain a search space `[lo, hi)` such that the answer is always inside it.\n\n- Invariant: all indices `< lo` are **definitely false** (`a[i] < x`)\n- Invariant: all indices `>= hi` are **definitely true** (or “past the array”)\n- Shrink until `lo == hi` → that index is the first `true`","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"public class LowerBoundBinarySearch {\n    // Returns the first index i such that a[i] >= x, or n if it doesn''t exist.\n    public static int lowerBound(int[] a, int x) {\n        int lo = 0, hi = a.length; // [lo, hi)\n        while (lo < hi) {\n            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n            if (a[mid] >= x) {\n                hi = mid;   // mid might be the answer\n            } else {\n                lo = mid + 1; // answer is strictly right of mid\n            }\n        }\n        return lo;\n    }\n}\n"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(log N)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"- **Time `O(log N)`**: binary search halves the range each step.\n- **Space `O(1)`**: iterative binary search uses constant extra space.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Scan left to right; first a[i] >= x is the answer.\n- [ ] If none found, return n.\n- [ ] Time O(N), Space O(1)."}]}', 
  '["Model as first-true over predicate a[i] >= x in sorted array.","Use [lo, hi) and shrink until lo == hi.","Time O(log N), Space O(1)."]', '[]', '[]',
  'lower-bound
Lower Bound (Binary Search)
Binary Search
Lower Bound / First index with a[i] ≥ x
Given a **sorted** array `a` and a value `x`, return the **first index** `i` such that `a[i] >= x`.
If no such index exists, return `n` (the array length).
- `a`: sorted integer array
- `x`: integer target
- First index `i` with `a[i] >= x`, else `n`
```txt
a = [1, 3, 3, 5, 8], x = 4
output = 3
```
`a[3] = 5` is the first value `>= 4`.
Just find the first position where the condition becomes true.
Scan from left to right; return the first index with `a[i] >= x`. If none, return `n`.
- **Time `O(N)`**: in the worst case, you may scan all `n` elements.
- **Space `O(1)`**: only a few variables are used.
Think of **“first true”** in a monotonic boolean array.

Because the array is sorted, the predicate `a[i] >= x` becomes:

`false false false ... true true true`

The lower bound is the first index where it becomes `true`.
Maintain a search space `[lo, hi)` such that the answer is always inside it.

- Invariant: all indices `< lo` are **definitely false** (`a[i] < x`)
- Invariant: all indices `>= hi` are **definitely true** (or “past the array”)
- Shrink until `lo == hi` → that index is the first `true`
- **Time `O(log N)`**: binary search halves the range each step.
- **Space `O(1)`**: iterative binary search uses constant extra space.
Scan left to right; first a[i] >= x is the answer.
If none found, return n.
Time O(N), Space O(1).
Model as first-true over predicate a[i] >= x in sorted array.
Use [lo, hi) and shrink until lo == hi.
Time O(log N), Space O(1).', '2026-04-05T10:35:58.283Z'
);

