  'jump-game-ii', 'problem', 'Jump Game II', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/jump-game-ii/', 'medium', 'Greedy', 'Range expansion (BFS levels)',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"You are given an array `nums` where `nums[i]` is the maximum jump length from index `i`.\nStarting at index `0`, return the **minimum number of jumps** needed to reach the last index.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\n- `nums`: integer array\n\n### Output\n- Minimum jumps to reach `n - 1`","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"```txt\nnums = [2, 3, 1, 1, 4]\noutput = 2\n```","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Jump `0 -> 1`, then `1 -> 4`.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"If we know the best answer to reach smaller indices, we can build the best answer for later indices.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"Let `dp[i]` be the minimum jumps to reach index `i`.\nFor each `i`, try all `j < i` that can reach `i`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class JumpGameIIBruteForce {\n    public static int jump(int[] nums) {\n        int n = nums.length;\n        int[] dp = new int[n];\n        Arrays.fill(dp, (int)1e9);\n        dp[0] = 0;\n\n        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {\n                if (j + nums[j] >= i) {\n                    dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], dp[j] + 1);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return dp[n - 1];\n    }\n}\n"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N^2)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"- **Time `O(N^2)`**: for every `i`, you scan all earlier `j`.\n- **Space `O(N)`**: the DP array stores answers for all indices.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Every index you can reach with `k` jumps forms a **range**.\nFrom that range, one more jump can expand to a new range.\n\nTrack:\n\n- `currentEnd`: end of the current jump range\n- `farthest`: farthest index reachable from within the current range","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"Scan once, maintaining the current “level” end.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"public class JumpGameIIOptimal {\n    public static int jump(int[] nums) {\n        int n = nums.length;\n        if (n <= 1) return 0;\n\n        int jumps = 0;\n        int currentEnd = 0;\n        int farthest = 0;\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n            farthest = Math.max(farthest, i + nums[i]);\n\n            // We reached the end of the current jump range: take a jump.\n            if (i == currentEnd) {\n                jumps++;\n                currentEnd = farthest;\n            }\n        }\n        return jumps;\n    }\n}\n"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"- **Time `O(N)`**: each index is processed once.\n- **Space `O(1)`**: only a few pointers/counters are used.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] DP: dp[i] = min jumps to i; try all j<i that can reach i.\n- [ ] Simple but slow for large N.\n- [ ] Time O(N^2), Space O(N)."}]}', 
  '["Greedy BFS-level view: currentEnd is current range, farthest is next range.","When i hits currentEnd, take a jump and extend to farthest.","Time O(N), Space O(1)."]', '[]', '[]',
  'jump-game-ii
Jump Game II
Greedy
Range expansion (BFS levels)
You are given an array `nums` where `nums[i]` is the maximum jump length from index `i`.
Starting at index `0`, return the **minimum number of jumps** needed to reach the last index.
- `nums`: integer array
- Minimum jumps to reach `n - 1`
```txt
nums = [2, 3, 1, 1, 4]
output = 2
```
Jump `0 -> 1`, then `1 -> 4`.
If we know the best answer to reach smaller indices, we can build the best answer for later indices.
Let `dp[i]` be the minimum jumps to reach index `i`.
For each `i`, try all `j < i` that can reach `i`.
- **Time `O(N^2)`**: for every `i`, you scan all earlier `j`.
- **Space `O(N)`**: the DP array stores answers for all indices.
Every index you can reach with `k` jumps forms a **range**.
From that range, one more jump can expand to a new range.

Track:

- `currentEnd`: end of the current jump range
- `farthest`: farthest index reachable from within the current range
Scan once, maintaining the current “level” end.
- **Time `O(N)`**: each index is processed once.
- **Space `O(1)`**: only a few pointers/counters are used.
DP: dp[i] = min jumps to i; try all j<i that can reach i.
Simple but slow for large N.
Time O(N^2), Space O(N).
Greedy BFS-level view: currentEnd is current range, farthest is next range.
When i hits currentEnd, take a jump and extend to farthest.
Time O(N), Space O(1).', '2026-04-15T08:33:07.412Z'
);

