  'n-queen', 'problem', 'N-Queen', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/n-queens/', 'medium', 'Backtracking', 'Row-by-row + hashing (cols/diagonals)',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Place `n` queens on an `n x n` chessboard such that **no two queens attack each other**.\nReturn **all distinct solutions**, where each solution is a board of `''.''` and `''Q''`.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\n- `n`: board size and number of queens\n\n### Output\n- List of all valid boards","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"```txt\nn = 4\noutput = 2 solutions\n```","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"There are exactly 2 non-attacking configurations for `n = 4`.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Try to place queens row by row; for each placement, check if it conflicts with earlier queens.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"Backtrack row by row. To validate a placement, scan up-left, up-right, and up (column).","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic class NQueensBruteForce {\n    public static List<List<String>> solveNQueens(int n) {\n        char[][] board = new char[n][n];\n        for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {\n            for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) board[r][c] = ''.'';\n        }\n\n        List<List<String>> ans = new ArrayList<>();\n        backtrack(0, board, ans);\n        return ans;\n    }\n\n    private static void backtrack(int row, char[][] board, List<List<String>> ans) {\n        int n = board.length;\n        if (row == n) {\n            ans.add(toList(board));\n            return;\n        }\n\n        for (int col = 0; col < n; col++) {\n            if (isSafe(row, col, board)) {\n                board[row][col] = ''Q'';\n                backtrack(row + 1, board, ans);\n                board[row][col] = ''.'';\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    private static boolean isSafe(int row, int col, char[][] board) {\n        int n = board.length;\n        // Check column\n        for (int r = 0; r < row; r++) if (board[r][col] == ''Q'') return false;\n        // Diagonal up-left\n        for (int r = row - 1, c = col - 1; r >= 0 && c >= 0; r--, c--)\n            if (board[r][c] == ''Q'') return false;\n        // Diagonal up-right\n        for (int r = row - 1, c = col + 1; r >= 0 && c < n; r--, c++)\n            if (board[r][c] == ''Q'') return false;\n        return true;\n    }\n\n    private static List<String> toList(char[][] board) {\n        List<String> out = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (char[] row : board) out.add(new String(row));\n        return out;\n    }\n}\n"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N! · N)","space":"O(N^2)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"- **Time ~`O(N! · N)`**: backtracking explores permutations of columns; safety checks cost `O(N)` by scanning.\n- **Space `O(N^2)`**: the board is stored as an `n x n` grid.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"When you place a queen at `(row, col)`, you block:\n\n- the column `col`\n- the main diagonal `(row - col)`\n- the anti-diagonal `(row + col)`\n\nHash these constraints to get O(1) safety checks.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"Use boolean arrays for O(1) safety checks:\n\n- `cols[c]`\n- `diag1[row - col + (n - 1)]`\n- `diag2[row + col]`","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic class NQueensOptimal {\n    public static List<List<String>> solveNQueens(int n) {\n        char[][] board = new char[n][n];\n        for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {\n            for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) board[r][c] = ''.'';\n        }\n\n        boolean[] cols = new boolean[n];\n        boolean[] diag1 = new boolean[2 * n - 1]; // row - col + (n - 1)\n        boolean[] diag2 = new boolean[2 * n - 1]; // row + col\n\n        List<List<String>> ans = new ArrayList<>();\n        backtrack(0, board, cols, diag1, diag2, ans);\n        return ans;\n    }\n\n    private static void backtrack(\n            int row,\n            char[][] board,\n            boolean[] cols,\n            boolean[] diag1,\n            boolean[] diag2,\n            List<List<String>> ans\n    ) {\n        int n = board.length;\n        if (row == n) {\n            ans.add(toList(board));\n            return;\n        }\n\n        for (int col = 0; col < n; col++) {\n            int d1 = row - col + (n - 1);\n            int d2 = row + col;\n            if (cols[col] || diag1[d1] || diag2[d2]) continue;\n\n            cols[col] = diag1[d1] = diag2[d2] = true;\n            board[row][col] = ''Q'';\n            backtrack(row + 1, board, cols, diag1, diag2, ans);\n            board[row][col] = ''.'';\n            cols[col] = diag1[d1] = diag2[d2] = false;\n        }\n    }\n\n    private static List<String> toList(char[][] board) {\n        List<String> out = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (char[] row : board) out.add(new String(row));\n        return out;\n    }\n}\n"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N!)","space":"O(N^2)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"- **Time ~`O(N!)`**: same backtracking search space, but safety checks are O(1).\n- **Space `O(N^2)`**: board plus O(N) hashing arrays.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Backtrack row-by-row; validate by scanning column/diagonals each time.\n- [ ] Safety check costs O(N) per placement attempt.\n- [ ] Time ~O(N!·N), Space O(N^2)."}]}', 
  '["Backtrack row-by-row; hash used cols/diagonals with boolean arrays.","Safety check becomes O(1).","Time ~O(N!), Space O(N^2)."]', '[]', '[]',
  'n-queen
N-Queen
Backtracking
Row-by-row + hashing (cols/diagonals)
Place `n` queens on an `n x n` chessboard such that **no two queens attack each other**.
Return **all distinct solutions**, where each solution is a board of `''.''` and `''Q''`.
- `n`: board size and number of queens
- List of all valid boards
```txt
n = 4
output = 2 solutions
```
There are exactly 2 non-attacking configurations for `n = 4`.
Try to place queens row by row; for each placement, check if it conflicts with earlier queens.
Backtrack row by row. To validate a placement, scan up-left, up-right, and up (column).
- **Time ~`O(N! · N)`**: backtracking explores permutations of columns; safety checks cost `O(N)` by scanning.
- **Space `O(N^2)`**: the board is stored as an `n x n` grid.
When you place a queen at `(row, col)`, you block:

- the column `col`
- the main diagonal `(row - col)`
- the anti-diagonal `(row + col)`

Hash these constraints to get O(1) safety checks.
Use boolean arrays for O(1) safety checks:

- `cols[c]`
- `diag1[row - col + (n - 1)]`
- `diag2[row + col]`
- **Time ~`O(N!)`**: same backtracking search space, but safety checks are O(1).
- **Space `O(N^2)`**: board plus O(N) hashing arrays.
Backtrack row-by-row; validate by scanning column/diagonals each time.
Safety check costs O(N) per placement attempt.
Time ~O(N!·N), Space O(N^2).
Backtrack row-by-row; hash used cols/diagonals with boolean arrays.
Safety check becomes O(1).
Time ~O(N!), Space O(N^2).', '2026-04-05T10:35:58.388Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
