  'find-leaf-nodes', 'problem', 'Find Leaf Nodes (Tree)', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/print-leaf-nodes-binary-tree-right-left/', 'medium', 'Trees', 'DFS/BFS traversal',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given the root of a binary tree, return (or print) all **leaf nodes**.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\n- `root`: binary tree root\n\n### Output\n- List of leaf values (any order depending on traversal)","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"```txt\n    1\n   / \\\n  2   3\n /\n4\n\noutput = [4, 3]\n```","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Nodes `4` and `3` have no children.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Traverse all nodes; whenever you see a node with no children, it’s a leaf.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"Level-order (BFS): visit all nodes and collect leaves.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.LinkedList;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Queue;\n\npublic class FindLeafNodesBFS {\n    static class TreeNode {\n        int val;\n        TreeNode left, right;\n        TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }\n    }\n\n    public static List<Integer> leafNodes(TreeNode root) {\n        List<Integer> leaves = new ArrayList<>();\n        if (root == null) return leaves;\n\n        Queue<TreeNode> q = new LinkedList<>();\n        q.add(root);\n        while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n            TreeNode node = q.poll();\n            if (node.left == null && node.right == null) {\n                leaves.add(node.val);\n                continue;\n            }\n            if (node.left != null) q.add(node.left);\n            if (node.right != null) q.add(node.right);\n        }\n        return leaves;\n    }\n}\n"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"- **Time `O(N)`**: each node is visited once.\n- **Space `O(N)`**: worst-case queue size can be `O(N)` for a wide tree.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"A **leaf** is any node with `left == null` and `right == null`.\nDFS naturally reaches leaves and can collect them with only recursion stack space.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"DFS is simpler and uses stack space proportional to tree height.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic class FindLeafNodesDFS {\n    static class TreeNode {\n        int val;\n        TreeNode left, right;\n        TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }\n    }\n\n    public static List<Integer> leafNodes(TreeNode root) {\n        List<Integer> leaves = new ArrayList<>();\n        dfs(root, leaves);\n        return leaves;\n    }\n\n    private static void dfs(TreeNode node, List<Integer> leaves) {\n        if (node == null) return;\n        if (node.left == null && node.right == null) {\n            leaves.add(node.val);\n            return;\n        }\n        dfs(node.left, leaves);\n        dfs(node.right, leaves);\n    }\n}\n"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(H)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"- **Time `O(N)`**: each node is visited once.\n- **Space `O(H)`**: recursion stack depth is tree height `H` (worst-case `N` for a skewed tree).","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] BFS/level-order: visit all nodes and collect those with no children.\n- [ ] Queue can grow large for wide trees.\n- [ ] Time O(N), Space O(N)."}]}', 
  '["DFS: when node has no children, add it to answer.","Uses recursion stack proportional to height.","Time O(N), Space O(H)."]', '[]', '[]',
  'find-leaf-nodes
Find Leaf Nodes (Tree)
Trees
DFS/BFS traversal
Given the root of a binary tree, return (or print) all **leaf nodes**.
- `root`: binary tree root
- List of leaf values (any order depending on traversal)
```txt
    1
   / \
  2   3
 /
4

output = [4, 3]
```
Nodes `4` and `3` have no children.
Traverse all nodes; whenever you see a node with no children, it’s a leaf.
Level-order (BFS): visit all nodes and collect leaves.
- **Time `O(N)`**: each node is visited once.
- **Space `O(N)`**: worst-case queue size can be `O(N)` for a wide tree.
A **leaf** is any node with `left == null` and `right == null`.
DFS naturally reaches leaves and can collect them with only recursion stack space.
DFS is simpler and uses stack space proportional to tree height.
- **Time `O(N)`**: each node is visited once.
- **Space `O(H)`**: recursion stack depth is tree height `H` (worst-case `N` for a skewed tree).
BFS/level-order: visit all nodes and collect those with no children.
Queue can grow large for wide trees.
Time O(N), Space O(N).
DFS: when node has no children, add it to answer.
Uses recursion stack proportional to height.
Time O(N), Space O(H).', '2026-04-05T10:35:58.508Z'
);

