  'shortest-job-first', 'problem', 'Shortest Job First', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/shortest-job-first/1', 'medium', 'Other', 'Greedy',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given an array of integers burstTime[] representing the burst time of processes, find the average waiting time using the Shortest Job First (SJF) scheduling algorithm.\n\nIn SJF, processes are executed in increasing order of their burst time.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn array burstTime[] of size n representing execution time of each process.\n\n### Output\nReturn the average waiting time of all processes.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input:\nburstTime = [6, 8, 7, 3]\n\nOutput:\n7","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Step 1: Sort burst times → [3,6,7,8]\nStep 2: Waiting times:\nFirst process = 0\nSecond = 3\nThird = 3 + 6 = 9\nFourth = 3 + 6 + 7 = 16\n\nTotal waiting time = 0 + 3 + 9 + 16 = 28\nAverage = 28 / 4 = 7","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Try all possible execution orders and compute waiting time. This guarantees the minimum but is computationally infeasible.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Generate all permutations of burstTime\n2. For each permutation:\n   - Calculate total waiting time\n3. Return minimum average waiting time","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"public class Solution {\n    public int solve(int[] bt) {\n        Arrays.sort(bt);\n        int wait = 0, total = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < bt.length; i++) {\n            total += wait;\n            wait += bt[i];\n        }\n        return total / bt.length;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(n!)","space":"O(n)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Generating all permutations leads to factorial time complexity, which is impractical.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Executing shorter jobs first minimizes cumulative waiting time because longer jobs delay more processes if placed earlier.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Sort burstTime array in ascending order\n2. Initialize wait = 0, total = 0\n3. For each process:\n   - Add current wait to total\n   - Increase wait by burstTime[i]\n4. Return total / n","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Solution {\n    public int solve(int[] bt) {\n        Arrays.sort(bt);\n        int wait = 0, total = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < bt.length; i++) {\n            total += wait;\n            wait += bt[i];\n        }\n        return total / bt.length;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(n log n)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"Sorted = [3,6,7,8]\nWait = 0 → 3 → 9 → 16\nTotal = 28\nAverage = 7"},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Sorting dominates the time complexity. No extra space is required apart from variables.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Try all permutations\n- [ ] Compute waiting time\n- [ ] Pick minimum"}]}', 
  '["Sort burst times","Accumulate waiting time","Return average"]', '[]', '[]',
  'shortest-job-first
Shortest Job First
Other
Greedy
Given an array of integers burstTime[] representing the burst time of processes, find the average waiting time using the Shortest Job First (SJF) scheduling algorithm.

In SJF, processes are executed in increasing order of their burst time.
An array burstTime[] of size n representing execution time of each process.
Return the average waiting time of all processes.
Input:
burstTime = [6, 8, 7, 3]

Output:
7
Step 1: Sort burst times → [3,6,7,8]
Step 2: Waiting times:
First process = 0
Second = 3
Third = 3 + 6 = 9
Fourth = 3 + 6 + 7 = 16

Total waiting time = 0 + 3 + 9 + 16 = 28
Average = 28 / 4 = 7
Try all possible execution orders and compute waiting time. This guarantees the minimum but is computationally infeasible.
1. Generate all permutations of burstTime
2. For each permutation:
   - Calculate total waiting time
3. Return minimum average waiting time
Compare different orders like [6,8,7,3] vs [3,6,7,8]. Sorted order gives minimum waiting time.
Generating all permutations leads to factorial time complexity, which is impractical.
Executing shorter jobs first minimizes cumulative waiting time because longer jobs delay more processes if placed earlier.
1. Sort burstTime array in ascending order
2. Initialize wait = 0, total = 0
3. For each process:
   - Add current wait to total
   - Increase wait by burstTime[i]
4. Return total / n
Sorted = [3,6,7,8]
Wait = 0 → 3 → 9 → 16
Total = 28
Average = 7
Sorting dominates the time complexity. No extra space is required apart from variables.
Try all permutations
Compute waiting time
Pick minimum
Sort burst times
Accumulate waiting time
Return average', '2026-04-11T05:42:30.129Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'page-faults-in-lru', 'problem', 'Page Faults in LRU', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/page-faults-in-lru5603/1', 'medium', 'Operating System', 'Page Replacement',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given a sequence of pages and a memory capacity, find the number of page faults using the Least Recently Used (LRU) page replacement algorithm. A page fault occurs when a requested page is not present in memory and must be loaded, possibly replacing an existing page if memory is full. The LRU algorithm replaces the page that has not been used for the longest period of time.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\n- `N`: Length of the page reference sequence.\n- `C`: Capacity of memory (number of page frames).\n- `pages[]`: Array representing the sequence of page references.\n\n### Output\n- Return the total number of page faults that occur.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"```\nInput: N = 9, C = 4, pages = [5, 0, 1, 3, 2, 4, 1, 0, 5]\nOutput: 8\nExplanation:\n- Initially, all frames are empty, so the first 4 pages (5, 0, 1, 3) cause page faults.\n- Next, page 2 replaces LRU page 5.\n- Page 4 replaces LRU page 0.\n- Page 1 is already in memory.\n- Page 0 replaces LRU page 3.\n- Page 5 replaces LRU page 2.\nTotal page faults: 8\n```","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"The output is 8 because each time a new page is referenced and not found in memory, a page fault occurs. When memory is full, the least recently used page is replaced, which may also cause a page fault if the new page is not already in memory.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"The brute force approach involves simulating the LRU process using a list to keep track of pages in memory. For each page in the sequence, check if it is already in memory. If not, add it to memory (causing a page fault). If memory is full, remove the least recently used page (the first element in the list) before adding the new page. This approach is straightforward but inefficient for large inputs due to O(N*C) time complexity, as it requires searching the list for each page.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Initialize an empty list to represent memory.\n2. For each page in the sequence:\n   - If the page is not in memory, increment the page fault count.\n   - If memory is full, remove the first page (LRU) from the list.\n   - Add the current page to the end of the list.\n3. Return the total page fault count.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"// Brute Force Approach: Using ArrayList to simulate LRU\nimport java.util.*;\n\nclass Solution {\n    static int pageFaults(int N, int C, int[] pages) {\n        ArrayList<Integer> memory = new ArrayList<>();\n        int pageFaults = 0;\n\n        for (int page : pages) {\n            if (!memory.contains(page)) {\n                pageFaults++;\n                if (memory.size() == C) {\n                    memory.remove(0); // Remove LRU page (first element)\n                }\n                memory.add(page); // Add new page to end\n            } else {\n                memory.remove((Integer) page); // Remove to update LRU order\n                memory.add(page); // Add to end (most recently used)\n            }\n        }\n        return pageFaults;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int N = 9, C = 4;\n        int[] pages = {5, 0, 1, 3, 2, 4, 1, 0, 5};\n        System.out.println(pageFaults(N, C, pages)); // Output: 8\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N*C)","space":"O(C)"},"visuals":[{"id":"page-faults-in-lru-brute-vis","type":"reactflow","title":"Brute Force Visualization","nodes":[{"id":"A","position":{"x":0,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Start"}},{"id":"B","position":{"x":180,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Check Page in Memory"}},{"id":"C","position":{"x":360,"y":-100},"data":{"label":"Page Found"}},{"id":"D","position":{"x":360,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Page Not Found"}},{"id":"E","position":{"x":540,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Memory Full?"}},{"id":"F","position":{"x":720,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Remove LRU Page"}},{"id":"G","position":{"x":900,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Add New Page"}},{"id":"H","position":{"x":1080,"y":0},"data":{"label":"End"}}],"edges":[{"id":"eAB","source":"A","target":"B"},{"id":"eBC","source":"B","target":"C","label":"Found"},{"id":"eBD","source":"B","target":"D","label":"Not Found"},{"id":"eDE","source":"D","target":"E"},{"id":"eEF","source":"E","target":"F","label":"Yes"},{"id":"eEG","source":"E","target":"G","label":"No"},{"id":"eFG","source":"F","target":"G"},{"id":"eGH","source":"G","target":"H"},{"id":"eCH","source":"C","target":"H"}]}]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"- **Time**: O(N*C) because for each page, we may search the entire memory list (O(C)) to check for presence and update order.\n- **Space**: O(C) for storing the pages in memory.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"The optimal approach for LRU uses a combination of a hash map and a doubly linked list. The hash map allows O(1) lookups to check if a page is in memory, while the doubly linked list maintains the order of pages from least to most recently used. This reduces the time complexity to O(N) for the entire sequence, as each operation (insert, delete, update) on the linked list is O(1).","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Use a hash map to store page keys and their corresponding nodes in a doubly linked list.\n2. For each page in the sequence:\n   - If the page is in the hash map, move its node to the end of the list (most recently used).\n   - If not, increment the page fault count. If memory is full, remove the first node (LRU) from the list and its entry from the hash map. Add the new page to the end of the list and update the hash map.\n3. Return the total page fault count.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"// Optimal Approach: Using HashMap and Doubly Linked List\nimport java.util.*;\n\nclass Node {\n    int key;\n    Node prev, next;\n    Node(int key) { this.key = key; }\n}\n\nclass Solution {\n    static int pageFaults(int N, int C, int[] pages) {\n        HashMap<Integer, Node> map = new HashMap<>();\n        Node head = new Node(-1), tail = new Node(-1);\n        head.next = tail; tail.prev = head;\n        int pageFaults = 0;\n\n        for (int page : pages) {\n            if (map.containsKey(page)) {\n                Node node = map.get(page);\n                removeNode(node);\n                addToEnd(node);\n            } else {\n                pageFaults++;\n                if (map.size() == C) {\n                    Node lru = head.next;\n                    removeNode(lru);\n                    map.remove(lru.key);\n                }\n                Node newNode = new Node(page);\n                addToEnd(newNode);\n                map.put(page, newNode);\n            }\n        }\n        return pageFaults;\n    }\n\n    static void removeNode(Node node) {\n        node.prev.next = node.next;\n        node.next.prev = node.prev;\n    }\n\n    static void addToEnd(Node node) {\n        Node last = tail.prev;\n        last.next = node;\n        node.prev = last;\n        node.next = tail;\n        tail.prev = node;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int N = 9, C = 4;\n        int[] pages = {5, 0, 1, 3, 2, 4, 1, 0, 5};\n        System.out.println(pageFaults(N, C, pages)); // Output: 8\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(C)"},"visuals":[{"id":"page-faults-in-lru-optimal-vis","type":"reactflow","title":"Optimal Visualization","nodes":[{"id":"A","position":{"x":0,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Start"}},{"id":"B","position":{"x":180,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Check Page in HashMap"}},{"id":"C","position":{"x":360,"y":-100},"data":{"label":"Page Found"}},{"id":"D","position":{"x":360,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Page Not Found"}},{"id":"E","position":{"x":540,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Memory Full?"}},{"id":"F","position":{"x":720,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Remove LRU Node"}},{"id":"G","position":{"x":900,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Add New Node"}},{"id":"H","position":{"x":1080,"y":0},"data":{"label":"End"}}],"edges":[{"id":"eAB","source":"A","target":"B"},{"id":"eBC","source":"B","target":"C","label":"Found"},{"id":"eBD","source":"B","target":"D","label":"Not Found"},{"id":"eDE","source":"D","target":"E"},{"id":"eEF","source":"E","target":"F","label":"Yes"},{"id":"eEG","source":"E","target":"G","label":"No"},{"id":"eFG","source":"F","target":"G"},{"id":"eGH","source":"G","target":"H"},{"id":"eCH","source":"C","target":"H"}]}]},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"- **Time**: O(N) because each operation (insert, delete, update) on the hash map and doubly linked list is O(1).\n- **Space**: O(C) for storing the pages in memory and the hash map.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Use an ArrayList to simulate memory and LRU order.\n- [ ] For each page, check if it is in memory; if not, add it and increment page fault count.\n- [ ] If memory is full, remove the first page (LRU) before adding the new page.\n- [ ] Time: O(N*C), Space: O(C)."}]}', 
  '["Use a HashMap for O(1) lookups and a doubly linked list to maintain LRU order.","For each page, if present, move it to the end of the list (most recently used).","If not present, add it to the end; if memory is full, remove the first node (LRU).","Time: O(N), Space: O(C)."]', '[]', '[{"id":"page-faults-in-lru-brute-vis","type":"reactflow","title":"Brute Force Visualization","nodes":[{"id":"A","position":{"x":0,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Start"}},{"id":"B","position":{"x":180,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Check Page in Memory"}},{"id":"C","position":{"x":360,"y":-100},"data":{"label":"Page Found"}},{"id":"D","position":{"x":360,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Page Not Found"}},{"id":"E","position":{"x":540,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Memory Full?"}},{"id":"F","position":{"x":720,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Remove LRU Page"}},{"id":"G","position":{"x":900,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Add New Page"}},{"id":"H","position":{"x":1080,"y":0},"data":{"label":"End"}}],"edges":[{"id":"eAB","source":"A","target":"B"},{"id":"eBC","source":"B","target":"C","label":"Found"},{"id":"eBD","source":"B","target":"D","label":"Not Found"},{"id":"eDE","source":"D","target":"E"},{"id":"eEF","source":"E","target":"F","label":"Yes"},{"id":"eEG","source":"E","target":"G","label":"No"},{"id":"eFG","source":"F","target":"G"},{"id":"eGH","source":"G","target":"H"},{"id":"eCH","source":"C","target":"H"}]},{"id":"page-faults-in-lru-optimal-vis","type":"reactflow","title":"Optimal Visualization","nodes":[{"id":"A","position":{"x":0,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Start"}},{"id":"B","position":{"x":180,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Check Page in HashMap"}},{"id":"C","position":{"x":360,"y":-100},"data":{"label":"Page Found"}},{"id":"D","position":{"x":360,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Page Not Found"}},{"id":"E","position":{"x":540,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Memory Full?"}},{"id":"F","position":{"x":720,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Remove LRU Node"}},{"id":"G","position":{"x":900,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Add New Node"}},{"id":"H","position":{"x":1080,"y":0},"data":{"label":"End"}}],"edges":[{"id":"eAB","source":"A","target":"B"},{"id":"eBC","source":"B","target":"C","label":"Found"},{"id":"eBD","source":"B","target":"D","label":"Not Found"},{"id":"eDE","source":"D","target":"E"},{"id":"eEF","source":"E","target":"F","label":"Yes"},{"id":"eEG","source":"E","target":"G","label":"No"},{"id":"eFG","source":"F","target":"G"},{"id":"eGH","source":"G","target":"H"},{"id":"eCH","source":"C","target":"H"}]}]',
  'page-faults-in-lru
Page Faults in LRU
Operating System
Page Replacement
Given a sequence of pages and a memory capacity, find the number of page faults using the Least Recently Used (LRU) page replacement algorithm. A page fault occurs when a requested page is not present in memory and must be loaded, possibly replacing an existing page if memory is full. The LRU algorithm replaces the page that has not been used for the longest period of time.
- `N`: Length of the page reference sequence.
- `C`: Capacity of memory (number of page frames).
- `pages[]`: Array representing the sequence of page references.
- Return the total number of page faults that occur.
```
Input: N = 9, C = 4, pages = [5, 0, 1, 3, 2, 4, 1, 0, 5]
Output: 8
Explanation:
- Initially, all frames are empty, so the first 4 pages (5, 0, 1, 3) cause page faults.
- Next, page 2 replaces LRU page 5.
- Page 4 replaces LRU page 0.
- Page 1 is already in memory.
- Page 0 replaces LRU page 3.
- Page 5 replaces LRU page 2.
Total page faults: 8
```
The output is 8 because each time a new page is referenced and not found in memory, a page fault occurs. When memory is full, the least recently used page is replaced, which may also cause a page fault if the new page is not already in memory.
The brute force approach involves simulating the LRU process using a list to keep track of pages in memory. For each page in the sequence, check if it is already in memory. If not, add it to memory (causing a page fault). If memory is full, remove the least recently used page (the first element in the list) before adding the new page. This approach is straightforward but inefficient for large inputs due to O(N*C) time complexity, as it requires searching the list for each page.
1. Initialize an empty list to represent memory.
2. For each page in the sequence:
   - If the page is not in memory, increment the page fault count.
   - If memory is full, remove the first page (LRU) from the list.
   - Add the current page to the end of the list.
3. Return the total page fault count.
{"nodes":[{"id":"A","position":{"x":0,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Start"}},{"id":"B","position":{"x":180,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Check Page in Memory"}},{"id":"C","position":{"x":360,"y":-100},"data":{"label":"Page Found"}},{"id":"D","position":{"x":360,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Page Not Found"}},{"id":"E","position":{"x":540,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Memory Full?"}},{"id":"F","position":{"x":720,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Remove LRU Page"}},{"id":"G","position":{"x":900,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Add New Page"}},{"id":"H","position":{"x":1080,"y":0},"data":{"label":"End"}}],"edges":[{"id":"eAB","source":"A","target":"B"},{"id":"eBC","source":"B","target":"C","label":"Found"},{"id":"eBD","source":"B","target":"D","label":"Not Found"},{"id":"eDE","source":"D","target":"E"},{"id":"eEF","source":"E","target":"F","label":"Yes"},{"id":"eEG","source":"E","target":"G","label":"No"},{"id":"eFG","source":"F","target":"G"},{"id":"eGH","source":"G","target":"H"},{"id":"eCH","source":"C","target":"H"}]}
- **Time**: O(N*C) because for each page, we may search the entire memory list (O(C)) to check for presence and update order.
- **Space**: O(C) for storing the pages in memory.
The optimal approach for LRU uses a combination of a hash map and a doubly linked list. The hash map allows O(1) lookups to check if a page is in memory, while the doubly linked list maintains the order of pages from least to most recently used. This reduces the time complexity to O(N) for the entire sequence, as each operation (insert, delete, update) on the linked list is O(1).
1. Use a hash map to store page keys and their corresponding nodes in a doubly linked list.
2. For each page in the sequence:
   - If the page is in the hash map, move its node to the end of the list (most recently used).
   - If not, increment the page fault count. If memory is full, remove the first node (LRU) from the list and its entry from the hash map. Add the new page to the end of the list and update the hash map.
3. Return the total page fault count.
{"nodes":[{"id":"A","position":{"x":0,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Start"}},{"id":"B","position":{"x":180,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Check Page in HashMap"}},{"id":"C","position":{"x":360,"y":-100},"data":{"label":"Page Found"}},{"id":"D","position":{"x":360,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Page Not Found"}},{"id":"E","position":{"x":540,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Memory Full?"}},{"id":"F","position":{"x":720,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Remove LRU Node"}},{"id":"G","position":{"x":900,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Add New Node"}},{"id":"H","position":{"x":1080,"y":0},"data":{"label":"End"}}],"edges":[{"id":"eAB","source":"A","target":"B"},{"id":"eBC","source":"B","target":"C","label":"Found"},{"id":"eBD","source":"B","target":"D","label":"Not Found"},{"id":"eDE","source":"D","target":"E"},{"id":"eEF","source":"E","target":"F","label":"Yes"},{"id":"eEG","source":"E","target":"G","label":"No"},{"id":"eFG","source":"F","target":"G"},{"id":"eGH","source":"G","target":"H"},{"id":"eCH","source":"C","target":"H"}]}
- **Time**: O(N) because each operation (insert, delete, update) on the hash map and doubly linked list is O(1).
- **Space**: O(C) for storing the pages in memory and the hash map.
Use an ArrayList to simulate memory and LRU order.
For each page, check if it is in memory; if not, add it and increment page fault count.
If memory is full, remove the first page (LRU) before adding the new page.
Time: O(N*C), Space: O(C).
Use a HashMap for O(1) lookups and a doubly linked list to maintain LRU order.
For each page, if present, move it to the end of the list (most recently used).
If not present, add it to the end; if memory is full, remove the first node (LRU).
Time: O(N), Space: O(C).', '2026-04-06T02:47:15.291Z'
);

