  'remove-duplicates-from-sorted-array', 'problem', 'Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/remove-duplicates-from-sorted-array/', 'medium', 'Array', 'Two Pointers',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given an integer array `nums` sorted in non-decreasing order, remove the duplicates **in-place** such that each unique element appears only once. The relative order of the elements should be kept the same.\n\nReturn the number of unique elements `k`. You must modify the array such that the first `k` elements of `nums` contain the unique elements in the order they were present initially.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nA sorted integer array `nums`.\n\n### Output\nThe number of unique elements `k`.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: nums = [0,0,1,1,1,2,2,3,3,4]\nOutput: 5, nums = [0,1,2,3,4,_,_,_,_,_]","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"We keep the first ''0'', skip the second ''0'', pick the first ''1'', skip the rest of ''1''s, and so on. The unique elements are moved to the earliest possible empty slots.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Use a Set data structure to store unique elements. Since a Set only keeps unique values, we can push everything into it and then put them back into the array.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Create a LinkedHashSet to maintain order and uniqueness.\n2. Iterate through `nums` and add every element to the set.\n3. Iterate through the set and overwrite the beginning of the `nums` array with these values.\n4. Return the size of the set.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"import java.util.*;\n\nclass Solution {\n    public int removeDuplicates(int[] nums) {\n        Set<Integer> set = new LinkedHashSet<>();\n        for (int x : nums) set.add(x);\n        int i = 0;\n        for (int x : set) {\n            nums[i++] = x;\n        }\n        return set.size();\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"We use a Set to store up to N elements, which requires extra linear space.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Since the array is sorted, we can use two pointers: `i` stays at the last known unique element, and `j` searches for the next different element. When `nums[j]` is different from `nums[i]`, we know we''ve found a new unique number.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. If the array is empty, return 0.\n2. Initialize `i = 0` (this is our ''unique'' pointer).\n3. Iterate `j` from 1 to N-1.\n4. If `nums[j] != nums[i]`, it means we found a new element. Increment `i` and set `nums[i] = nums[j]`.\n5. Return `i + 1`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int removeDuplicates(int[] nums) {\n        if (nums.length == 0) return 0;\n        int i = 0;\n        for (int j = 1; j < nums.length; j++) {\n            if (nums[j] != nums[i]) {\n                i++;\n                nums[i] = nums[j];\n            }\n        }\n        return i + 1;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"nums = [1, 1, 2]\n- i=0, j=1: nums[0]==nums[1] (1==1), j continues.\n- i=0, j=2: nums[0]!=nums[2] (1!=2), i becomes 1, nums[1]=2.\n- End: return i+1 = 2."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"We traverse the array only once and modify it in-place without extra storage.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Use a Set for uniqueness\n- [ ] Copy back to array\n- [ ] O(N) space"}]}', 
  '["Two-Pointer approach","Compare nums[i] and nums[j]","Update nums[++i] when new element found","In-place, O(1) space"]', '[]', '[]',
  'remove-duplicates-from-sorted-array
Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array
Array
Two Pointers
Given an integer array `nums` sorted in non-decreasing order, remove the duplicates **in-place** such that each unique element appears only once. The relative order of the elements should be kept the same.

Return the number of unique elements `k`. You must modify the array such that the first `k` elements of `nums` contain the unique elements in the order they were present initially.
A sorted integer array `nums`.
The number of unique elements `k`.
Input: nums = [0,0,1,1,1,2,2,3,3,4]
Output: 5, nums = [0,1,2,3,4,_,_,_,_,_]
We keep the first ''0'', skip the second ''0'', pick the first ''1'', skip the rest of ''1''s, and so on. The unique elements are moved to the earliest possible empty slots.
Use a Set data structure to store unique elements. Since a Set only keeps unique values, we can push everything into it and then put them back into the array.
1. Create a LinkedHashSet to maintain order and uniqueness.
2. Iterate through `nums` and add every element to the set.
3. Iterate through the set and overwrite the beginning of the `nums` array with these values.
4. Return the size of the set.
nums = [1,1,2]
Set = {1, 2}
nums[0]=1, nums[1]=2
Return 2.
We use a Set to store up to N elements, which requires extra linear space.
Since the array is sorted, we can use two pointers: `i` stays at the last known unique element, and `j` searches for the next different element. When `nums[j]` is different from `nums[i]`, we know we''ve found a new unique number.
1. If the array is empty, return 0.
2. Initialize `i = 0` (this is our ''unique'' pointer).
3. Iterate `j` from 1 to N-1.
4. If `nums[j] != nums[i]`, it means we found a new element. Increment `i` and set `nums[i] = nums[j]`.
5. Return `i + 1`.
nums = [1, 1, 2]
- i=0, j=1: nums[0]==nums[1] (1==1), j continues.
- i=0, j=2: nums[0]!=nums[2] (1!=2), i becomes 1, nums[1]=2.
- End: return i+1 = 2.
We traverse the array only once and modify it in-place without extra storage.
Use a Set for uniqueness
Copy back to array
O(N) space
Two-Pointer approach
Compare nums[i] and nums[j]
Update nums[++i] when new element found
In-place, O(1) space', '2026-04-15T07:06:39.217Z'
);

