  'missing-number', 'problem', 'Missing Number', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/missing-number/', 'medium', 'Array', 'Bit Manipulation / Math',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given an array `nums` containing `n` distinct numbers in the range `[0, n]`, return the only number in the range that is missing from the array.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn array of n distinct integers in the range [0, n].\n\n### Output\nThe single missing integer.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: nums = [3,0,1]\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: n = 3 since there are 3 numbers, so all numbers are in the range [0,3]. 2 is the missing number in the range.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"The array has a length of 3, meaning the numbers should be 0, 1, 2, 3. Comparing the sum of the expected range to the actual sum reveals the missing value.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Sort the array first. After sorting, the number at each index should match the index itself (nums[i] == i). The first index where this fails is the missing number.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Sort the input array nums.\n2. Iterate through the array from i = 0 to n-1.\n3. If nums[i] != i, return i.\n4. If the loop completes, the missing number is n.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int missingNumber(int[] nums) {\n        Arrays.sort(nums);\n        int n = nums.length;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            if (nums[i] != i) return i;\n        }\n        return n;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N log N)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Sorting the array takes O(N log N) time.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Mathematical Approach: Use the formula for the sum of the first n natural numbers. Subtract the actual sum of the array from this expected sum to find the missing number.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Calculate n (length of nums).\n2. Calculate expectedSum = n * (n + 1) / 2.\n3. Calculate actualSum by adding all elements in nums.\n4. Return expectedSum - actualSum.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int missingNumber(int[] nums) {\n        int n = nums.length;\n        int expectedSum = n * (n + 1) / 2;\n        int actualSum = 0;\n        for (int num : nums) {\n            actualSum += num;\n        }\n        return expectedSum - actualSum;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"nums = [3,0,1], n = 3\nExpected: 3 * (4) / 2 = 6\nActual: 3 + 0 + 1 = 4\nMissing: 6 - 4 = 2"},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"We iterate through the array once to calculate the actual sum.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Sort and compare with index\n- [ ] O(N log N) time"}]}', 
  '["Math: Expected Sum - Actual Sum","XOR: XOR all indices and values","O(N) time, O(1) space"]', '[]', '[]',
  'missing-number
Missing Number
Array
Bit Manipulation / Math
Given an array `nums` containing `n` distinct numbers in the range `[0, n]`, return the only number in the range that is missing from the array.
An array of n distinct integers in the range [0, n].
The single missing integer.
Input: nums = [3,0,1]
Output: 2
Explanation: n = 3 since there are 3 numbers, so all numbers are in the range [0,3]. 2 is the missing number in the range.
The array has a length of 3, meaning the numbers should be 0, 1, 2, 3. Comparing the sum of the expected range to the actual sum reveals the missing value.
Sort the array first. After sorting, the number at each index should match the index itself (nums[i] == i). The first index where this fails is the missing number.
1. Sort the input array nums.
2. Iterate through the array from i = 0 to n-1.
3. If nums[i] != i, return i.
4. If the loop completes, the missing number is n.
nums = [3,0,1]
Sorted: [0,1,3]
i=0: 0 == 0 (OK)
i=1: 1 == 1 (OK)
i=2: 3 != 2 (Found! Return 2)
Sorting the array takes O(N log N) time.
Mathematical Approach: Use the formula for the sum of the first n natural numbers. Subtract the actual sum of the array from this expected sum to find the missing number.
1. Calculate n (length of nums).
2. Calculate expectedSum = n * (n + 1) / 2.
3. Calculate actualSum by adding all elements in nums.
4. Return expectedSum - actualSum.
nums = [3,0,1], n = 3
Expected: 3 * (4) / 2 = 6
Actual: 3 + 0 + 1 = 4
Missing: 6 - 4 = 2
We iterate through the array once to calculate the actual sum.
Sort and compare with index
O(N log N) time
Math: Expected Sum - Actual Sum
XOR: XOR all indices and values
O(N) time, O(1) space', '2026-04-15T06:53:28.065Z'
);

