INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'insert-interval', 'problem', 'Insert Interval', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/insert-interval/', 'medium', 'Greedy', 'Merge Intervals',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"You are given a list of non-overlapping intervals sorted by their start time.\n\nInsert a new interval into the intervals such that the list remains sorted and non-overlapping. Merge intervals if necessary.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\n- intervals: [[start, end]] sorted and non-overlapping\n- newInterval: [start, end]\n\n### Output\n- Return updated list after insertion and merging","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"```txt\nintervals = [[1,3],[6,9]]\nnewInterval = [2,5]\n```","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"New interval overlaps with [1,3], so we merge → [1,5]\n\nFinal:\n[[1,5],[6,9]]","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Insert the new interval into the list and solve using the classic merge intervals approach.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Add newInterval to intervals\n2. Sort all intervals\n3. Merge overlapping intervals\n4. Return result","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"import java.util.*;\n\nclass Solution {\n    public int[][] insert(int[][] intervals, int[] newInterval) {\n        List<int[]> list = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(intervals));\n        list.add(newInterval);\n\n        list.sort((a, b) -> a[0] - b[0]);\n\n        List<int[]> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        int[] current = list.get(0);\n\n        for (int i = 1; i < list.size(); i++) {\n            int[] next = list.get(i);\n            if (current[1] >= next[0]) {\n                current[1] = Math.max(current[1], next[1]);\n            } else {\n                result.add(current);\n                current = next;\n            }\n        }\n\n        result.add(current);\n        return result.toArray(new int[result.size()][]);\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N log N)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Sorting dominates → O(N log N). Merging is linear.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Since intervals are sorted, process them in one pass by dividing into three phases: before overlap, merging, and after.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Add intervals ending before newInterval starts\n2. Merge overlapping intervals\n3. Add remaining intervals","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"import java.util.*;\n\nclass Solution {\n    public int[][] insert(int[][] intervals, int[] newInterval) {\n        List<int[]> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        int i = 0;\n\n        while (i < intervals.length && intervals[i][1] < newInterval[0]) {\n            result.add(intervals[i]);\n            i++;\n        }\n\n        while (i < intervals.length && intervals[i][0] <= newInterval[1]) {\n            newInterval[0] = Math.min(newInterval[0], intervals[i][0]);\n            newInterval[1] = Math.max(newInterval[1], intervals[i][1]);\n            i++;\n        }\n\n        result.add(newInterval);\n\n        while (i < intervals.length) {\n            result.add(intervals[i]);\n            i++;\n        }\n\n        return result.toArray(new int[result.size()][]);\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"Input:\nintervals = [[1,2], [3,5], [6,7], [8,10], [12,16]]\nnewInterval = [4,8]\n\nStep 1: Check before overlap\n- [1,2]: 2 < 4? Yes → Add [1,2] to result.\n- [3,5]: 5 < 4? No → Potential overlap starts.\n- Current Result: [[1,2]]\n\nStep 2: Merge\n- [3,5]: 3 <= 8? Yes (Overlap)\n   - Update newInterval: [min(4,3), max(8,5)] → [3,8]\n- [6,7]: 6 <= 8? Yes (Overlap)\n   - Update newInterval: [min(3,6), max(8,7)] → [3,8]\n- [8,10]: 8 <= 8? Yes (Overlap)\n   - Update newInterval: [min(3,8), max(8,10)] → [3,10]\n\nStep 3: Check next\n- [12,16]: 12 <= 10? No → Overlap period is over.\n\nStep 4: Add merged interval\n- Add the final version of newInterval ([3,10]) to the result.\n- Current Result: [[1,2], [3,10]]\n\nStep 5: Add remaining\n- [12,16]: Remaining after the merge. Add to result.\n- Final Result: [[1,2], [3,10], [12,16]]"},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Single pass → O(N). No sorting required.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Insert → Sort → Merge\n- [ ] Easy but slower\n- [ ] O(N log N)"}]}', 
  '["3 phases: before, merge, after","Single pass","O(N)"]', '[]', '[]',
  'insert-interval
Insert Interval
Greedy
Merge Intervals
You are given a list of non-overlapping intervals sorted by their start time.

Insert a new interval into the intervals such that the list remains sorted and non-overlapping. Merge intervals if necessary.
- intervals: [[start, end]] sorted and non-overlapping
- newInterval: [start, end]
- Return updated list after insertion and merging
```txt
intervals = [[1,3],[6,9]]
newInterval = [2,5]
```
