  'unique-paths', 'problem', 'Unique Paths', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/unique-paths/', 'medium', 'Dynamic Programming', '2D Grid DP',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"There is a robot on an `m x n` grid. The robot is initially located at the top-left corner `(0, 0)` and wants to reach the bottom-right corner `(m - 1, n - 1)`. The robot can only move either **down** or **right** at any point in time.\n\nGiven the two integers `m` and `n`, return the number of possible unique paths that the robot can take to reach the bottom-right corner.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nTwo integers `m` (rows) and `n` (columns).\n\n### Output\nTotal unique paths (integer).","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: m = 3, n = 2\nOutput: 3\nExplanation:\n1. Right -> Down -> Down\n2. Down -> Down -> Right\n3. Down -> Right -> Down","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Plain Recursion\nThe robot has two choices at any cell: move Right or move Down. We can use recursion to explore every possible branch until we either hit the destination or go out of bounds.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Start at `(0,0)`.\n2. If current position is `(m-1, n-1)`, return 1 (Path found).\n3. If out of bounds, return 0.\n4. Result = `count(Right) + count(Down)`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int uniquePaths(int m, int n) {\n        return solve(0, 0, m, n);\n    }\n\n    private int solve(int r, int c, int m, int n) {\n        if (r == m - 1 && c == n - 1) return 1;\n        if (r >= m || c >= n) return 0;\n        return solve(r + 1, c, m, n) + solve(r, c + 1, m, n);\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(2^(m+n))","space":"O(m+n)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"The time complexity is exponential because each state branches into two. Space is O(m+n) due to the recursion stack depth.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Tabulation (Bottom-Up DP) with Space Optimization\nInstead of calculating the same cells repeatedly (Memoization), we build a table. Notice that the value of `dp[i][j]` only depends on the row above it and the cell to its left. This allows us to reduce a 2D `O(m*n)` space complexity into a 1D `O(n)` space complexity.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Create a 1D array `prev` of size `n` initialized to 1 (since there is only 1 way to reach any cell in the first row).\n2. For each subsequent row, update the array: `curr[j] = curr[j-1] + prev[j]`.\n3. The value `curr[j-1]` represents the cell to the left, and `prev[j]` represents the cell above.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int uniquePaths(int m, int n) {\n        int[] dp = new int[n];\n        Arrays.fill(dp, 1);\n\n        for (int i = 1; i < m; i++) {\n            for (int j = 1; j < n; j++) {\n                dp[j] = dp[j] + dp[j - 1];\n            }\n        }\n        return dp[n - 1];\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(m * n)","space":"O(n)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"m=3, n=3:\nRow 0 (Init): [1, 1, 1]\nRow 1: [1, (1+1)=2, (2+1)=3]\nRow 2: [1, (1+2)=3, (3+3)=6]\nResult: 6"},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"We iterate through the grid once. By using a 1D array and updating it in place, we only use O(n) space.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Recursive f(r,c) = f(r+1,c) + f(r,c+1)\n- [ ] Overlapping subproblems cause exponential time"}]}', 
  '["Tabulation: Build paths row by row","Current cell = Top + Left","Space can be optimized to O(n) using a 1D array"]', '[]', '[]',
  'unique-paths
Unique Paths
Dynamic Programming
2D Grid DP
There is a robot on an `m x n` grid. The robot is initially located at the top-left corner `(0, 0)` and wants to reach the bottom-right corner `(m - 1, n - 1)`. The robot can only move either **down** or **right** at any point in time.

Given the two integers `m` and `n`, return the number of possible unique paths that the robot can take to reach the bottom-right corner.
Two integers `m` (rows) and `n` (columns).
Total unique paths (integer).
Input: m = 3, n = 2
Output: 3
Explanation:
1. Right -> Down -> Down
2. Down -> Down -> Right
3. Down -> Right -> Down
### Plain Recursion
The robot has two choices at any cell: move Right or move Down. We can use recursion to explore every possible branch until we either hit the destination or go out of bounds.
1. Start at `(0,0)`.
2. If current position is `(m-1, n-1)`, return 1 (Path found).
3. If out of bounds, return 0.
4. Result = `count(Right) + count(Down)`.
For m=2, n=2:
f(0,0) -> f(0,1) + f(1,0)
f(0,1) -> f(0,2)[Out] + f(1,1)[Target] = 0 + 1 = 1
f(1,0) -> f(1,1)[Target] + f(2,0)[Out] = 1 + 0 = 1
Result = 1 + 1 = 2
The time complexity is exponential because each state branches into two. Space is O(m+n) due to the recursion stack depth.
### Tabulation (Bottom-Up DP) with Space Optimization
Instead of calculating the same cells repeatedly (Memoization), we build a table. Notice that the value of `dp[i][j]` only depends on the row above it and the cell to its left. This allows us to reduce a 2D `O(m*n)` space complexity into a 1D `O(n)` space complexity.
1. Create a 1D array `prev` of size `n` initialized to 1 (since there is only 1 way to reach any cell in the first row).
2. For each subsequent row, update the array: `curr[j] = curr[j-1] + prev[j]`.
3. The value `curr[j-1]` represents the cell to the left, and `prev[j]` represents the cell above.
m=3, n=3:
Row 0 (Init): [1, 1, 1]
Row 1: [1, (1+1)=2, (2+1)=3]
Row 2: [1, (1+2)=3, (3+3)=6]
Result: 6
We iterate through the grid once. By using a 1D array and updating it in place, we only use O(n) space.
Recursive f(r,c) = f(r+1,c) + f(r,c+1)
Overlapping subproblems cause exponential time
Tabulation: Build paths row by row
Current cell = Top + Left
Space can be optimized to O(n) using a 1D array', '2026-05-12T17:55:59.090Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
