INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'longest-common-subsequence', 'problem', 'Longest Common Subsequence', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-common-subsequence/', 'medium', 'Dynamic Programming', 'DP on Strings',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given two strings `text1` and `text2`, return the length of their longest common subsequence. If there is no common subsequence, return `0`.\n\nA subsequence of a string is a new string generated from the original string with some characters (can be none) deleted without changing the relative order of the remaining characters.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nTwo strings `text1` and `text2`.\n\n### Output\nAn integer representing the length of the longest common subsequence.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: text1 = \"abcde\", text2 = \"ace\"\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: The longest common subsequence is \"ace\" and its length is 3.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Plain Recursion\nWe start comparing characters from the end of both strings. If the characters match, we have found a piece of our common subsequence, so we add `1` and shrink both strings. If they do not match, we don''t know which character to throw away, so we branch out and try both options (shrinking the first string, then shrinking the second string) and take the maximum result.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Define `solve(i, j)` returning the LCS of `text1[0..i]` and `text2[0..j]`.\n2. **Base Case**: If `i < 0` or `j < 0`, return `0` (one of the strings is exhausted).\n3. **Decisions**:\n   - Match: If `text1[i] == text2[j]`, return `1 + solve(i-1, j-1)`.\n   - Mismatch: Return `Math.max(solve(i-1, j), solve(i, j-1))`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int longestCommonSubsequence(String text1, String text2) {\n        return solve(text1.length() - 1, text2.length() - 1, text1, text2);\n    }\n    private int solve(int i, int j, String s1, String s2) {\n        if (i < 0 || j < 0) return 0;\n        if (s1.charAt(i) == s2.charAt(j)) {\n            return 1 + solve(i - 1, j - 1, s1, s2);\n        }\n        return Math.max(solve(i - 1, j, s1, s2), solve(i, j - 1, s1, s2));\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(2^(N + M))","space":"O(N + M)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Why is this DP?\n1. **Optimal Substructure**: The LCS of two strings is built from the LCS of their prefixes.\n2. **Overlapping Subproblems**: The recursive tree evaluates the exact same index pairs `(i, j)` repeatedly. By caching these coordinate states, we flatten the exponential tree into a predictable grid.\n\n*Index Shifting:* To handle the base case where a string is exhausted (`-1` index) without throwing OutOfBounds exceptions in our arrays, we shift our DP coordinates by +1. Index `0` in our array now represents an empty string.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"We provide both efficient implementations bundled together:\n1. **Tabulation (Space-Optimized Bottom-Up)**: Since calculating the current row of matches only requires looking at the current row and the immediate `prev` row, we discard the full 2D grid and use two 1D arrays of size `M + 1`.\n2. **Memoization (Top-Down)**: Caches `(i, j)` states in a 2D `memo` table initialized with `-1`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int longestCommonSubsequence(String text1, String text2) {\n        int n = text1.length();\n        int m = text2.length();\n        int[] prev = new int[m + 1];\n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n            int[] curr = new int[m + 1];\n            for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {\n                if (text1.charAt(i - 1) == text2.charAt(j - 1)) {\n                    curr[j] = 1 + prev[j - 1];\n                } else {\n                    curr[j] = Math.max(prev[j], curr[j - 1]);\n                }\n            }\n            prev = curr;\n        }\n        return prev[m];\n    }\n    public int longestCommonSubsequenceMemo(String text1, String text2) {\n        int n = text1.length();\n        int m = text2.length();\n        int[][] memo = new int[n][m];\n        for (int[] row : memo) Arrays.fill(row, -1);\n        return memoSolve(n - 1, m - 1, text1, text2, memo);\n    }\n    private int memoSolve(int i, int j, String s1, String s2, int[][] memo) {\n        if (i < 0 || j < 0) return 0;\n        if (memo[i][j] != -1) return memo[i][j];\n        if (s1.charAt(i) == s2.charAt(j)) {\n            return memo[i][j] = 1 + memoSolve(i - 1, j - 1, s1, s2, memo);\n        }\n        return memo[i][j] = Math.max(memoSolve(i - 1, j, s1, s2, memo), memoSolve(i, j - 1, s1, s2, memo));\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N * M)","space":"O(M)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"text1 = \"abc\", text2 = \"ac\". We shift to 1-based indexing. Compare ''c'' (i=3) and ''c'' (j=2): Match! Result is 1 + dp[2][1]. Compare ''b'' (i=2) and ''a'' (j=1): Mismatch! Result is max(dp[1][1], dp[2][0]). The grid builds bottom-up to yield a final length of 2."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time Complexity: O(N * M) because we evaluate each character combination from both strings exactly once. Space Complexity: Tabulation reduces the required memory from a full 2D matrix (N * M) to just O(M) by overwriting the previous row array dynamically.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] String matching generates exponential branches upon character mismatch.\n- [ ] Compares from right to left using i and j index pointers."}]}', 
  '["Index Shifting Strategy: Arrays are sized N+1 and M+1 so that index 0 represents an empty string boundary.","Grid Traversal: Match adds 1 to the diagonal top-left cell. Mismatch takes the max of the cell above or left.","Space Optimization: Flattens the 2D grid into two 1D arrays (prev and curr) because data strictly flows downwards one row at a time."]', '[]', '[]',
  'longest-common-subsequence
Longest Common Subsequence
Dynamic Programming
DP on Strings
Given two strings `text1` and `text2`, return the length of their longest common subsequence. If there is no common subsequence, return `0`.

A subsequence of a string is a new string generated from the original string with some characters (can be none) deleted without changing the relative order of the remaining characters.
Two strings `text1` and `text2`.
An integer representing the length of the longest common subsequence.
Input: text1 = "abcde", text2 = "ace"
Output: 3
Explanation: The longest common subsequence is "ace" and its length is 3.
### Plain Recursion
We start comparing characters from the end of both strings. If the characters match, we have found a piece of our common subsequence, so we add `1` and shrink both strings. If they do not match, we don''t know which character to throw away, so we branch out and try both options (shrinking the first string, then shrinking the second string) and take the maximum result.
1. Define `solve(i, j)` returning the LCS of `text1[0..i]` and `text2[0..j]`.
2. **Base Case**: If `i < 0` or `j < 0`, return `0` (one of the strings is exhausted).
3. **Decisions**:
   - Match: If `text1[i] == text2[j]`, return `1 + solve(i-1, j-1)`.
   - Mismatch: Return `Math.max(solve(i-1, j), solve(i, j-1))`.
### Why is this DP?
1. **Optimal Substructure**: The LCS of two strings is built from the LCS of their prefixes.
2. **Overlapping Subproblems**: The recursive tree evaluates the exact same index pairs `(i, j)` repeatedly. By caching these coordinate states, we flatten the exponential tree into a predictable grid.

*Index Shifting:* To handle the base case where a string is exhausted (`-1` index) without throwing OutOfBounds exceptions in our arrays, we shift our DP coordinates by +1. Index `0` in our array now represents an empty string.
We provide both efficient implementations bundled together:
1. **Tabulation (Space-Optimized Bottom-Up)**: Since calculating the current row of matches only requires looking at the current row and the immediate `prev` row, we discard the full 2D grid and use two 1D arrays of size `M + 1`.
2. **Memoization (Top-Down)**: Caches `(i, j)` states in a 2D `memo` table initialized with `-1`.
text1 = "abc", text2 = "ac". We shift to 1-based indexing. Compare ''c'' (i=3) and ''c'' (j=2): Match! Result is 1 + dp[2][1]. Compare ''b'' (i=2) and ''a'' (j=1): Mismatch! Result is max(dp[1][1], dp[2][0]). The grid builds bottom-up to yield a final length of 2.
Time Complexity: O(N * M) because we evaluate each character combination from both strings exactly once. Space Complexity: Tabulation reduces the required memory from a full 2D matrix (N * M) to just O(M) by overwriting the previous row array dynamically.
String matching generates exponential branches upon character mismatch.
Compares from right to left using i and j index pointers.
Index Shifting Strategy: Arrays are sized N+1 and M+1 so that index 0 represents an empty string boundary.
Grid Traversal: Match adds 1 to the diagonal top-left cell. Mismatch takes the max of the cell above or left.
Space Optimization: Flattens the 2D grid into two 1D arrays (prev and curr) because data strictly flows downwards one row at a time.', '2026-05-21T17:10:53.557Z'
);

