  'longest-common-substring', 'problem', 'Longest Common Substring', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/longest-common-substring1452/1', 'medium', 'Dynamic Programming', 'DP on Strings',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given two strings `S1` and `S2`, find the length of their longest common substring. A substring is a strictly contiguous sequence of characters within a string.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nTwo strings `S1` and `S2`, and their lengths `n` and `m`.\n\n### Output\nAn integer representing the length of the longest contiguous common substring.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: S1 = \"ABCDGH\", S2 = \"ACDGHR\"\nOutput: 4\nExplanation: The longest common substring is \"CDGH\" which has a length of 4.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Plain Recursion\nWe explore the strings starting from the end. We carry a `count` variable to track the current contiguous matching streak. If the characters match, we increment the streak and move diagonally. If they mismatch, the streak is broken (reset to 0), and we explore skipping a character in either string. The answer is the maximum streak encountered across all recursive paths.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Define `solve(i, j, count)` which tracks current indices and the active match streak.\n2. **Base Case**: If `i == 0` or `j == 0`, return the current `count`.\n3. **Decisions**:\n   - Match: If `S1[i-1] == S2[j-1]`, calculate the streak by calling `solve(i-1, j-1, count+1)`.\n   - Mismatch: The streak is broken, reset count to 0 and branch into `solve(i-1, j, 0)` and `solve(i, j-1, 0)`.\n4. Return the maximum of all these computed values.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int longestCommonSubstr(String S1, String S2, int n, int m) {\n        return solve(n, m, 0, S1, S2);\n    }\n    private int solve(int i, int j, int count, String S1, String S2) {\n        if (i == 0 || j == 0) return count;\n        int matchCount = count;\n        if (S1.charAt(i - 1) == S2.charAt(j - 1)) {\n            matchCount = solve(i - 1, j - 1, count + 1, S1, S2);\n        }\n        int skipS1 = solve(i - 1, j, 0, S1, S2);\n        int skipS2 = solve(i, j - 1, 0, S1, S2);\n        return Math.max(matchCount, Math.max(skipS1, skipS2));\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(3^(N + M))","space":"O(N + M)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Why is this DP?\nThis problem is a strict variation of the Longest Common Subsequence. The only difference is the **contiguous constraint**. If two characters do not match, we cannot carry over the previous subsequence length; we must immediately reset the current cell to `0`. \n\nBecause the longest common substring can end anywhere in the matrix (not necessarily at the very end of both strings), we maintain a global `maxLength` variable to continuously track the highest value generated in any cell.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. **Tabulation (Space-Optimized Bottom-Up)**: Create a 1D array `prev` of size `m + 1` representing the previous row of evaluations.\n2. Use index shifting (1-based indexing) where index `0` represents an empty string.\n3. Iterate through both strings. If characters match, `curr[j] = 1 + prev[j - 1]` and update `maxLength`.\n4. If characters mismatch, `curr[j] = 0` (resetting the contiguous streak).\n5. Shift `curr` to `prev` and repeat.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int longestCommonSubstr(String S1, String S2, int n, int m) {\n        int[] prev = new int[m + 1];\n        int maxLen = 0;\n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n            int[] curr = new int[m + 1];\n            for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {\n                if (S1.charAt(i - 1) == S2.charAt(j - 1)) {\n                    curr[j] = 1 + prev[j - 1];\n                    maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, curr[j]);\n                } else {\n                    curr[j] = 0;\n                }\n            }\n            prev = curr;\n        }\n        return maxLen;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N * M)","space":"O(M)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"S1 = \"abc\", S2 = \"xbcy\". Compare ''b'' (i=2) and ''b'' (j=2): Match! curr[2] = 1 + prev[1] = 1. Compare ''c'' and ''c'': Match! curr[3] = 1 + prev[2] = 2. Update maxLen = 2. When ''y'' is compared against any char in S1, it mismatches, resetting the contiguous cell to 0."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time Complexity: O(N * M) as we evaluate every combination of characters between the two strings exactly once. Space Complexity: Reduced to O(M) because calculating the current row of substring streaks only relies on the immediate diagonal value from the previous row.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Tracks streak via a count parameter in recursion.\n- [ ] Resets count to 0 and branches to skip character on mismatch."}]}', 
  '["Contiguous constraint alters LCS logic: Mismatch immediately resets cell value to 0.","The longest substring can end anywhere, so we must track a global maximum variable instead of just returning the bottom-right cell.","Space optimized using two 1D arrays (prev and curr)."]', '[]', '[]',
  'longest-common-substring
Longest Common Substring
Dynamic Programming
DP on Strings
Given two strings `S1` and `S2`, find the length of their longest common substring. A substring is a strictly contiguous sequence of characters within a string.
Two strings `S1` and `S2`, and their lengths `n` and `m`.
An integer representing the length of the longest contiguous common substring.
Input: S1 = "ABCDGH", S2 = "ACDGHR"
Output: 4
Explanation: The longest common substring is "CDGH" which has a length of 4.
### Plain Recursion
We explore the strings starting from the end. We carry a `count` variable to track the current contiguous matching streak. If the characters match, we increment the streak and move diagonally. If they mismatch, the streak is broken (reset to 0), and we explore skipping a character in either string. The answer is the maximum streak encountered across all recursive paths.
1. Define `solve(i, j, count)` which tracks current indices and the active match streak.
2. **Base Case**: If `i == 0` or `j == 0`, return the current `count`.
3. **Decisions**:
   - Match: If `S1[i-1] == S2[j-1]`, calculate the streak by calling `solve(i-1, j-1, count+1)`.
   - Mismatch: The streak is broken, reset count to 0 and branch into `solve(i-1, j, 0)` and `solve(i, j-1, 0)`.
4. Return the maximum of all these computed values.
### Why is this DP?
This problem is a strict variation of the Longest Common Subsequence. The only difference is the **contiguous constraint**. If two characters do not match, we cannot carry over the previous subsequence length; we must immediately reset the current cell to `0`. 

Because the longest common substring can end anywhere in the matrix (not necessarily at the very end of both strings), we maintain a global `maxLength` variable to continuously track the highest value generated in any cell.
1. **Tabulation (Space-Optimized Bottom-Up)**: Create a 1D array `prev` of size `m + 1` representing the previous row of evaluations.
2. Use index shifting (1-based indexing) where index `0` represents an empty string.
3. Iterate through both strings. If characters match, `curr[j] = 1 + prev[j - 1]` and update `maxLength`.
4. If characters mismatch, `curr[j] = 0` (resetting the contiguous streak).
5. Shift `curr` to `prev` and repeat.
S1 = "abc", S2 = "xbcy". Compare ''b'' (i=2) and ''b'' (j=2): Match! curr[2] = 1 + prev[1] = 1. Compare ''c'' and ''c'': Match! curr[3] = 1 + prev[2] = 2. Update maxLen = 2. When ''y'' is compared against any char in S1, it mismatches, resetting the contiguous cell to 0.
Time Complexity: O(N * M) as we evaluate every combination of characters between the two strings exactly once. Space Complexity: Reduced to O(M) because calculating the current row of substring streaks only relies on the immediate diagonal value from the previous row.
Tracks streak via a count parameter in recursion.
Resets count to 0 and branches to skip character on mismatch.
Contiguous constraint alters LCS logic: Mismatch immediately resets cell value to 0.
The longest substring can end anywhere, so we must track a global maximum variable instead of just returning the bottom-right cell.
Space optimized using two 1D arrays (prev and curr).', '2026-05-22T05:35:25.134Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'longest-palindromic-subsequence', 'problem', 'Longest Palindromic Subsequence', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-palindromic-subsequence/', 'medium', 'Dynamic Programming', 'DP on Strings / Interval DP',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given a string `s`, find the longest palindromic subsequence''s length in `s`.\n\nA subsequence is a sequence that can be derived from another sequence by deleting some or no elements without changing the order of the remaining elements.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nA single string `s`.\n\n### Output\nAn integer representing the length of the longest palindromic subsequence.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: s = \"bbbab\"\nOutput: 4\nExplanation: One possible longest palindromic subsequence is \"bbbb\".","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Plain Recursion (Two Pointers)\nWe can check for palindromes by placing pointers at the beginning (`left`) and end (`right`) of the string. If the characters match, they form the outer boundaries of a palindrome, so we add 2 to our length and move both pointers inward. If they don''t match, the palindrome might be hiding by skipping either the left character or the right character, so we branch into both paths and take the maximum.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Define `solve(l, r)` mapping to the `left` and `right` indices of the string.\n2. **Base Cases**:\n   - If `l > r`, return 0 (pointers crossed).\n   - If `l == r`, return 1 (a single character is a palindrome of length 1).\n3. **Decisions**:\n   - Match: If `s[l] == s[r]`, return `2 + solve(l + 1, r - 1)`.\n   - Mismatch: Return `Math.max(solve(l + 1, r), solve(l, r - 1))`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int longestPalindromeSubseq(String s) {\n        return solve(0, s.length() - 1, s);\n    }\n    private int solve(int l, int r, String s) {\n        if (l > r) return 0;\n        if (l == r) return 1;\n        if (s.charAt(l) == s.charAt(r)) {\n            return 2 + solve(l + 1, r - 1, s);\n        }\n        return Math.max(solve(l + 1, r, s), solve(l, r - 1, s));\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(2^N)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Why is this DP?\n1. **Overlapping Subproblems**: If using the two-pointer approach, the same internal substring boundaries `(l, r)` are evaluated multiple times. We can cache these states to flatten our exponential tree into a predictable grid.\n2. **Interval DP Logic**: Instead of reversing the string, we can process the string natively. We shrink our evaluation interval from the outside in. By caching the results, we ensure every `(left, right)` boundary combination is only processed once.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"We provide two native DP implementations bundled together:\n1. **Tabulation (1D Interval DP)**: We evaluate intervals starting from the end of the string backward to the front. We maintain a single 1D array `dp` where `dp[j]` represents the right pointer. A temporary variable `prevDiag` safely stores the bottom-left diagonal state before it gets overwritten.\n2. **Memoization (Top-Down)**: Caches the `(l, r)` recursive boundaries inside a 2D matrix. We rely on Java''s default `0` initialization as our unvisited check, completely eliminating the need for external `Arrays` imports.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int longestPalindromeSubseq(String s) {\n        int n = s.length();\n        int[] dp = new int[n];\n        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n            dp[i] = 1;\n            int prevDiag = 0;\n            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n                int temp = dp[j];\n                if (s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j)) {\n                    dp[j] = prevDiag + 2;\n                } else {\n                    dp[j] = Math.max(dp[j], dp[j - 1]);\n                }\n                prevDiag = temp;\n            }\n        }\n        return dp[n - 1];\n    }\n    public int longestPalindromeSubseqMemo(String s) {\n        int n = s.length();\n        int[][] memo = new int[n][n];\n        return memoSolve(0, n - 1, s, memo);\n    }\n    private int memoSolve(int l, int r, String s, int[][] memo) {\n        if (l > r) return 0;\n        if (l == r) return 1;\n        if (memo[l][r] != 0) return memo[l][r];\n        if (s.charAt(l) == s.charAt(r)) {\n            return memo[l][r] = 2 + memoSolve(l + 1, r - 1, s, memo);\n        }\n        return memo[l][r] = Math.max(memoSolve(l + 1, r, s, memo), memoSolve(l, r - 1, s, memo));\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N^2)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"s = \"bbbab\". The DP array shrinks the evaluation window dynamically. For substring \"bbba\", checking outer ''b''s adds 2 to the inner \"bb\" max length. The 1D array updates in-place, yielding a final maximum length of 4 at dp[n-1]."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time Complexity: O(N^2) as the nested loops evaluate every combination of the left and right string boundaries. Space Complexity: The native Tabulation approach perfectly limits auxiliary memory down to O(N) by overwriting tracking values in a single 1D array.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Two-pointer branching approach: Match adds 2, mismatch branches left and right.\n- [ ] Generates exponential paths simulating every possible deletion combination."}]}', 
  '["Interval State Caching: By storing evaluations for (left, right) coordinate pairs, we skip duplicate subproblems.","Zero-Dependency Initialization: Java natively initializes integer arrays to 0, which perfectly doubles as our ''unvisited'' flag since a valid palindromic length is always >= 1.","1D Space Optimization: A clever single loop updates the right pointers in-place, relying on a temporary variable to act as the diagonal cache."]', '[]', '[]',
  'longest-palindromic-subsequence
Longest Palindromic Subsequence
Dynamic Programming
DP on Strings / Interval DP
Given a string `s`, find the longest palindromic subsequence''s length in `s`.

A subsequence is a sequence that can be derived from another sequence by deleting some or no elements without changing the order of the remaining elements.
A single string `s`.
An integer representing the length of the longest palindromic subsequence.
Input: s = "bbbab"
Output: 4
Explanation: One possible longest palindromic subsequence is "bbbb".
### Plain Recursion (Two Pointers)
We can check for palindromes by placing pointers at the beginning (`left`) and end (`right`) of the string. If the characters match, they form the outer boundaries of a palindrome, so we add 2 to our length and move both pointers inward. If they don''t match, the palindrome might be hiding by skipping either the left character or the right character, so we branch into both paths and take the maximum.
1. Define `solve(l, r)` mapping to the `left` and `right` indices of the string.
2. **Base Cases**:
   - If `l > r`, return 0 (pointers crossed).
   - If `l == r`, return 1 (a single character is a palindrome of length 1).
3. **Decisions**:
   - Match: If `s[l] == s[r]`, return `2 + solve(l + 1, r - 1)`.
   - Mismatch: Return `Math.max(solve(l + 1, r), solve(l, r - 1))`.
### Why is this DP?
1. **Overlapping Subproblems**: If using the two-pointer approach, the same internal substring boundaries `(l, r)` are evaluated multiple times. We can cache these states to flatten our exponential tree into a predictable grid.
2. **Interval DP Logic**: Instead of reversing the string, we can process the string natively. We shrink our evaluation interval from the outside in. By caching the results, we ensure every `(left, right)` boundary combination is only processed once.
We provide two native DP implementations bundled together:
1. **Tabulation (1D Interval DP)**: We evaluate intervals starting from the end of the string backward to the front. We maintain a single 1D array `dp` where `dp[j]` represents the right pointer. A temporary variable `prevDiag` safely stores the bottom-left diagonal state before it gets overwritten.
2. **Memoization (Top-Down)**: Caches the `(l, r)` recursive boundaries inside a 2D matrix. We rely on Java''s default `0` initialization as our unvisited check, completely eliminating the need for external `Arrays` imports.
s = "bbbab". The DP array shrinks the evaluation window dynamically. For substring "bbba", checking outer ''b''s adds 2 to the inner "bb" max length. The 1D array updates in-place, yielding a final maximum length of 4 at dp[n-1].
Time Complexity: O(N^2) as the nested loops evaluate every combination of the left and right string boundaries. Space Complexity: The native Tabulation approach perfectly limits auxiliary memory down to O(N) by overwriting tracking values in a single 1D array.
Two-pointer branching approach: Match adds 2, mismatch branches left and right.
Generates exponential paths simulating every possible deletion combination.
Interval State Caching: By storing evaluations for (left, right) coordinate pairs, we skip duplicate subproblems.
Zero-Dependency Initialization: Java natively initializes integer arrays to 0, which perfectly doubles as our ''unvisited'' flag since a valid palindromic length is always >= 1.
1D Space Optimization: A clever single loop updates the right pointers in-place, relying on a temporary variable to act as the diagonal cache.', '2026-05-22T17:04:33.482Z'
);

