INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'shortest-common-supersequence', 'problem', 'Shortest Common Supersequence', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/shortest-common-supersequence/', 'medium', 'Dynamic Programming', 'DP on Strings',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given two strings `str1` and `str2`, return the shortest string that has both `str1` and `str2` as subsequences. If there are multiple valid strings, return any of them.\n\nA string `s` is a supersequence of string `t` if deleting some number of characters from `s` (possibly 0) results in the string `t`.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nTwo strings `str1` and `str2`.\n\n### Output\nA string representing the shortest common supersequence.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: str1 = \"abac\", str2 = \"cab\"\nOutput: \"cabac\"\nExplanation: \nstr1 = \"abac\" is a subsequence of \"cabac\" because we can delete the first \"c\".\nstr2 = \"cab\" is a subsequence of \"cabac\" because we can delete \"ac\" at the end.\nThe answer \"cabac\" is the shortest such string.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Plain Recursion (String Generation)\nWe can build the supersequence backwards character by character. If the last characters of both strings match, it''s optimal to include that character exactly once in our supersequence and move both pointers left. If they mismatch, we don''t know which one to include first, so we try both paths: including `str1`''s character or including `str2`''s character, and then we return the shorter of the two resulting strings.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Define `solve(i, j)` returning the shortest supersequence for `str1[0..i]` and `str2[0..j]`.\n2. **Base Cases**: If `str1` is exhausted (`i < 0`), return the remaining characters of `str2`. If `str2` is exhausted (`j < 0`), return the remaining characters of `str1`.\n3. **Decisions**:\n   - Match: If `str1[i] == str2[j]`, append the character and recursively call `solve(i-1, j-1)`.\n   - Mismatch: Generate strings for both `solve(i-1, j)` and `solve(i, j-1)`, append their respective characters, and return the shorter string.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public String shortestCommonSupersequence(String str1, String str2) {\n        return solve(str1.length() - 1, str2.length() - 1, str1, str2);\n    }\n    private String solve(int i, int j, String s1, String s2) {\n        if (i < 0 && j < 0) return \"\";\n        if (i < 0) return s2.substring(0, j + 1);\n        if (j < 0) return s1.substring(0, i + 1);\n        \n        if (s1.charAt(i) == s2.charAt(j)) {\n            return solve(i - 1, j - 1, s1, s2) + s1.charAt(i);\n        }\n        \n        String pickS1 = solve(i - 1, j, s1, s2) + s1.charAt(i);\n        String pickS2 = solve(i, j - 1, s1, s2) + s2.charAt(j);\n        \n        return pickS1.length() < pickS2.length() ? pickS1 : pickS2;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(2^(N+M))","space":"O(N+M)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Why is this DP?\n1. **The \"Aha!\" Moment**: The length of the shortest common supersequence is exactly `len(str1) + len(str2) - len(LCS)`. To construct the actual string, we must find the **Longest Common Subsequence (LCS)**, include those shared characters exactly once, and wrap the remaining unique characters around them.\n2. **Table Backtracking Constraint**: Notice that in previous problems, we optimized the LCS table to a 1D array. Here, **we cannot space-optimize**. We need the *entire* 2D DP grid to act as a map so we can successfully backtrack and trace our string construction path.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. **Build the LCS Grid**: Create a 2D `dp` array of size `(N+1) x (M+1)` and fill it using the standard LCS tabulation approach.\n2. **Backtrack via Pointers**: Start at the bottom-right `(n, m)`.\n   - If `str1[i-1] == str2[j-1]`, it''s an LCS character! Append it and move diagonally (`i--`, `j--`).\n   - If they mismatch, look at the grid. Move in the direction of the larger DP value (up or left). The character from the string we move away from gets appended.\n3. **Exhaustion**: If one string runs out before the other, append the rest of the remaining string.\n4. **Reverse**: Since we backtracked from the end, the string is built backward. Reverse it and return.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public String shortestCommonSupersequence(String str1, String str2) {\n        int n = str1.length();\n        int m = str2.length();\n        int[][] dp = new int[n + 1][m + 1];\n        \n        // 1. Build the LCS Table natively (defaults to 0)\n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n            for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {\n                if (str1.charAt(i - 1) == str2.charAt(j - 1)) {\n                    dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i - 1][j - 1];\n                } else {\n                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        \n        // 2. Backtrack to build the Shortest Common Supersequence\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        int i = n, j = m;\n        \n        while (i > 0 && j > 0) {\n            if (str1.charAt(i - 1) == str2.charAt(j - 1)) {\n                sb.append(str1.charAt(i - 1));\n                i--;\n                j--;\n            } else if (dp[i - 1][j] > dp[i][j - 1]) {\n                sb.append(str1.charAt(i - 1));\n                i--;\n            } else {\n                sb.append(str2.charAt(j - 1));\n                j--;\n            }\n        }\n        \n        // 3. Add any leftover characters\n        while (i > 0) {\n            sb.append(str1.charAt(i - 1));\n            i--;\n        }\n        while (j > 0) {\n            sb.append(str2.charAt(j - 1));\n            j--;\n        }\n        \n        return sb.reverse().toString();\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N * M)","space":"O(N * M)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"str1 = \"abac\", str2 = \"cab\". The LCS is \"ab\". Backtracking from dp[4][3]: mismatch at ''c'' and ''b''. dp[3][3] > dp[4][2], so we take ''c'' from str1 and move up. Match at ''a'' and ''a'', we take ''a'' and move diagonally. Continuing this traces exactly \"cabac\" backward -> \"cabac\"."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time Complexity: O(N * M) because generating the LCS grid takes O(N * M), and backtracking through it takes O(N + M) steps. Space Complexity: We intentionally maintain the full O(N * M) 2D array matrix because the historical cell states are strictly required for the backtracking phase.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] String concatenation at every branching path creates massive recursive memory overhead.\n- [ ] Returns the shorter generated string out of the two mismatch branches."}]}', 
  '["LCS determines shared characters. Length = len1 + len2 - LCS.","Backtracking Rule: Move diagonally on match. On mismatch, move towards the larger adjacent DP cell and append the character of the string you consume.","Must use the full 2D array. Space optimization to 1D destroys the coordinate history needed to retrace the string."]', '[]', '[]',
  'shortest-common-supersequence
Shortest Common Supersequence
Dynamic Programming
DP on Strings
Given two strings `str1` and `str2`, return the shortest string that has both `str1` and `str2` as subsequences. If there are multiple valid strings, return any of them.

A string `s` is a supersequence of string `t` if deleting some number of characters from `s` (possibly 0) results in the string `t`.
Two strings `str1` and `str2`.
A string representing the shortest common supersequence.
Input: str1 = "abac", str2 = "cab"
Output: "cabac"
Explanation: 
str1 = "abac" is a subsequence of "cabac" because we can delete the first "c".
str2 = "cab" is a subsequence of "cabac" because we can delete "ac" at the end.
The answer "cabac" is the shortest such string.
### Plain Recursion (String Generation)
We can build the supersequence backwards character by character. If the last characters of both strings match, it''s optimal to include that character exactly once in our supersequence and move both pointers left. If they mismatch, we don''t know which one to include first, so we try both paths: including `str1`''s character or including `str2`''s character, and then we return the shorter of the two resulting strings.
1. Define `solve(i, j)` returning the shortest supersequence for `str1[0..i]` and `str2[0..j]`.
2. **Base Cases**: If `str1` is exhausted (`i < 0`), return the remaining characters of `str2`. If `str2` is exhausted (`j < 0`), return the remaining characters of `str1`.
3. **Decisions**:
   - Match: If `str1[i] == str2[j]`, append the character and recursively call `solve(i-1, j-1)`.
   - Mismatch: Generate strings for both `solve(i-1, j)` and `solve(i, j-1)`, append their respective characters, and return the shorter string.
### Why is this DP?
1. **The "Aha!" Moment**: The length of the shortest common supersequence is exactly `len(str1) + len(str2) - len(LCS)`. To construct the actual string, we must find the **Longest Common Subsequence (LCS)**, include those shared characters exactly once, and wrap the remaining unique characters around them.
2. **Table Backtracking Constraint**: Notice that in previous problems, we optimized the LCS table to a 1D array. Here, **we cannot space-optimize**. We need the *entire* 2D DP grid to act as a map so we can successfully backtrack and trace our string construction path.
1. **Build the LCS Grid**: Create a 2D `dp` array of size `(N+1) x (M+1)` and fill it using the standard LCS tabulation approach.
2. **Backtrack via Pointers**: Start at the bottom-right `(n, m)`.
   - If `str1[i-1] == str2[j-1]`, it''s an LCS character! Append it and move diagonally (`i--`, `j--`).
   - If they mismatch, look at the grid. Move in the direction of the larger DP value (up or left). The character from the string we move away from gets appended.
3. **Exhaustion**: If one string runs out before the other, append the rest of the remaining string.
4. **Reverse**: Since we backtracked from the end, the string is built backward. Reverse it and return.
str1 = "abac", str2 = "cab". The LCS is "ab". Backtracking from dp[4][3]: mismatch at ''c'' and ''b''. dp[3][3] > dp[4][2], so we take ''c'' from str1 and move up. Match at ''a'' and ''a'', we take ''a'' and move diagonally. Continuing this traces exactly "cabac" backward -> "cabac".
Time Complexity: O(N * M) because generating the LCS grid takes O(N * M), and backtracking through it takes O(N + M) steps. Space Complexity: We intentionally maintain the full O(N * M) 2D array matrix because the historical cell states are strictly required for the backtracking phase.
String concatenation at every branching path creates massive recursive memory overhead.
Returns the shorter generated string out of the two mismatch branches.
LCS determines shared characters. Length = len1 + len2 - LCS.
Backtracking Rule: Move diagonally on match. On mismatch, move towards the larger adjacent DP cell and append the character of the string you consume.
Must use the full 2D array. Space optimization to 1D destroys the coordinate history needed to retrace the string.', '2026-05-23T16:56:01.987Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'distinct-subsequences', 'problem', 'Distinct Subsequences', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/distinct-subsequences/', 'medium', 'Dynamic Programming', 'DP on Strings',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given two strings `s` and `t`, return the number of distinct subsequences of `s` which equals `t`.\n\nThe test cases are generated so that the answer fits on a 32-bit signed integer.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nTwo strings `s` and `t`.\n\n### Output\nAn integer representing the number of distinct subsequences of `s` that match `t`.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: s = \"rabbbit\", t = \"rabbit\"\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: There are 3 ways to form \"rabbit\" by dropping exactly one ''b'' from \"rabbbit\".","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Plain Recursion\nWe can explore all possible subsequences of `s`. If the current characters match, we branch into two universes: one where we use the character in `s` to match `t`, and one where we ignore it and hope to find another matching character earlier in `s`. If they don''t match, we have no choice but to ignore the character in `s` and keep searching backward.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Define `solve(i, j)` representing the number of ways to form `t[0..j]` using `s[0..i]`.\n2. **Base Cases**: If `j < 0`, we matched all of `t` successfully, return 1. If `i < 0` but `j >= 0`, `s` is empty but `t` isn''t, return 0.\n3. **Decisions**:\n   - Match: If `s[i] == t[j]`, return `solve(i - 1, j - 1) + solve(i - 1, j)`.\n   - Mismatch: Return `solve(i - 1, j)`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int numDistinct(String s, String t) {\n        return solve(s.length() - 1, t.length() - 1, s, t);\n    }\n    private int solve(int i, int j, String s, String t) {\n        if (j < 0) return 1;\n        if (i < 0) return 0;\n        if (s.charAt(i) == t.charAt(j)) {\n            return solve(i - 1, j - 1, s, t) + solve(i - 1, j, s, t);\n        }\n        return solve(i - 1, j, s, t);\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(2^N)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Why is this DP?\n1. **Overlapping Subproblems**: The recursive tree evaluates the same index pairs `(i, j)` repeatedly. We can use DP to cache these states.\n2. **1D Array Trick**: Notice that computing the current row only requires values from the previous row. Furthermore, `dp[j]` only depends on `dp[j-1]` and its own previous state `dp[j]`. By iterating our inner loop **backwards**, we can compress the entire 2D matrix into a single 1D array without overwriting data we need for the current step!","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"We provide two native DP implementations bundled together:\n1. **Tabulation (1D Space-Optimized)**: Create a 1D array `dp` of size `M + 1`. Initialize `dp[0] = 1` because an empty string `t` can be formed exactly 1 way. Iterate through `s`. For each character, iterate backwards through `t` (from `M` down to 1). Update `dp[j] += dp[j - 1]` on a match.\n2. **Memoization (Top-Down)**: Caches the `(i, j)` recursive boundaries. We initialize the matrix to `-1` using nested loops to guarantee zero external import dependencies.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int numDistinct(String s, String t) {\n        int n = s.length();\n        int m = t.length();\n        int[] dp = new int[m + 1];\n        dp[0] = 1;\n        \n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n            for (int j = m; j >= 1; j--) {\n                if (s.charAt(i - 1) == t.charAt(j - 1)) {\n                    dp[j] = dp[j] + dp[j - 1];\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return dp[m];\n    }\n    \n    public int numDistinctMemo(String s, String t) {\n        int n = s.length();\n        int m = t.length();\n        int[][] memo = new int[n][m];\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {\n                memo[i][j] = -1;\n            }\n        }\n        return memoSolve(n - 1, m - 1, s, t, memo);\n    }\n    \n    private int memoSolve(int i, int j, String s, String t, int[][] memo) {\n        if (j < 0) return 1;\n        if (i < 0) return 0;\n        if (memo[i][j] != -1) return memo[i][j];\n        if (s.charAt(i) == t.charAt(j)) {\n            return memo[i][j] = memoSolve(i - 1, j - 1, s, t, memo) + memoSolve(i - 1, j, s, t, memo);\n        }\n        return memo[i][j] = memoSolve(i - 1, j, s, t, memo);\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N * M)","space":"O(M)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"s = \"babgbag\", t = \"bag\". DP array initialized with dp[0] = 1. When ''b'' is found, dp[1] increments. Later when ''a'' is found, dp[2] increments by the value in dp[1]. Iterating backwards ensures we don''t accidentally reuse a newly updated value from the same `s` character twice in one pass."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time Complexity: O(N * M) since we evaluate every character of string `s` against every character of string `t`. Space Complexity: O(M) for Tabulation because the 2D grid is strictly flattened into a single 1D array that dynamically updates in-place.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Match: Branch into taking the character AND ignoring the character (Take + Leave).\n- [ ] Mismatch: Branch into ignoring the character (Leave).\n- [ ] Base Case: Reaching the end of string ''t'' returns 1 (success)."}]}', 
  '["1D Space Optimization trick: Inner loop MUST run backwards from M down to 1.","Running backwards prevents a character in ''s'' from being matched multiple times against ''t'' in the same pass.","Zero-Dependency Memoization: Loops are used to manually fill the -1 state to prevent platform import crashes."]', '[]', '[]',
  'distinct-subsequences
Distinct Subsequences
Dynamic Programming
DP on Strings
Given two strings `s` and `t`, return the number of distinct subsequences of `s` which equals `t`.

The test cases are generated so that the answer fits on a 32-bit signed integer.
Two strings `s` and `t`.
An integer representing the number of distinct subsequences of `s` that match `t`.
Input: s = "rabbbit", t = "rabbit"
Output: 3
Explanation: There are 3 ways to form "rabbit" by dropping exactly one ''b'' from "rabbbit".
### Plain Recursion
We can explore all possible subsequences of `s`. If the current characters match, we branch into two universes: one where we use the character in `s` to match `t`, and one where we ignore it and hope to find another matching character earlier in `s`. If they don''t match, we have no choice but to ignore the character in `s` and keep searching backward.
1. Define `solve(i, j)` representing the number of ways to form `t[0..j]` using `s[0..i]`.
2. **Base Cases**: If `j < 0`, we matched all of `t` successfully, return 1. If `i < 0` but `j >= 0`, `s` is empty but `t` isn''t, return 0.
3. **Decisions**:
   - Match: If `s[i] == t[j]`, return `solve(i - 1, j - 1) + solve(i - 1, j)`.
   - Mismatch: Return `solve(i - 1, j)`.
### Why is this DP?
1. **Overlapping Subproblems**: The recursive tree evaluates the same index pairs `(i, j)` repeatedly. We can use DP to cache these states.
2. **1D Array Trick**: Notice that computing the current row only requires values from the previous row. Furthermore, `dp[j]` only depends on `dp[j-1]` and its own previous state `dp[j]`. By iterating our inner loop **backwards**, we can compress the entire 2D matrix into a single 1D array without overwriting data we need for the current step!
We provide two native DP implementations bundled together:
1. **Tabulation (1D Space-Optimized)**: Create a 1D array `dp` of size `M + 1`. Initialize `dp[0] = 1` because an empty string `t` can be formed exactly 1 way. Iterate through `s`. For each character, iterate backwards through `t` (from `M` down to 1). Update `dp[j] += dp[j - 1]` on a match.
2. **Memoization (Top-Down)**: Caches the `(i, j)` recursive boundaries. We initialize the matrix to `-1` using nested loops to guarantee zero external import dependencies.
s = "babgbag", t = "bag". DP array initialized with dp[0] = 1. When ''b'' is found, dp[1] increments. Later when ''a'' is found, dp[2] increments by the value in dp[1]. Iterating backwards ensures we don''t accidentally reuse a newly updated value from the same `s` character twice in one pass.
Time Complexity: O(N * M) since we evaluate every character of string `s` against every character of string `t`. Space Complexity: O(M) for Tabulation because the 2D grid is strictly flattened into a single 1D array that dynamically updates in-place.
Match: Branch into taking the character AND ignoring the character (Take + Leave).
Mismatch: Branch into ignoring the character (Leave).
Base Case: Reaching the end of string ''t'' returns 1 (success).
1D Space Optimization trick: Inner loop MUST run backwards from M down to 1.
Running backwards prevents a character in ''s'' from being matched multiple times against ''t'' in the same pass.
Zero-Dependency Memoization: Loops are used to manually fill the -1 state to prevent platform import crashes.', '2026-05-23T17:45:25.764Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'wildcard-matching', 'problem', 'Wildcard Matching', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/wildcard-matching/', 'medium', 'Dynamic Programming', 'DP on Strings',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given an input string `s` and a pattern `p`, implement wildcard pattern matching with support for `''?''` and `''*''` where:\n* `''?''` Matches any single character.\n* `''*''` Matches any sequence of characters (including the empty sequence).\n\nThe matching should cover the entire input string (not partial).","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nTwo strings `s` (input) and `p` (pattern).\n\n### Output\nA boolean representing whether the pattern matches the entire input string.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: s = \"cb\", p = \"?a\"\nOutput: false\nExplanation: ''?'' matches ''c'', but the second letter is ''a'', which does not match ''b''.\n\nInput: s = \"adceb\", p = \"*a*b\"\nOutput: true\nExplanation: The first ''*'' matches the empty sequence, while the second ''*'' matches the substring \"dce\".","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Plain Recursion\nWe compare the strings from right to left. If characters match natively or via a `?`, we simply shrink both pointers. If we hit a `*`, we face a crucial decision: does this `*` represent zero characters, or does it represent one (or more) characters? We must explore both universes. If we assume it''s zero characters, we throw the `*` away (move `j`). If we assume it consumes a character, we swallow the character in the string but keep the `*` active (move `i`).","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Define `solve(i, j)` to check if `s[0..i]` matches `p[0..j]`.\n2. **Base Cases**:\n   - Both strings exhausted (`i < 0 && j < 0`): Return `true`.\n   - Pattern exhausted but string remains (`j < 0`): Return `false`.\n   - String exhausted but pattern remains (`i < 0`): Return `true` ONLY if all remaining characters in the pattern are `*`.\n3. **Decisions**:\n   - Match/`?`: Return `solve(i - 1, j - 1)`.\n   - `*`: Return `solve(i - 1, j) || solve(i, j - 1)`.\n   - Mismatch: Return `false`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public boolean isMatch(String s, String p) {\n        return solve(s.length() - 1, p.length() - 1, s, p);\n    }\n    private boolean solve(int i, int j, String s, String p) {\n        if (i < 0 && j < 0) return true;\n        if (i >= 0 && j < 0) return false;\n        if (i < 0 && j >= 0) {\n            for (int k = 0; k <= j; k++) {\n                if (p.charAt(k) != ''*'') return false;\n            }\n            return true;\n        }\n        if (s.charAt(i) == p.charAt(j) || p.charAt(j) == ''?'') {\n            return solve(i - 1, j - 1, s, p);\n        }\n        if (p.charAt(j) == ''*'') {\n            return solve(i - 1, j, s, p) || solve(i, j - 1, s, p);\n        }\n        return false;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(2^(N + M))","space":"O(N + M)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Why is this DP?\n1. **Overlapping Subproblems**: Handling `*` recursively splits the execution path wildly. Evaluating combinations of keeping or dropping the `*` repeatedly hits identical string prefix states `(i, j)`. Caching these evaluations turns exponential backtracking into a strict grid check.\n2. **1D Target Flattening**: The state of any index pair relies only on the current string character (current row) and the previous string character (previous row). We can crush the 2D evaluation space into two highly efficient 1D boolean arrays.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"We provide two native DP implementations bundled together:\n1. **Tabulation (Space-Optimized Bottom-Up)**: Uses 1-based indexing to handle empty string base cases elegantly. We initialize a `prev` boolean array where `prev[0] = true` and populate trailing `*` characters. For each character in `s`, we evaluate matches using a `curr` array, shifting it forward upon row completion.\n2. **Memoization (Top-Down)**: Caches `(i, j)` coordinate outcomes inside a 2D integer matrix `memo`. Using a zero-dependency Native Array, `0` acts as Unvisited, `1` as True, and `-1` as False.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public boolean isMatch(String s, String p) {\n        int n = s.length();\n        int m = p.length();\n        boolean[] prev = new boolean[m + 1];\n        prev[0] = true;\n        \n        for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {\n            if (p.charAt(j - 1) == ''*'') prev[j] = prev[j - 1];\n        }\n        \n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n            boolean[] curr = new boolean[m + 1];\n            for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {\n                if (s.charAt(i - 1) == p.charAt(j - 1) || p.charAt(j - 1) == ''?'') {\n                    curr[j] = prev[j - 1];\n                } else if (p.charAt(j - 1) == ''*'') {\n                    curr[j] = curr[j - 1] || prev[j];\n                } else {\n                    curr[j] = false;\n                }\n            }\n            prev = curr;\n        }\n        return prev[m];\n    }\n    \n    public boolean isMatchMemo(String s, String p) {\n        int n = s.length();\n        int m = p.length();\n        int[][] memo = new int[n][m];\n        return memoSolve(n - 1, m - 1, s, p, memo) == 1;\n    }\n    \n    private int memoSolve(int i, int j, String s, String p, int[][] memo) {\n        if (i < 0 && j < 0) return 1;\n        if (j < 0) return -1;\n        if (i < 0) {\n            for (int k = 0; k <= j; k++) {\n                if (p.charAt(k) != ''*'') return -1;\n            }\n            return 1;\n        }\n        if (memo[i][j] != 0) return memo[i][j];\n        \n        if (s.charAt(i) == p.charAt(j) || p.charAt(j) == ''?'') {\n            return memo[i][j] = memoSolve(i - 1, j - 1, s, p, memo);\n        }\n        if (p.charAt(j) == ''*'') {\n            boolean matchEmpty = memoSolve(i, j - 1, s, p, memo) == 1;\n            boolean matchChar = memoSolve(i - 1, j, s, p, memo) == 1;\n            return memo[i][j] = (matchEmpty || matchChar) ? 1 : -1;\n        }\n        return memo[i][j] = -1;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N * M)","space":"O(M)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"s = \"adceb\", p = \"*a*b\". Shift to 1-based indexing. When p[j] is ''*'', curr[j] pulls truth either from curr[j-1] (assuming ''*'' is empty) or prev[j] (assuming ''*'' swallows the current string character). This cascading logic allows ''*'' to stretch infinitely to swallow \"dce\" seamlessly."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time Complexity: O(N * M) because every index combination of the input string and pattern string must be checked at most once. Space Complexity: Tabulation reduces the full 2D evaluation space into two simple 1D boolean arrays, dropping our memory overhead strictly down to O(M).","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Empty String Check: When the string is fully evaluated but the pattern remains, it ONLY counts as a match if all remaining pattern characters are exactly ''*''.\n- [ ] The Asterisk Branch: `*` forces a split logic of taking 0 characters `solve(i, j-1)` or swallowing a character `solve(i-1, j)`."}]}', 
  '["1-Based Grid Initialization: By using `m + 1` size array, `0` indices represent the base cases where matching empty strings occurs seamlessly.","Zero-Dependency Memoization: Utilizing `int[][]` prevents platform crashes. 0 = Unvisited, 1 = True, -1 = False.","Space Optimization: Flattens calculations to two arrays since computing a wildcard match strictly references adjacent diagonal, top, or left grid cells."]', '[]', '[]',
  'wildcard-matching
Wildcard Matching
Dynamic Programming
DP on Strings
Given an input string `s` and a pattern `p`, implement wildcard pattern matching with support for `''?''` and `''*''` where:
* `''?''` Matches any single character.
* `''*''` Matches any sequence of characters (including the empty sequence).

The matching should cover the entire input string (not partial).
Two strings `s` (input) and `p` (pattern).
A boolean representing whether the pattern matches the entire input string.
Input: s = "cb", p = "?a"
Output: false
Explanation: ''?'' matches ''c'', but the second letter is ''a'', which does not match ''b''.

Input: s = "adceb", p = "*a*b"
Output: true
Explanation: The first ''*'' matches the empty sequence, while the second ''*'' matches the substring "dce".
### Plain Recursion
We compare the strings from right to left. If characters match natively or via a `?`, we simply shrink both pointers. If we hit a `*`, we face a crucial decision: does this `*` represent zero characters, or does it represent one (or more) characters? We must explore both universes. If we assume it''s zero characters, we throw the `*` away (move `j`). If we assume it consumes a character, we swallow the character in the string but keep the `*` active (move `i`).
1. Define `solve(i, j)` to check if `s[0..i]` matches `p[0..j]`.
2. **Base Cases**:
   - Both strings exhausted (`i < 0 && j < 0`): Return `true`.
   - Pattern exhausted but string remains (`j < 0`): Return `false`.
   - String exhausted but pattern remains (`i < 0`): Return `true` ONLY if all remaining characters in the pattern are `*`.
3. **Decisions**:
   - Match/`?`: Return `solve(i - 1, j - 1)`.
   - `*`: Return `solve(i - 1, j) || solve(i, j - 1)`.
   - Mismatch: Return `false`.
### Why is this DP?
1. **Overlapping Subproblems**: Handling `*` recursively splits the execution path wildly. Evaluating combinations of keeping or dropping the `*` repeatedly hits identical string prefix states `(i, j)`. Caching these evaluations turns exponential backtracking into a strict grid check.
2. **1D Target Flattening**: The state of any index pair relies only on the current string character (current row) and the previous string character (previous row). We can crush the 2D evaluation space into two highly efficient 1D boolean arrays.
We provide two native DP implementations bundled together:
1. **Tabulation (Space-Optimized Bottom-Up)**: Uses 1-based indexing to handle empty string base cases elegantly. We initialize a `prev` boolean array where `prev[0] = true` and populate trailing `*` characters. For each character in `s`, we evaluate matches using a `curr` array, shifting it forward upon row completion.
2. **Memoization (Top-Down)**: Caches `(i, j)` coordinate outcomes inside a 2D integer matrix `memo`. Using a zero-dependency Native Array, `0` acts as Unvisited, `1` as True, and `-1` as False.
s = "adceb", p = "*a*b". Shift to 1-based indexing. When p[j] is ''*'', curr[j] pulls truth either from curr[j-1] (assuming ''*'' is empty) or prev[j] (assuming ''*'' swallows the current string character). This cascading logic allows ''*'' to stretch infinitely to swallow "dce" seamlessly.
Time Complexity: O(N * M) because every index combination of the input string and pattern string must be checked at most once. Space Complexity: Tabulation reduces the full 2D evaluation space into two simple 1D boolean arrays, dropping our memory overhead strictly down to O(M).
Empty String Check: When the string is fully evaluated but the pattern remains, it ONLY counts as a match if all remaining pattern characters are exactly ''*''.
The Asterisk Branch: `*` forces a split logic of taking 0 characters `solve(i, j-1)` or swallowing a character `solve(i-1, j)`.
1-Based Grid Initialization: By using `m + 1` size array, `0` indices represent the base cases where matching empty strings occurs seamlessly.
Zero-Dependency Memoization: Utilizing `int[][]` prevents platform crashes. 0 = Unvisited, 1 = True, -1 = False.
Space Optimization: Flattens calculations to two arrays since computing a wildcard match strictly references adjacent diagonal, top, or left grid cells.', '2026-05-24T16:12:22.903Z'
);

