INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'longest-increasing-subsequence', 'problem', 'Longest Increasing Subsequence', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-increasing-subsequence/', 'medium', 'Dynamic Programming', 'DP on Subsequences',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given an integer array `nums`, return the length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn integer array `nums`.\n\n### Output\nAn integer representing the length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: nums = [10,9,2,5,3,7,101,18]\nOutput: 4\nExplanation: The longest increasing subsequence is [2,3,7,101], therefore the length is 4.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Plain Recursion\nWe explore every possible subsequence by evaluating elements one by one. To ensure the subsequence remains strictly increasing, we must track the index of the **previously included element**. If the current element is strictly greater than the previous one, we have the option to include it. Otherwise, we must skip it. We branch into all valid decisions and take the maximum length.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Define `solve(ind, prev_index)` which returns the LIS starting from `ind`.\n2. **Base Case**: If `ind == nums.length`, we have reached the end of the array, return `0`.\n3. **Decisions**:\n   - Skip: We can always skip the current element. `skip = solve(ind + 1, prev_index)`.\n   - Take: If `prev_index == -1` (no elements taken yet) OR `nums[ind] > nums[prev_index]`, we can take it. `take = 1 + solve(ind + 1, ind)`.\n4. Return `Math.max(take, skip)`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int lengthOfLIS(int[] nums) {\n        return solve(0, -1, nums);\n    }\n    private int solve(int ind, int prev_index, int[] nums) {\n        if (ind == nums.length) return 0;\n        \n        int skip = solve(ind + 1, prev_index, nums);\n        int take = 0;\n        \n        if (prev_index == -1 || nums[ind] > nums[prev_index]) {\n            take = 1 + solve(ind + 1, ind, nums);\n        }\n        \n        return Math.max(take, skip);\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(2^N)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Why is this DP?\n1. **Overlapping Subproblems**: The recursive tree frequently lands on the same `(ind, prev_index)` state. Caching these bounds stops the exponential branching.\n2. **1D State Flattening (The LIS Trick)**: Instead of tracking both the current index and previous index in a 2D matrix, we can redefine our state. Let `dp[i]` represent the **longest increasing subsequence that strictly ends at index `i`**. By iterating backwards from `i - 1` down to `0`, we can find the longest valid previous chain and simply add 1 to it. This crushes the 2D coordinate space into a single 1D array.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"We provide both implementations bundled securely together:\n1. **Tabulation (Classic 1D DP)**: Create a 1D `dp` array of size `N`, initialized to 1 (every element is an LIS of length 1 on its own). For every element `i`, look at all previous elements `prev`. If `nums[i] > nums[prev]`, update `dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], 1 + dp[prev])`. Maintain a global maximum variable.\n2. **Memoization (Coordinate DP)**: Caches the `(ind, prev_index)` recursive state. Because `prev_index` starts at `-1`, we shift the coordinate axis by `+1` (storing it at `prev_index + 1`) to avoid Out-Of-Bounds exceptions natively.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int lengthOfLIS(int[] nums) {\n        int n = nums.length;\n        int[] dp = new int[n];\n        int maxLIS = 1;\n        \n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            dp[i] = 1;\n            for (int prev = 0; prev < i; prev++) {\n                if (nums[i] > nums[prev]) {\n                    dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], 1 + dp[prev]);\n                }\n            }\n            maxLIS = Math.max(maxLIS, dp[i]);\n        }\n        \n        return maxLIS;\n    }\n\n    public int lengthOfLISMemo(int[] nums) {\n        int n = nums.length;\n        int[][] memo = new int[n][n + 1];\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) {\n                memo[i][j] = -1;\n            }\n        }\n        return memoSolve(0, -1, nums, memo);\n    }\n\n    private int memoSolve(int ind, int prev_index, int[] nums, int[][] memo) {\n        if (ind == nums.length) return 0;\n        if (memo[ind][prev_index + 1] != -1) return memo[ind][prev_index + 1];\n        \n        int skip = memoSolve(ind + 1, prev_index, nums, memo);\n        int take = 0;\n        \n        if (prev_index == -1 || nums[ind] > nums[prev_index]) {\n            take = 1 + memoSolve(ind + 1, ind, nums, memo);\n        }\n        \n        return memo[ind][prev_index + 1] = Math.max(take, skip);\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N^2)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"nums = [10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7]. DP array initialized to [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]. \ni=3 (value 5): looks back at 2. 5 > 2, so dp[3] = max(1, 1+dp[2]) = 2. \ni=4 (value 3): looks back at 2. 3 > 2, so dp[4] = 2. \ni=5 (value 7): looks back at 5, 3, 2. dp[5] = 1 + dp[3] = 3. Max LIS is 3."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time Complexity: O(N^2) because both the 1D Tabulation nested loops and the 2D Memoization state evaluations process N elements against all N prior possibilities. Space Complexity: Reduced completely to O(N) using Tabulation by transforming the tracking state to ''LIS ending at index i''.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Recursive branches dictate checking the current number against a `prev_index` variable.\n- [ ] Initial state uses `prev_index = -1` to symbolize an empty starting chain."}]}', 
  '["Index Shifting Strategy: To store `prev_index = -1` in an array, we must shift the access coordinate to `prev_index + 1`.","Zero-Dependency Initialization: Matrices are initialized using nested loops to `-1` natively.","The Classic 1D Trick: Redefining the state to `dp[i] = max length ending at i` instantly eliminates the need for a 2D grid."]', '[]', '[]',
  'longest-increasing-subsequence
Longest Increasing Subsequence
Dynamic Programming
DP on Subsequences
Given an integer array `nums`, return the length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence.
An integer array `nums`.
An integer representing the length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence.
Input: nums = [10,9,2,5,3,7,101,18]
Output: 4
Explanation: The longest increasing subsequence is [2,3,7,101], therefore the length is 4.
### Plain Recursion
We explore every possible subsequence by evaluating elements one by one. To ensure the subsequence remains strictly increasing, we must track the index of the **previously included element**. If the current element is strictly greater than the previous one, we have the option to include it. Otherwise, we must skip it. We branch into all valid decisions and take the maximum length.
1. Define `solve(ind, prev_index)` which returns the LIS starting from `ind`.
2. **Base Case**: If `ind == nums.length`, we have reached the end of the array, return `0`.
3. **Decisions**:
   - Skip: We can always skip the current element. `skip = solve(ind + 1, prev_index)`.
   - Take: If `prev_index == -1` (no elements taken yet) OR `nums[ind] > nums[prev_index]`, we can take it. `take = 1 + solve(ind + 1, ind)`.
4. Return `Math.max(take, skip)`.
### Why is this DP?
1. **Overlapping Subproblems**: The recursive tree frequently lands on the same `(ind, prev_index)` state. Caching these bounds stops the exponential branching.
2. **1D State Flattening (The LIS Trick)**: Instead of tracking both the current index and previous index in a 2D matrix, we can redefine our state. Let `dp[i]` represent the **longest increasing subsequence that strictly ends at index `i`**. By iterating backwards from `i - 1` down to `0`, we can find the longest valid previous chain and simply add 1 to it. This crushes the 2D coordinate space into a single 1D array.
We provide both implementations bundled securely together:
1. **Tabulation (Classic 1D DP)**: Create a 1D `dp` array of size `N`, initialized to 1 (every element is an LIS of length 1 on its own). For every element `i`, look at all previous elements `prev`. If `nums[i] > nums[prev]`, update `dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], 1 + dp[prev])`. Maintain a global maximum variable.
2. **Memoization (Coordinate DP)**: Caches the `(ind, prev_index)` recursive state. Because `prev_index` starts at `-1`, we shift the coordinate axis by `+1` (storing it at `prev_index + 1`) to avoid Out-Of-Bounds exceptions natively.
nums = [10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7]. DP array initialized to [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]. 
i=3 (value 5): looks back at 2. 5 > 2, so dp[3] = max(1, 1+dp[2]) = 2. 
i=4 (value 3): looks back at 2. 3 > 2, so dp[4] = 2. 
i=5 (value 7): looks back at 5, 3, 2. dp[5] = 1 + dp[3] = 3. Max LIS is 3.
Time Complexity: O(N^2) because both the 1D Tabulation nested loops and the 2D Memoization state evaluations process N elements against all N prior possibilities. Space Complexity: Reduced completely to O(N) using Tabulation by transforming the tracking state to ''LIS ending at index i''.
Recursive branches dictate checking the current number against a `prev_index` variable.
Initial state uses `prev_index = -1` to symbolize an empty starting chain.
Index Shifting Strategy: To store `prev_index = -1` in an array, we must shift the access coordinate to `prev_index + 1`.
Zero-Dependency Initialization: Matrices are initialized using nested loops to `-1` natively.
The Classic 1D Trick: Redefining the state to `dp[i] = max length ending at i` instantly eliminates the need for a 2D grid.', '2026-05-27T17:22:09.585Z'
);

