INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'largest-divisible-subset', 'problem', 'Largest Divisible Subset', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/largest-divisible-subset/', 'medium', 'Dynamic Programming', 'DP on Subsequences (LIS Variation)',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given a set of distinct positive integers `nums`, return the largest subset `answer` such that every pair `(answer[i], answer[j])` of elements in this subset satisfies:\n* `answer[i] % answer[j] == 0`, or\n* `answer[j] % answer[i] == 0`\n\nIf there are multiple solutions, return any of them.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn integer array `nums` of distinct positive integers.\n\n### Output\nA list/array of integers representing the largest divisible subset.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: nums = [1,2,3]\nOutput: [1,2]\nExplanation: [1,3] is also accepted since 1 divides 3.\n\nInput: nums = [1,2,4,8]\nOutput: [1,2,4,8]\nExplanation: Every pair divides each other smoothly (e.g., 8 % 4 == 0, 4 % 2 == 0).","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Plain Recursion (Generating Lists)\nBecause we sort the array first, we can explore all valid subsets sequentially just like an LIS problem. Instead of tracking the maximum length, we build and return the actual `List<Integer>`. At each step, we branch into ''Take'' (if the modulo condition passes) and ''Skip'', returning whichever generated list ends up larger.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. **Sort the array** `nums` ascending.\n2. Define `solve(ind, prev_index)` which returns a `List<Integer>` starting from `ind`.\n3. **Base Case**: If `ind == nums.length`, return an empty list.\n4. **Decisions**:\n   - Skip: Recursively call `solve(ind + 1, prev_index)`.\n   - Take: If `prev_index == -1` OR `nums[ind] % nums[prev_index] == 0`, create a new list, add the current element, and append the results of `solve(ind + 1, ind)`.\n5. Return the list with the greater `.size()`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public List<Integer> largestDivisibleSubset(int[] nums) {\n        Arrays.sort(nums);\n        return solve(0, -1, nums);\n    }\n    private List<Integer> solve(int ind, int prev_index, int[] nums) {\n        if (ind == nums.length) return new ArrayList<>();\n        \n        List<Integer> skip = solve(ind + 1, prev_index, nums);\n        List<Integer> take = new ArrayList<>();\n        \n        if (prev_index == -1 || nums[ind] % nums[prev_index] == 0) {\n            take.add(nums[ind]);\n            take.addAll(solve(ind + 1, ind, nums));\n        }\n        \n        return take.size() > skip.size() ? take : skip;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(2^N)","space":"O(N^2)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Why is this DP + Path Reconstruction?\n1. **The Transitive Property**: By sorting the array, we guarantee that if $c \\% b == 0$ and $b \\% a == 0$, then $c \\% a == 0$. This perfectly aligns with the Longest Increasing Subsequence pattern where we only need to look at the immediate previous element we picked.\n2. **The Hash Array Trick**: Since we need to return the actual subset, simply tracking lengths in a `dp` array is not enough. We introduce a `hash` array (parent tracker). Every time we update `dp[i]` with a longer valid chain from `prev`, we log `hash[i] = prev`. Finally, we backtrack from the maximum `dp` value to construct the exact sequence.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. **Sort `nums`**.\n2. Initialize `dp` (filled with 1s) and `hash` (filled with its own indices) arrays of size `N`.\n3. Loop `i` through the array. For each `i`, loop `prev` from `0` to `i-1`.\n4. If `nums[i] % nums[prev] == 0` AND `1 + dp[prev] > dp[i]`, update `dp[i] = 1 + dp[prev]` and explicitly update `hash[i] = prev`.\n5. Track the absolute maximum `dp` value and its index `lastIndex`.\n6. **Reconstruct**: Trace the `hash` array backwards from `lastIndex`, adding elements to the result list until `hash[i] == i`. Reverse the list and return.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public List<Integer> largestDivisibleSubset(int[] nums) {\n        int n = nums.length;\n        Arrays.sort(nums);\n        \n        int[] dp = new int[n];\n        int[] hash = new int[n];\n        int maxLIS = 1;\n        int lastIndex = 0;\n        \n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            hash[i] = i;\n            dp[i] = 1;\n            for (int prev = 0; prev < i; prev++) {\n                if (nums[i] % nums[prev] == 0 && 1 + dp[prev] > dp[i]) {\n                    dp[i] = 1 + dp[prev];\n                    hash[i] = prev;\n                }\n            }\n            if (dp[i] > maxLIS) {\n                maxLIS = dp[i];\n                lastIndex = i;\n            }\n        }\n        \n        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        result.add(nums[lastIndex]);\n        \n        while (hash[lastIndex] != lastIndex) {\n            lastIndex = hash[lastIndex];\n            result.add(nums[lastIndex]);\n        }\n        \n        int left = 0, right = result.size() - 1;\n        while (left < right) {\n            int temp = result.get(left);\n            result.set(left, result.get(right));\n            result.set(right, temp);\n            left++;\n            right--;\n        }\n        \n        return result;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N^2)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"nums = [1, 16, 7, 8, 4]. Sorted: [1, 4, 7, 8, 16].\n1 (dp=1, hash=0)\n4 % 1 == 0 -> dp=2, hash=0\n7 % 1 == 0 -> dp=2, hash=0\n8 % 4 == 0 -> dp=3, hash=1 (8 traces back to 4)\n16 % 8 == 0 -> dp=4, hash=3 (16 traces back to 8)\nMax length is 4 at lastIndex 4 (value 16). Backtrack: hash[4]->3 (val 8), hash[3]->1 (val 4), hash[1]->0 (val 1). Path: 16, 8, 4, 1. Reversed: [1, 4, 8, 16]."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time Complexity: O(N^2) because we utilize the standard nested double-loop associated with the LIS Tabulation architecture after an initial O(N log N) sorting operation. Space Complexity: O(N) since we allocate two 1D primitive integer arrays (`dp` and `hash`) alongside our output ArrayList.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Sorting is absolutely mandatory before evaluating. Without sorting, subset configurations become chaotic and unpredictable.\n- [ ] Generates massive memory overhead as a new sequence list is instantiated at every recursive branch."}]}', 
  '["Transitive Modulo Math: If sorted, a % b == 0 and b % c == 0 guarantees a % c == 0. This turns the problem into finding the Longest Increasing Subsequence.","The Hash Parent Strategy: By remembering *which* index provided the maximum subproblem value, we map a trace-route through our DP array to print the subset.","Zero-Dependency Reverse: Using a manual two-pointer swap on the final ArrayList guarantees the code compiles on strict platforms without needing utility imports."]', '[]', '[]',
  'largest-divisible-subset
Largest Divisible Subset
Dynamic Programming
DP on Subsequences (LIS Variation)
Given a set of distinct positive integers `nums`, return the largest subset `answer` such that every pair `(answer[i], answer[j])` of elements in this subset satisfies:
* `answer[i] % answer[j] == 0`, or
* `answer[j] % answer[i] == 0`

If there are multiple solutions, return any of them.
An integer array `nums` of distinct positive integers.
A list/array of integers representing the largest divisible subset.
Input: nums = [1,2,3]
Output: [1,2]
Explanation: [1,3] is also accepted since 1 divides 3.

Input: nums = [1,2,4,8]
Output: [1,2,4,8]
Explanation: Every pair divides each other smoothly (e.g., 8 % 4 == 0, 4 % 2 == 0).
### Plain Recursion (Generating Lists)
Because we sort the array first, we can explore all valid subsets sequentially just like an LIS problem. Instead of tracking the maximum length, we build and return the actual `List<Integer>`. At each step, we branch into ''Take'' (if the modulo condition passes) and ''Skip'', returning whichever generated list ends up larger.
1. **Sort the array** `nums` ascending.
2. Define `solve(ind, prev_index)` which returns a `List<Integer>` starting from `ind`.
3. **Base Case**: If `ind == nums.length`, return an empty list.
4. **Decisions**:
   - Skip: Recursively call `solve(ind + 1, prev_index)`.
   - Take: If `prev_index == -1` OR `nums[ind] % nums[prev_index] == 0`, create a new list, add the current element, and append the results of `solve(ind + 1, ind)`.
5. Return the list with the greater `.size()`.
### Why is this DP + Path Reconstruction?
1. **The Transitive Property**: By sorting the array, we guarantee that if $c \% b == 0$ and $b \% a == 0$, then $c \% a == 0$. This perfectly aligns with the Longest Increasing Subsequence pattern where we only need to look at the immediate previous element we picked.
2. **The Hash Array Trick**: Since we need to return the actual subset, simply tracking lengths in a `dp` array is not enough. We introduce a `hash` array (parent tracker). Every time we update `dp[i]` with a longer valid chain from `prev`, we log `hash[i] = prev`. Finally, we backtrack from the maximum `dp` value to construct the exact sequence.
1. **Sort `nums`**.
2. Initialize `dp` (filled with 1s) and `hash` (filled with its own indices) arrays of size `N`.
3. Loop `i` through the array. For each `i`, loop `prev` from `0` to `i-1`.
4. If `nums[i] % nums[prev] == 0` AND `1 + dp[prev] > dp[i]`, update `dp[i] = 1 + dp[prev]` and explicitly update `hash[i] = prev`.
5. Track the absolute maximum `dp` value and its index `lastIndex`.
6. **Reconstruct**: Trace the `hash` array backwards from `lastIndex`, adding elements to the result list until `hash[i] == i`. Reverse the list and return.
nums = [1, 16, 7, 8, 4]. Sorted: [1, 4, 7, 8, 16].
1 (dp=1, hash=0)
4 % 1 == 0 -> dp=2, hash=0
7 % 1 == 0 -> dp=2, hash=0
8 % 4 == 0 -> dp=3, hash=1 (8 traces back to 4)
16 % 8 == 0 -> dp=4, hash=3 (16 traces back to 8)
Max length is 4 at lastIndex 4 (value 16). Backtrack: hash[4]->3 (val 8), hash[3]->1 (val 4), hash[1]->0 (val 1). Path: 16, 8, 4, 1. Reversed: [1, 4, 8, 16].
Time Complexity: O(N^2) because we utilize the standard nested double-loop associated with the LIS Tabulation architecture after an initial O(N log N) sorting operation. Space Complexity: O(N) since we allocate two 1D primitive integer arrays (`dp` and `hash`) alongside our output ArrayList.
Sorting is absolutely mandatory before evaluating. Without sorting, subset configurations become chaotic and unpredictable.
Generates massive memory overhead as a new sequence list is instantiated at every recursive branch.
Transitive Modulo Math: If sorted, a % b == 0 and b % c == 0 guarantees a % c == 0. This turns the problem into finding the Longest Increasing Subsequence.
The Hash Parent Strategy: By remembering *which* index provided the maximum subproblem value, we map a trace-route through our DP array to print the subset.
Zero-Dependency Reverse: Using a manual two-pointer swap on the final ArrayList guarantees the code compiles on strict platforms without needing utility imports.', '2026-05-27T17:51:12.677Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'longest-bitonic-subsequence', 'problem', 'Longest Bitonic Subsequence', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/longest-bitonic-subsequence0824/1', 'medium', 'Dynamic Programming', 'DP on Subsequences (LIS Variation)',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given an array of positive integers, find the maximum length of a Bitonic subsequence. \n\nA subsequence is called Bitonic if it is first strictly increasing, then strictly decreasing.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn integer `n` representing the size of the array, and an integer array `nums`.\n\n### Output\nAn integer representing the maximum length of a bitonic subsequence.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: nums = [1, 11, 2, 10, 4, 5, 2, 1]\nOutput: 6\nExplanation: The longest bitonic subsequence is [1, 2, 10, 5, 2, 1]. It increases up to 10, then strictly decreases.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Plain Recursion (Evaluating Every Peak)\nA bitonic sequence revolves around a single \"peak\" element. If we arbitrarily pick an index `i` to act as the peak, the maximum bitonic sequence around it would be the Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS) strictly to its left, plus the Longest Decreasing Subsequence (LDS) strictly to its right. The brute force approach calculates the recursive LIS and LDS dynamically for every single index acting as the pivot.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Iterate a pivot pointer `i` from `0` to `n-1`.\n2. For each `i`, launch a standard recursive LIS function to find the longest increasing chain ending at `i`.\n3. Similarly, launch a standard recursive LDS function (which is just LIS operating backwards) to find the longest decreasing chain starting at `i`.\n4. Add both recursive results together and subtract `1` (because the peak element `i` is counted twice in both chains).\n5. Return the maximum calculated sum across all pivots.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int LongestBitonicSequence(int n, int[] nums) {\n        int maxBitonic = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            int leftLIS = getLIS(i, -1, nums, 1);\n            int rightLDS = getLDS(i, -1, nums, 1);\n            maxBitonic = Math.max(maxBitonic, leftLIS + rightLDS - 1);\n        }\n        return maxBitonic;\n    }\n    private int getLIS(int ind, int prev, int[] nums, int dir) {\n        // Extremely slow exponential backtracking for every single peak\n        // (Code abbreviated for conceptual focus: evaluates all sub-paths)\n        return 1; \n    }\n    private int getLDS(int ind, int prev, int[] nums, int dir) {\n        return 1;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N * 2^N)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Why is this DP?\n1. **Decoupling the Problem**: The Bitonic sequence is just two independent Longest Increasing Subsequence problems mathematically stitched together. \n2. **The Two-Array Strategy**: Instead of calculating the left and right chains for every pivot from scratch, we run the standard Tabulation LIS algorithm once from left-to-right (`dp1`) and once from right-to-left (`dp2`). Once we have these two cached arrays, checking any index `i` to see how good of a \"peak\" it makes is an instant $O(1)$ calculation: `dp1[i] + dp2[i] - 1`.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Initialize two arrays: `dp1` and `dp2` of size `N`, completely filled with `1` (as every standalone element is a valid sequence of length 1).\n2. **Left-to-Right LIS**: Run the classic LIS nested loops. `dp1[i]` will store the length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence ending exactly at `i`.\n3. **Right-to-Left LIS (LDS)**: Run the classic LIS nested loops, but start from `N-1` and step backwards down to `0`. `dp2[i]` will store the length of the longest strictly decreasing subsequence starting exactly at `i`.\n4. **Find the Peak**: Loop through `i` from `0` to `N-1`. The maximum bitonic sequence length will be the absolute maximum value of `dp1[i] + dp2[i] - 1`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int LongestBitonicSequence(int n, int[] nums) {\n        if (n == 0) return 0;\n        \n        int[] dp1 = new int[n];\n        int[] dp2 = new int[n];\n        \n        // Step 1: LIS from left to right\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            dp1[i] = 1;\n            for (int prev = 0; prev < i; prev++) {\n                if (nums[i] > nums[prev]) {\n                    dp1[i] = Math.max(dp1[i], 1 + dp1[prev]);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        \n        // Step 2: LDS from right to left\n        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n            dp2[i] = 1;\n            for (int prev = n - 1; prev > i; prev--) {\n                if (nums[i] > nums[prev]) {\n                    dp2[i] = Math.max(dp2[i], 1 + dp2[prev]);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        \n        // Step 3: Find the maximum Bitonic peak\n        int maxBitonic = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            // Note: If a strict mountain is required by the platform (must go up AND down),\n            // you would wrap this in: if (dp1[i] > 1 && dp2[i] > 1)\n            maxBitonic = Math.max(maxBitonic, dp1[i] + dp2[i] - 1);\n        }\n        \n        return maxBitonic;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N^2)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"nums = [1, 11, 2, 10, 4, 5, 2, 1]\ndp1 (LIS left-to-right) = [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 2, 1]\ndp2 (LIS right-to-left) = [1, 5, 1, 4, 2, 3, 2, 1]\n\nAt index 5 (value 5): dp1 is 4 (path: 1, 2, 4, 5). dp2 is 3 (path: 5, 2, 1). \nBitonic length = 4 + 3 - 1 = 6. This is the absolute maximum across all indices."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time Complexity: O(N^2) because we run the classic Tabulated LIS double-loop twice (once forward, once backward). Space Complexity: O(N) since we allocate two 1D integer tracking arrays `dp1` and `dp2`.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Treats every single index as the pivot/peak.\n- [ ] Recursively evaluates the maximum increasing chains exclusively to the left and right of the pivot."}]}', 
  '["Double Tabulation Trick: Cache LIS logic going forwards `dp1` and backwards `dp2` independently.","Intersection Mathematics: Combining the two arrays at index `i` yields `dp1[i] + dp2[i] - 1` (subtracting 1 to account for the pivot element being counted twice).","Strict Mountain Condition: In some variations of this problem, a sequence MUST both increase and decrease. If so, simply enforce `if (dp1[i] > 1 && dp2[i] > 1)` before maximizing."]', '[]', '[]',
  'longest-bitonic-subsequence
Longest Bitonic Subsequence
Dynamic Programming
DP on Subsequences (LIS Variation)
Given an array of positive integers, find the maximum length of a Bitonic subsequence. 

A subsequence is called Bitonic if it is first strictly increasing, then strictly decreasing.
An integer `n` representing the size of the array, and an integer array `nums`.
An integer representing the maximum length of a bitonic subsequence.
Input: nums = [1, 11, 2, 10, 4, 5, 2, 1]
Output: 6
Explanation: The longest bitonic subsequence is [1, 2, 10, 5, 2, 1]. It increases up to 10, then strictly decreases.
### Plain Recursion (Evaluating Every Peak)
A bitonic sequence revolves around a single "peak" element. If we arbitrarily pick an index `i` to act as the peak, the maximum bitonic sequence around it would be the Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS) strictly to its left, plus the Longest Decreasing Subsequence (LDS) strictly to its right. The brute force approach calculates the recursive LIS and LDS dynamically for every single index acting as the pivot.
1. Iterate a pivot pointer `i` from `0` to `n-1`.
2. For each `i`, launch a standard recursive LIS function to find the longest increasing chain ending at `i`.
3. Similarly, launch a standard recursive LDS function (which is just LIS operating backwards) to find the longest decreasing chain starting at `i`.
4. Add both recursive results together and subtract `1` (because the peak element `i` is counted twice in both chains).
5. Return the maximum calculated sum across all pivots.
### Why is this DP?
1. **Decoupling the Problem**: The Bitonic sequence is just two independent Longest Increasing Subsequence problems mathematically stitched together. 
2. **The Two-Array Strategy**: Instead of calculating the left and right chains for every pivot from scratch, we run the standard Tabulation LIS algorithm once from left-to-right (`dp1`) and once from right-to-left (`dp2`). Once we have these two cached arrays, checking any index `i` to see how good of a "peak" it makes is an instant $O(1)$ calculation: `dp1[i] + dp2[i] - 1`.
1. Initialize two arrays: `dp1` and `dp2` of size `N`, completely filled with `1` (as every standalone element is a valid sequence of length 1).
2. **Left-to-Right LIS**: Run the classic LIS nested loops. `dp1[i]` will store the length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence ending exactly at `i`.
3. **Right-to-Left LIS (LDS)**: Run the classic LIS nested loops, but start from `N-1` and step backwards down to `0`. `dp2[i]` will store the length of the longest strictly decreasing subsequence starting exactly at `i`.
4. **Find the Peak**: Loop through `i` from `0` to `N-1`. The maximum bitonic sequence length will be the absolute maximum value of `dp1[i] + dp2[i] - 1`.
nums = [1, 11, 2, 10, 4, 5, 2, 1]
dp1 (LIS left-to-right) = [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 2, 1]
dp2 (LIS right-to-left) = [1, 5, 1, 4, 2, 3, 2, 1]

At index 5 (value 5): dp1 is 4 (path: 1, 2, 4, 5). dp2 is 3 (path: 5, 2, 1). 
Bitonic length = 4 + 3 - 1 = 6. This is the absolute maximum across all indices.
Time Complexity: O(N^2) because we run the classic Tabulated LIS double-loop twice (once forward, once backward). Space Complexity: O(N) since we allocate two 1D integer tracking arrays `dp1` and `dp2`.
Treats every single index as the pivot/peak.
Recursively evaluates the maximum increasing chains exclusively to the left and right of the pivot.
Double Tabulation Trick: Cache LIS logic going forwards `dp1` and backwards `dp2` independently.
Intersection Mathematics: Combining the two arrays at index `i` yields `dp1[i] + dp2[i] - 1` (subtracting 1 to account for the pivot element being counted twice).
Strict Mountain Condition: In some variations of this problem, a sequence MUST both increase and decrease. If so, simply enforce `if (dp1[i] > 1 && dp2[i] > 1)` before maximizing.', '2026-05-28T04:16:45.593Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
