INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'merge-overlapping-subintervals', 'problem', 'Merge Overlapping Subintervals', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-intervals/', 'medium', 'Greedy', 'Merge Intervals',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given an array of intervals where intervals[i] = [start, end], merge all overlapping intervals and return an array of non-overlapping intervals that cover all the intervals in the input.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\n- intervals: array of intervals [[start, end]]\n\n### Output\n- Return merged intervals","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"```txt\nintervals = [[1,3],[2,6],[8,10],[15,18]]\n```","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Intervals [1,3] and [2,6] overlap → merge into [1,6]\n\nFinal:\n[[1,6],[8,10],[15,18]]","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Compare each interval with every other interval and merge if overlapping. Keep repeating until no overlaps remain.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Compare all pairs of intervals\n2. Merge overlapping ones\n3. Repeat until no overlaps remain","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"import java.util.*;\n\nclass Solution {\n    public int[][] merge(int[][] intervals) {\n        List<int[]> list = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(intervals));\n        boolean merged = true;\n\n        while (merged) {\n            merged = false;\n            for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++) {\n                for (int j = i + 1; j < list.size(); j++) {\n                    int[] a = list.get(i);\n                    int[] b = list.get(j);\n\n                    if (a[1] >= b[0] && b[1] >= a[0]) {\n                        int start = Math.min(a[0], b[0]);\n                        int end = Math.max(a[1], b[1]);\n\n                        list.remove(j);\n                        list.remove(i);\n                        list.add(new int[]{start, end});\n\n                        merged = true;\n                        break;\n                    }\n                }\n                if (merged) break;\n            }\n        }\n\n        return list.toArray(new int[list.size()][]);\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N^2)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Nested comparisons lead to O(N^2). Repeated merging increases cost.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"If intervals are sorted by start time, overlapping intervals will always be adjacent. So we only need to compare with the last merged interval.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Sort intervals by start\n2. Initialize result with first interval\n3. For each interval:\n   - If overlap → merge\n   - Else → add to result","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"import java.util.*;\n\nclass Solution {\n    public int[][] merge(int[][] intervals) {\n        Arrays.sort(intervals, (a, b) -> a[0] - b[0]);\n\n        List<int[]> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        int[] current = intervals[0];\n\n        for (int i = 1; i < intervals.length; i++) {\n            if (current[1] >= intervals[i][0]) {\n                current[1] = Math.max(current[1], intervals[i][1]);\n            } else {\n                result.add(current);\n                current = intervals[i];\n            }\n        }\n\n        result.add(current);\n        return result.toArray(new int[result.size()][]);\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N log N)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"Input:\n[[1,3],[2,6],[8,10],[15,18]]\n\nStep 1: Sort (already sorted)\n\nStep 2: Start with [1,3]\n\nStep 3: Compare [1,3] with [2,6]\n→ Overlap → merge → [1,6]\n\nStep 4: Compare [1,6] with [8,10]\n→ No overlap → push [1,6]\n\nStep 5: Compare [8,10] with [15,18]\n→ No overlap → push [8,10]\n\nFinal:\n[[1,6],[8,10],[15,18]]"},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Sorting takes O(N log N), merging is O(N).","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Compare all pairs\n- [ ] Merge repeatedly\n- [ ] O(N^2)"}]}', 
  '["Sort + linear scan","Only check last interval","O(N log N)"]', '[]', '[]',
  'merge-overlapping-subintervals
Merge Overlapping Subintervals
Greedy
Merge Intervals
Given an array of intervals where intervals[i] = [start, end], merge all overlapping intervals and return an array of non-overlapping intervals that cover all the intervals in the input.
- intervals: array of intervals [[start, end]]
- Return merged intervals
```txt
intervals = [[1,3],[2,6],[8,10],[15,18]]
```
Intervals [1,3] and [2,6] overlap → merge into [1,6]

Final:
[[1,6],[8,10],[15,18]]
Compare each interval with every other interval and merge if overlapping. Keep repeating until no overlaps remain.
1. Compare all pairs of intervals
2. Merge overlapping ones
3. Repeat until no overlaps remain
Input:
[[1,3],[2,6],[8,10],[15,18]]

Step 1: Compare [1,3] and [2,6]
→ Overlap → merge → [1,6]

Now list becomes:
[[1,6],[8,10],[15,18]]

Step 2: Compare [1,6] with others
→ No more overlaps

Final:
[[1,6],[8,10],[15,18]]
Nested comparisons lead to O(N^2). Repeated merging increases cost.
If intervals are sorted by start time, overlapping intervals will always be adjacent. So we only need to compare with the last merged interval.
1. Sort intervals by start
2. Initialize result with first interval
3. For each interval:
   - If overlap → merge
   - Else → add to result
Input:
[[1,3],[2,6],[8,10],[15,18]]

Step 1: Sort (already sorted)

Step 2: Start with [1,3]

Step 3: Compare [1,3] with [2,6]
→ Overlap → merge → [1,6]

Step 4: Compare [1,6] with [8,10]
→ No overlap → push [1,6]

Step 5: Compare [8,10] with [15,18]
→ No overlap → push [8,10]

Final:
[[1,6],[8,10],[15,18]]
Sorting takes O(N log N), merging is O(N).
Compare all pairs
Merge repeatedly
O(N^2)
Sort + linear scan
Only check last interval
O(N log N)', '2026-04-08T07:54:13.043Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'add-1-to-linked-list', 'problem', 'Add 1 to Linked List', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/add-1-to-a-number-represented-as-linked-list/1', 'medium', 'Linked List', 'Carry Propagation / Reverse List',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"You are given a linked list representing a non-negative number. Each node contains a single digit. The digits are stored such that the most significant digit is at the head.\n\nAdd 1 to the number and return the head of the modified linked list.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\n- Head of linked list representing a number\n- Example: 1 → 9 → 9 represents 199\n\n### Output\n- Return head of updated linked list after adding 1","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"```txt\nInput: 1 → 9 → 9\nOutput: 2 → 0 → 0\n```","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"199 + 1 = 200\n\nCarry propagates from last node to first.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Convert the linked list into a number, add 1, then rebuild the linked list. This works but is not efficient for large numbers.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Traverse list and convert to integer\n2. Add 1\n3. Convert number back to linked list","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"// Not recommended due to overflow risk\n// Skipping implementation"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Conversion requires O(N), but integer overflow makes it impractical.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"We need to simulate addition from the least significant digit. Since linked list is in forward order, reverse it, add 1, handle carry, and reverse back.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Reverse the linked list\n2. Add 1 to head\n3. Propagate carry\n4. Reverse list again\n5. If carry remains, add new node at front","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    static class Node {\n        int data;\n        Node next;\n        Node(int d) { data = d; }\n    }\n\n    public Node addOne(Node head) {\n        head = reverse(head);\n\n        Node curr = head;\n        int carry = 1;\n\n        while (curr != null && carry > 0) {\n            int sum = curr.data + carry;\n            curr.data = sum % 10;\n            carry = sum / 10;\n\n            if (curr.next == null && carry > 0) {\n                curr.next = new Node(carry);\n                carry = 0;\n                break;\n            }\n\n            curr = curr.next;\n        }\n\n        return reverse(head);\n    }\n\n    private Node reverse(Node head) {\n        Node prev = null;\n        while (head != null) {\n            Node next = head.next;\n            head.next = prev;\n            prev = head;\n            head = next;\n        }\n        return prev;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"Original: 1→9→9\nReverse: 9→9→1\n\nAdd 1:\n9+1=10 → 0 carry 1\n9+1=10 → 0 carry 1\n1+1=2 → carry 0\n\nReverse back → 2→0→0"},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"We traverse list twice (reverse + process) → O(N), constant space.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Convert to number\n- [ ] Add 1\n- [ ] Rebuild list\n- [ ] Overflow risk"}]}', 
  '["Reverse list","Add with carry","Reverse back","Handle final carry"]', '[]', '[]',
  'add-1-to-linked-list
Add 1 to Linked List
Linked List
Carry Propagation / Reverse List
You are given a linked list representing a non-negative number. Each node contains a single digit. The digits are stored such that the most significant digit is at the head.

Add 1 to the number and return the head of the modified linked list.
- Head of linked list representing a number
- Example: 1 → 9 → 9 represents 199
- Return head of updated linked list after adding 1
```txt
Input: 1 → 9 → 9
Output: 2 → 0 → 0
```
199 + 1 = 200

Carry propagates from last node to first.
Convert the linked list into a number, add 1, then rebuild the linked list. This works but is not efficient for large numbers.
1. Traverse list and convert to integer
2. Add 1
3. Convert number back to linked list
Convert 1→9→9 → 199
199 + 1 = 200
Rebuild → 2→0→0
