  'number-of-provinces', 'problem', 'Number of Provinces', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-provinces/', 'medium', 'Graphs', 'Connected Components / DFS',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"There are n cities. Some of them are connected, while some are not.\n\nIf city a is connected directly with city b, and city b is connected directly with city c, then city a is indirectly connected with city c.\n\nA province is a group of directly or indirectly connected cities.\n\nYou are given an n x n matrix isConnected where isConnected[i][j] = 1 if the ith city and jth city are directly connected, and 0 otherwise.\n\nReturn the total number of provinces.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\n- isConnected: n x n adjacency matrix\n- isConnected[i][j] = 1 means connection\n\n### Output\n- Return number of connected components (provinces)","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"```txt\nisConnected = [\n [1,1,0],\n [1,1,0],\n [0,0,1]\n]\n```","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Cities 0 and 1 are connected → 1 province\nCity 2 is isolated → another province\n\nTotal = 2","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Treat each city as a node. For every unvisited city, explore all reachable cities using DFS/BFS. Each traversal gives one province.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Maintain visited array\n2. Loop through each city\n3. If not visited → start DFS\n4. Mark all reachable cities\n5. Increment province count","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int findCircleNum(int[][] isConnected) {\n        int n = isConnected.length;\n        boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];\n        int count = 0;\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            if (!visited[i]) {\n                dfs(isConnected, visited, i);\n                count++;\n            }\n        }\n\n        return count;\n    }\n\n    private void dfs(int[][] graph, boolean[] visited, int node) {\n        visited[node] = true;\n\n        for (int j = 0; j < graph.length; j++) {\n            if (graph[node][j] == 1 && !visited[j]) {\n                dfs(graph, visited, j);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N^2)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"We traverse adjacency matrix → O(N^2). DFS stack → O(N).","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Use Disjoint Set (Union-Find) to group connected cities. Each connected component forms a province.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Initialize parent array\n2. For each connection, union nodes\n3. Count unique parents","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int findCircleNum(int[][] isConnected) {\n        int n = isConnected.length;\n        int[] parent = new int[n];\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n                if (isConnected[i][j] == 1) {\n                    union(parent, i, j);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            set.add(find(parent, i));\n        }\n\n        return set.size();\n    }\n\n    private int find(int[] parent, int x) {\n        if (parent[x] != x) {\n            parent[x] = find(parent, parent[x]);\n        }\n        return parent[x];\n    }\n\n    private void union(int[] parent, int a, int b) {\n        int rootA = find(parent, a);\n        int rootB = find(parent, b);\n        if (rootA != rootB) {\n            parent[rootB] = rootA;\n        }\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N^2 α(N))","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"Input:\n[[1,1,0],[1,1,0],[0,0,1]]\n\nStep 1: Initially → parent = [0,1,2]\n\nStep 2: Check connections\n- (0,1) → union → parent becomes same\n\nStep 3: Final parents → [0,0,2]\n\nStep 4: Unique parents → {0,2}\n\nAnswer = 2"},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Union-Find with path compression → near constant per operation.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] DFS each unvisited node\n- [ ] Count connected components\n- [ ] O(N^2)"}]}', 
  '["Union-Find","Group nodes","Count unique parents"]', '[]', '[]',
  'number-of-provinces
Number of Provinces
Graphs
Connected Components / DFS
There are n cities. Some of them are connected, while some are not.

If city a is connected directly with city b, and city b is connected directly with city c, then city a is indirectly connected with city c.

A province is a group of directly or indirectly connected cities.

You are given an n x n matrix isConnected where isConnected[i][j] = 1 if the ith city and jth city are directly connected, and 0 otherwise.

Return the total number of provinces.
- isConnected: n x n adjacency matrix
- isConnected[i][j] = 1 means connection
- Return number of connected components (provinces)
```txt
isConnected = [
 [1,1,0],
 [1,1,0],
 [0,0,1]
]
```
Cities 0 and 1 are connected → 1 province
City 2 is isolated → another province

Total = 2
Treat each city as a node. For every unvisited city, explore all reachable cities using DFS/BFS. Each traversal gives one province.
1. Maintain visited array
2. Loop through each city
3. If not visited → start DFS
4. Mark all reachable cities
5. Increment province count
Input:
[[1,1,0],[1,1,0],[0,0,1]]

Step 1: Start at city 0 → not visited
→ DFS(0)
→ Visit 1
→ Both marked visited
→ Province count = 1

Step 2: City 1 already visited → skip

Step 3: City 2 not visited
→ DFS(2)
→ Only itself
→ Province count = 2

Final Answer = 2
{"engine":"mermaid","steps":[{"label":"Initial Graph","description":"Cities 0 and 1 connected, 2 isolated","code":"graph LR\n    A[0] --- B[1]\n    C[2]"},{"label":"DFS from 0","description":"Visit 0 → then 1","code":"graph LR\n    A[0] --- B[1]\n    C[2]\n    style A fill:#22c55e\n    style B fill:#22c55e"},{"label":"DFS from 2","description":"Visit isolated node 2","code":"graph LR\n    A[0] --- B[1]\n    C[2]\n    style A fill:#22c55e\n    style B fill:#22c55e\n    style C fill:#22c55e"},{"label":"Final Provinces","description":"Two connected components","code":"graph LR\n    A[Province 1: 0-1] --> B[Province 2: 2]"}]}
We traverse adjacency matrix → O(N^2). DFS stack → O(N).
Use Disjoint Set (Union-Find) to group connected cities. Each connected component forms a province.
1. Initialize parent array
2. For each connection, union nodes
3. Count unique parents
Input:
[[1,1,0],[1,1,0],[0,0,1]]

Step 1: Initially → parent = [0,1,2]

Step 2: Check connections
- (0,1) → union → parent becomes same

Step 3: Final parents → [0,0,2]

Step 4: Unique parents → {0,2}

Answer = 2
{"engine":"mermaid","steps":[{"label":"Initial Disjoint Sets","description":"Each node is its own parent","code":"graph LR\n    A[0] --> A\n    B[1] --> B\n    C[2] --> C"},{"label":"Union (0,1)","description":"Merge sets of 0 and 1","code":"graph LR\n    A[0] --> B[1]\n    C[2] --> C"},{"label":"Final Groups","description":"Two provinces formed","code":"graph LR\n    A[Province 1: 0-1] --> B[Province 2: 2]"}]}
Union-Find with path compression → near constant per operation.
DFS each unvisited node
Count connected components
O(N^2)
Union-Find
Group nodes
Count unique parents', '2026-04-10T17:17:45.328Z'
);

