  'bfs-traversal-of-graph', 'problem', 'BFS Traversal of Graph', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/bfs-traversal-of-graph/1', 'medium', 'Graphs', 'BFS',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given a graph of V vertices represented by an adjacency list, perform Breadth First Search (BFS) starting from vertex 0.\n\nReturn the BFS traversal of the graph.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\n- V: number of vertices\n- adj: adjacency list where adj[i] contains neighbors of node i\n- Graph is connected\n\n### Output\n- Return list of nodes in BFS traversal order","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"```txt\nInput:\nV = 5\nadj = [[1,2,3],[0],[0,4],[0],[2]]\n\nOutput:\n0 1 2 3 4\n```","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Start from node 0 → visit all its neighbors (1,2,3) → then visit neighbors of 2 → node 4.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"BFS explores nodes level by level. We use a queue to process nodes in the order they are discovered. Start from node 0, visit all neighbors, then move to next level.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Create a visited array\n2. Initialize queue and push node 0\n3. Mark node 0 as visited\n4. While queue is not empty:\n   - Pop node\n   - Add to result\n   - Push all unvisited neighbors into queue\n5. Return result","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"import java.util.*;\n\nclass Solution {\n    public ArrayList<Integer> bfsOfGraph(int V, ArrayList<ArrayList<Integer>> adj) {\n        boolean[] visited = new boolean[V];\n        Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();\n        ArrayList<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\n\n        queue.offer(0);\n        visited[0] = true;\n\n        while(!queue.isEmpty()) {\n            int node = queue.poll();\n            result.add(node);\n\n            for(int neighbor : adj.get(node)) {\n                if(!visited[neighbor]) {\n                    visited[neighbor] = true;\n                    queue.offer(neighbor);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        return result;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(V + E)","space":"O(V)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"- Time: Each node and edge visited once → O(V + E)\n- Space: Queue + visited array → O(V)","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"BFS itself is optimal for traversal. No better approach exists for level-order traversal.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"Same as above BFS approach","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"// Same as brute (BFS is optimal)"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(V + E)","space":"O(V)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"- BFS is already optimal","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Use queue for BFS\n- [ ] Visit level by level\n- [ ] Mark visited when pushing"}]}', 
  '["BFS is optimal","Avoid revisiting nodes","Queue drives traversal"]', '[]', '[]',
  'bfs-traversal-of-graph
BFS Traversal of Graph
Graphs
BFS
Given a graph of V vertices represented by an adjacency list, perform Breadth First Search (BFS) starting from vertex 0.

Return the BFS traversal of the graph.
- V: number of vertices
- adj: adjacency list where adj[i] contains neighbors of node i
- Graph is connected
- Return list of nodes in BFS traversal order
```txt
Input:
V = 5
adj = [[1,2,3],[0],[0,4],[0],[2]]

Output:
0 1 2 3 4
```
Start from node 0 → visit all its neighbors (1,2,3) → then visit neighbors of 2 → node 4.
BFS explores nodes level by level. We use a queue to process nodes in the order they are discovered. Start from node 0, visit all neighbors, then move to next level.
1. Create a visited array
2. Initialize queue and push node 0
3. Mark node 0 as visited
4. While queue is not empty:
   - Pop node
   - Add to result
   - Push all unvisited neighbors into queue
5. Return result
{"engine":"mermaid","steps":[{"label":"Initial Graph","description":"Graph structure: 0 connects to 1,2,3. 2 connects to 4.","code":"graph TD\n    0((0)) --- 1((1))\n    0((0)) --- 2((2))\n    0((0)) --- 3((3))\n    2((2)) --- 4((4))\n    style 0 fill:#bfdbfe,stroke:#3b82f6,stroke-width:2"},{"label":"Start BFS - Queue: [0]","description":"Visit node 0, add to result, push neighbors to queue.","code":"graph TD\n    0((0)) --- 1((1))\n    0((0)) --- 2((2))\n    0((0)) --- 3((3))\n    2((2)) --- 4((4))\n    style 0 fill:#22c55e,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:3"},{"label":"Visit Level 1 - Queue: [1,2,3]","description":"Visit 1,2,3 (neighbors of 0). Result: [0,1,2,3]","code":"graph TD\n    0((0)) --- 1((1))\n    0((0)) --- 2((2))\n    0((0)) --- 3((3))\n    2((2)) --- 4((4))\n    style 0 fill:#22c55e,stroke:#16a34a\n    style 1 fill:#22c55e,stroke:#16a34a\n    style 2 fill:#22c55e,stroke:#16a34a\n    style 3 fill:#22c55e,stroke:#16a34a"},{"label":"Visit Level 2 - Queue: [4]","description":"Visit 4 (neighbor of 2). Result: [0,1,2,3,4]","code":"graph TD\n    0((0)) --- 1((1))\n    0((0)) --- 2((2))\n    0((0)) --- 3((3))\n    2((2)) --- 4((4))\n    style 0 fill:#22c55e,stroke:#16a34a\n    style 1 fill:#22c55e,stroke:#16a34a\n    style 2 fill:#22c55e,stroke:#16a34a\n    style 3 fill:#22c55e,stroke:#16a34a\n    style 4 fill:#22c55e,stroke:#16a34a"},{"label":"BFS Complete","description":"Final traversal order: 0 → 1 → 2 → 3 → 4","code":"graph LR\n    A[Result: 0] --> B[1] --> C[2] --> D[3] --> E[4]\n    style A fill:#22c55e,stroke:#16a34a\n    style B fill:#22c55e,stroke:#16a34a\n    style C fill:#22c55e,stroke:#16a34a\n    style D fill:#22c55e,stroke:#16a34a\n    style E fill:#22c55e,stroke:#16a34a"}]}
- Time: Each node and edge visited once → O(V + E)
- Space: Queue + visited array → O(V)
BFS itself is optimal for traversal. No better approach exists for level-order traversal.
Same as above BFS approach
- BFS is already optimal
Use queue for BFS
Visit level by level
Mark visited when pushing
BFS is optimal
Avoid revisiting nodes
Queue drives traversal', '2026-04-10T17:10:02.486Z'
);

