  'connected-components-in-an-undirected-graph', 'problem', 'Connected Components in an Undirected Graph', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/connected-components-in-an-undirected-graph/1', 'medium', 'Graphs', 'DFS',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given an undirected graph with V vertices numbered from 0 to V-1 and E edges, represented as a 2D array edges[][], where each entry edges[i] = [u, v] denotes an edge between vertices u and v.\n\nReturn a list of all connected components. Each connected component should be represented as a list of its vertices.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nInteger V (number of vertices)\n2D array edges[][] where edges[i] = [u, v] representing undirected edges\n\n### Output\nReturn a list of lists, where each inner list represents a connected component containing its vertices.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input:\nV = 5\nedges = [[0,1],[2,1],[3,4]]\n\nOutput:\n[[0,1,2],[3,4]]","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Start DFS from node 0 → reach 1 → reach 2 → first component = [0,1,2]\nNext unvisited node = 3 → reach 4 → second component = [3,4]","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Try to group nodes by checking reachability manually between nodes. This leads to redundant traversals and inefficient grouping.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Build adjacency list\n2. For each node, check reachable nodes using DFS\n3. Store reachable nodes as a component\n4. Mark visited nodes to avoid repetition","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Solution {\n    void dfs(int node, boolean[] visited, ArrayList<ArrayList<Integer>> adj, ArrayList<Integer> comp) {\n        visited[node] = true;\n        comp.add(node);\n        for (int nei : adj.get(node)) {\n            if (!visited[nei]) dfs(nei, visited, adj, comp);\n        }\n    }\n\n    public ArrayList<ArrayList<Integer>> connectedcomponents(int V, int[][] edges) {\n        ArrayList<ArrayList<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());\n\n        for (int[] e : edges) {\n            adj.get(e[0]).add(e[1]);\n            adj.get(e[1]).add(e[0]);\n        }\n\n        boolean[] visited = new boolean[V];\n        ArrayList<ArrayList<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {\n            if (!visited[i]) {\n                ArrayList<Integer> comp = new ArrayList<>();\n                dfs(i, visited, adj, comp);\n                res.add(comp);\n            }\n        }\n\n        return res;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(V + E)","space":"O(V)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"DFS visits every node once and processes each edge twice in undirected graph. Storing components requires O(V) space.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"This problem is inherently optimal with DFS/BFS. The only improvement is clean traversal and avoiding redundant work.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Build adjacency list\n2. Maintain visited array\n3. For each unvisited node, run DFS\n4. Collect nodes into component list\n5. Add component to result","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"/* Same as above */"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(V + E)","space":"O(V)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"i=0 → DFS → [0,1,2]\ni=3 → DFS → [3,4]"},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Optimal traversal since every node and edge is processed once.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Build adjacency list\n- [ ] Run DFS from each unvisited node\n- [ ] Store nodes in component list"}]}', 
  '["DFS/BFS traversal","Each DFS gives one component","Time complexity O(V+E)"]', '[]', '[]',
  'connected-components-in-an-undirected-graph
Connected Components in an Undirected Graph
Graphs
DFS
Given an undirected graph with V vertices numbered from 0 to V-1 and E edges, represented as a 2D array edges[][], where each entry edges[i] = [u, v] denotes an edge between vertices u and v.

Return a list of all connected components. Each connected component should be represented as a list of its vertices.
Integer V (number of vertices)
2D array edges[][] where edges[i] = [u, v] representing undirected edges
Return a list of lists, where each inner list represents a connected component containing its vertices.
Input:
V = 5
edges = [[0,1],[2,1],[3,4]]

Output:
[[0,1,2],[3,4]]
Start DFS from node 0 → reach 1 → reach 2 → first component = [0,1,2]
Next unvisited node = 3 → reach 4 → second component = [3,4]
Try to group nodes by checking reachability manually between nodes. This leads to redundant traversals and inefficient grouping.
1. Build adjacency list
2. For each node, check reachable nodes using DFS
3. Store reachable nodes as a component
4. Mark visited nodes to avoid repetition
Start from 0 → collect [0,1,2]
Then from 3 → collect [3,4]
{"engine":"mermaid","steps":[{"label":"Grouping","description":"Final components","code":"graph TD\nC1[[0,1,2]]\nC2[[3,4]]"}]}
DFS visits every node once and processes each edge twice in undirected graph. Storing components requires O(V) space.
This problem is inherently optimal with DFS/BFS. The only improvement is clean traversal and avoiding redundant work.
1. Build adjacency list
2. Maintain visited array
3. For each unvisited node, run DFS
4. Collect nodes into component list
5. Add component to result
i=0 → DFS → [0,1,2]
i=3 → DFS → [3,4]
Optimal traversal since every node and edge is processed once.
Build adjacency list
Run DFS from each unvisited node
Store nodes in component list
DFS/BFS traversal
Each DFS gives one component
Time complexity O(V+E)', '2026-04-11T04:10:59.611Z'
);

