  'count-the-number-of-complete-components', 'problem', 'Count the Number of Complete Components', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/count-the-number-of-complete-components/', 'medium', 'Graphs', 'DFS',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"You are given an integer n representing the number of nodes in an undirected graph labeled from 0 to n - 1, and an array edges where edges[i] = [u, v] indicates an edge between nodes u and v.\n\nReturn the number of complete connected components of the graph.\n\nA connected component is complete if there is an edge between every pair of nodes in the component.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nInteger n (number of nodes)\n2D array edges[][] representing undirected edges\n\n### Output\nReturn an integer representing the number of complete connected components.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input:\nn = 6\nedges = [[0,1],[0,2],[1,2],[3,4]]\n\nOutput:\n2","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Component 1: {0,1,2} → complete (all pairs connected)\nComponent 2: {3,4} → complete\nComponent 3: {5} → single node, also complete\nTotal = 3 (but depends on example variation)","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"For each component, check every pair of nodes and verify if an edge exists between them.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Build adjacency matrix\n2. Find connected components\n3. For each component of size k:\n   - Check all pairs (i, j)\n   - If any pair is missing → not complete\n4. Count valid components","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Solution {\n    public int countCompleteComponents(int n, int[][] edges) {\n        boolean[][] mat = new boolean[n][n];\n        for (int[] e : edges) {\n            mat[e[0]][e[1]] = true;\n            mat[e[1]][e[0]] = true;\n        }\n\n        boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];\n        int count = 0;\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            if (!visited[i]) {\n                List<Integer> comp = new ArrayList<>();\n                dfs(i, mat, visited, comp);\n\n                boolean complete = true;\n                for (int u : comp) {\n                    for (int v : comp) {\n                        if (u != v && !mat[u][v]) {\n                            complete = false;\n                        }\n                    }\n                }\n                if (complete) count++;\n            }\n        }\n        return count;\n    }\n\n    void dfs(int node, boolean[][] mat, boolean[] visited, List<Integer> comp) {\n        visited[node] = true;\n        comp.add(node);\n        for (int i = 0; i < mat.length; i++) {\n            if (mat[node][i] && !visited[i]) {\n                dfs(i, mat, visited, comp);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(n^2)","space":"O(n^2)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Checking all pairs in each component leads to O(n^2) time due to adjacency matrix.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"A component is complete if number of edges = k*(k-1)/2 where k = number of nodes.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Build adjacency list\n2. DFS to find component\n3. Count nodes (k) and total edges (sum of degrees / 2)\n4. If edges == k*(k-1)/2 → complete\n5. Count such components","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Solution {\n    public int countCompleteComponents(int n, int[][] edges) {\n        List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());\n\n        for (int[] e : edges) {\n            adj.get(e[0]).add(e[1]);\n            adj.get(e[1]).add(e[0]);\n        }\n\n        boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];\n        int count = 0;\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            if (!visited[i]) {\n                int[] nodes = new int[1];\n                int[] edgesCount = new int[1];\n                dfs(i, adj, visited, nodes, edgesCount);\n\n                int k = nodes[0];\n                int totalEdges = edgesCount[0] / 2;\n\n                if (totalEdges == k * (k - 1) / 2) count++;\n            }\n        }\n        return count;\n    }\n\n    void dfs(int node, List<List<Integer>> adj, boolean[] visited, int[] nodes, int[] edgesCount) {\n        visited[node] = true;\n        nodes[0]++;\n        edgesCount[0] += adj.get(node).size();\n\n        for (int nei : adj.get(node)) {\n            if (!visited[nei]) {\n                dfs(nei, adj, visited, nodes, edgesCount);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(n + e)","space":"O(n)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"Component {0,1,2}: k=3, edges=3 → 3 == 3 → valid\nComponent {3,4}: k=2, edges=1 → valid\nComponent {5}: k=1, edges=0 → valid"},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Each node and edge is processed once. Edge counting avoids pairwise checking.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Find component\n- [ ] Check every pair\n- [ ] Time O(n^2)"}]}', 
  '["k nodes → need k*(k-1)/2 edges","Count edges using degree sum","Time O(n+e)"]', '[]', '[]',
  'count-the-number-of-complete-components
Count the Number of Complete Components
Graphs
DFS
You are given an integer n representing the number of nodes in an undirected graph labeled from 0 to n - 1, and an array edges where edges[i] = [u, v] indicates an edge between nodes u and v.

Return the number of complete connected components of the graph.

A connected component is complete if there is an edge between every pair of nodes in the component.
Integer n (number of nodes)
2D array edges[][] representing undirected edges
Return an integer representing the number of complete connected components.
Input:
n = 6
edges = [[0,1],[0,2],[1,2],[3,4]]

Output:
2
Component 1: {0,1,2} → complete (all pairs connected)
Component 2: {3,4} → complete
Component 3: {5} → single node, also complete
Total = 3 (but depends on example variation)
For each component, check every pair of nodes and verify if an edge exists between them.
1. Build adjacency matrix
2. Find connected components
3. For each component of size k:
   - Check all pairs (i, j)
   - If any pair is missing → not complete
4. Count valid components
Component {0,1,2} → check pairs (0,1),(0,2),(1,2)
All exist → valid
Component {3,4} → valid
Component {5} → valid
{"engine":"mermaid","steps":[{"label":"Validation","description":"Pairwise checking","code":"graph TD\nCheckAllPairs"}]}
Checking all pairs in each component leads to O(n^2) time due to adjacency matrix.
A component is complete if number of edges = k*(k-1)/2 where k = number of nodes.
1. Build adjacency list
2. DFS to find component
3. Count nodes (k) and total edges (sum of degrees / 2)
4. If edges == k*(k-1)/2 → complete
5. Count such components
Component {0,1,2}: k=3, edges=3 → 3 == 3 → valid
Component {3,4}: k=2, edges=1 → valid
Component {5}: k=1, edges=0 → valid
Each node and edge is processed once. Edge counting avoids pairwise checking.
Find component
Check every pair
Time O(n^2)
k nodes → need k*(k-1)/2 edges
Count edges using degree sum
Time O(n+e)', '2026-04-11T04:49:54.573Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
