  'min-steps-to-reach-target-by-knight', 'problem', 'Minimum Steps to Reach Target (Knight''s Tour BFS)', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/steps-by-knight5927/1', 'medium', 'Graphs', 'Breadth First Search (BFS)',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given a square chessboard of size **N x N** and the initial position of a Knight and a target position. Find the minimum number of steps the Knight needs to reach the target position. The knight moves in an ''L'' shape: two squares in one cardinal direction and then one square perpendicular to that.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\n- `N`: Size of the board.\n- `knightPos[]`: Initial position {x, y}.\n- `targetPos[]`: Target position {x, y}.\n\n### Output\nInteger representing the minimum moves.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: N = 6, knightPos = {4, 5}, targetPos = {1, 1}\nOutput: 3","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"The knight can follow the path (4,5) -> (3,3) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) in 3 steps.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Explore all possible paths using Depth First Search (DFS).","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"Recursively try all 8 possible moves from the current position. However, this is highly inefficient as it leads to redundant calculations and potential infinite loops without strict path tracking.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int minStepToReachTarget(int knightPos[], int targetPos[], int n) {\n        // Brute force DFS usually results in TLE\n        return -1;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(8^(N^2))","space":"O(N^2)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Exponential time complexity due to exploring all branching paths.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Since the problem asks for the shortest path in an unweighted grid, BFS is the most efficient choice.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Use a Queue to store coordinates and a 2D array to avoid cycles.\n2. Start BFS from knightPos.\n3. For each level, explore all 8 L-shaped moves.\n4. If a move matches targetPos, return steps.\n5. Ensure moves stay within boundaries [1, N].","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    private int bfs(int knightPos[], int targetPos[], int n, int[][] nghPos) {\n        // 1. Immediate match check\n        if (knightPos[0] == targetPos[0] && knightPos[1] == targetPos[1]) return 0;\n\n        Queue<int[]> q = new LinkedList<>();\n        // 2. Use n + 1 size to avoid OutOfBounds if coordinates are 1 to N\n        int[][] visited = new int[n + 1][n + 1]; \n        \n        q.offer(new int[]{knightPos[0], knightPos[1]});\n        visited[knightPos[0]][knightPos[1]] = 1;\n        \n        int steps = 0;\n        \n        while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n            int size = q.size();\n            steps++; // Increment steps for this level of movement\n            \n            while (size > 0) {\n                int[] current = q.poll();\n                \n                for (int i = 0; i < nghPos.length; i++) {\n                    int nx = current[0] + nghPos[i][0];\n                    int ny = current[1] + nghPos[i][1];\n                    \n                    // 3. Check if we found the target\n                    if (nx == targetPos[0] && ny == targetPos[1]) return steps;\n                    \n                    // 4. Boundary Check (1 to N)\n                    if (nx >= 1 && nx <= n && ny >= 1 && ny <= n && visited[nx][ny] == 0) {\n                        visited[nx][ny] = 1;\n                        q.offer(new int[]{nx, ny});\n                    }\n                }\n                size--;\n            }\n        }\n        return -1;\n    }\n    \n    public int minStepToReachTarget(int knightPos[], int targetPos[], int n) {\n        int[][] nghPos = {\n            {-2, 1}, {-2, -1}, {2, -1}, {2, 1},\n            {-1, 2}, {1, 2}, {-1, -2}, {1, -2}\n        };\n        \n        // If the problem uses 1-based indexing, just use the coordinates directly \n        // and adjust the ''visited'' array size and boundary checks to match.\n        return bfs(knightPos, targetPos, n, nghPos);\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N^2)","space":"O(N^2)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"Level 0: {4,5}\nLevel 1: Moves from {4,5}\nLevel 2: Moves from Level 1..."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Every cell is visited at most once.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] DFS explores all paths.\n- [ ] Inefficient for shortest path."}]}', 
  '["BFS for shortest distance.","Visited array prevents OOM.","Check boundaries (1 to N)."]', '[]', '[]',
  'min-steps-to-reach-target-by-knight
Minimum Steps to Reach Target (Knight''s Tour BFS)
Graphs
Breadth First Search (BFS)
Given a square chessboard of size **N x N** and the initial position of a Knight and a target position. Find the minimum number of steps the Knight needs to reach the target position. The knight moves in an ''L'' shape: two squares in one cardinal direction and then one square perpendicular to that.
- `N`: Size of the board.
- `knightPos[]`: Initial position {x, y}.
- `targetPos[]`: Target position {x, y}.
Integer representing the minimum moves.
Input: N = 6, knightPos = {4, 5}, targetPos = {1, 1}
Output: 3
The knight can follow the path (4,5) -> (3,3) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) in 3 steps.
Explore all possible paths using Depth First Search (DFS).
Recursively try all 8 possible moves from the current position. However, this is highly inefficient as it leads to redundant calculations and potential infinite loops without strict path tracking.
Starting at (4,5), try (2,6), (2,4), (6,6), (6,4), etc., keeping track of visited nodes to avoid cycles.
Exponential time complexity due to exploring all branching paths.
Since the problem asks for the shortest path in an unweighted grid, BFS is the most efficient choice.
1. Use a Queue to store coordinates and a 2D array to avoid cycles.
2. Start BFS from knightPos.
3. For each level, explore all 8 L-shaped moves.
4. If a move matches targetPos, return steps.
5. Ensure moves stay within boundaries [1, N].
Level 0: {4,5}
Level 1: Moves from {4,5}
Level 2: Moves from Level 1...
Every cell is visited at most once.
DFS explores all paths.
Inefficient for shortest path.
BFS for shortest distance.
Visited array prevents OOM.
Check boundaries (1 to N).', '2026-04-14T04:01:34.099Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
