) VALUES (
  'flood-fill', 'problem', 'Flood Fill', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/flood-fill/', 'medium', 'Graph', 'DFS / BFS on Grid',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"You are given an image represented by an m x n grid. Each cell represents the color of a pixel.\n\nYou are also given a starting pixel (sr, sc) and a new color.\n\nPerform a flood fill: change the color of the starting pixel and all connected pixels with the same original color (4-directionally) to the new color.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nimage[][] → grid of colors\nsr, sc → starting pixel\ncolor → new color\n\n### Output\nReturn the modified image after flood fill.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input:\nimage = [[1,1,1],[1,1,0],[1,0,1]]\nsr = 1, sc = 1, color = 2\n\nOutput:\n[[2,2,2],[2,2,0],[2,0,1]]","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Start from (1,1). Traverse all connected cells with same color and update them.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Repeatedly scan the entire grid to update colors based on neighbors until no more changes occur.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"Iterate through the entire grid multiple times. For each cell, if it''s the target color and adjacent to an already-filled cell, update it. Stop when a full pass results in no changes.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"// Not recommended: Multi-pass approach\n// Time: O((M*N)^2)"}],"metadata":{"time":"O((m*n)^2)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"In the worst case (a long snake-like path), you might only fill one new pixel per full grid scan.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Treat the grid as a graph where each pixel is a node and edges exist between 4-directional neighbors of the same color. A single traversal (DFS or BFS) visits each connected node exactly once.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Identify the original color at (sr, sc).\n2. If original color is already the new color, return immediately to avoid infinite loops.\n3. Use a queue (BFS) or recursion (DFS) to visit all adjacent pixels with the original color.\n4. Change each visited pixel''s color to the new color.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"// DFS Approach\nclass Solution {\n    public int[][] floodFill(int[][] image, int sr, int sc, int color) {\n        int original = image[sr][sc];\n        if (original == color) return image;\n        dfs(image, sr, sc, original, color);\n        return image;\n    }\n\n    private void dfs(int[][] image, int r, int c, int original, int color) {\n        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= image.length || c >= image[0].length || image[r][c] != original)\n            return;\n\n        image[r][c] = color;\n        dfs(image, r + 1, c, original, color);\n        dfs(image, r - 1, c, original, color);\n        dfs(image, r, c + 1, original, color);\n        dfs(image, r, c - 1, original, color);\n    }\n}\n\n// BFS Approach\nclass Solution {\n    public int[][] floodFill(int[][] image, int sr, int sc, int color) {\n        int startColor = image[sr][sc];\n        if (startColor == color) return image;\n\n        int m = image.length, n = image[0].length;\n        int[][] dir = {{-1, 0}, {0, -1}, {1, 0}, {0, 1}};\n        \n        Queue<int[]> q = new LinkedList<>();\n        q.offer(new int[]{sr, sc});\n        image[sr][sc] = color; // Mark as visited by changing color\n\n        while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n            int[] curr = q.poll();\n            for (int[] d : dir) {\n                int i = curr[0] + d[0];\n                int j = curr[1] + d[1];\n\n                if (i >= 0 && i < m && j >= 0 && j < n && image[i][j] == startColor) {\n                    image[i][j] = color;\n                    q.offer(new int[]{i, j});\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return image;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(m*n)","space":"O(m*n)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"Queue: [(1,1)] -> Pop (1,1), Color it 2, Push neighbors with color 1 -> Queue: [(0,1), (1,0), (2,1)]..."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time: Every pixel is processed at most once. Space: In the worst case, the stack (DFS) or queue (BFS) holds O(m*n) pixels.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Repeated grid scans\n- [ ] O((N*M)^2) complexity"}]}', 
  '["Single traversal (DFS/BFS)","Edge Case: Original color == New color (must handle to avoid infinite loop)","4-directional connectivity"]', '[]', '[]',
  'flood-fill
Flood Fill
Graph
DFS / BFS on Grid
You are given an image represented by an m x n grid. Each cell represents the color of a pixel.

You are also given a starting pixel (sr, sc) and a new color.

Perform a flood fill: change the color of the starting pixel and all connected pixels with the same original color (4-directionally) to the new color.
image[][] → grid of colors
sr, sc → starting pixel
color → new color
Return the modified image after flood fill.
Input:
image = [[1,1,1],[1,1,0],[1,0,1]]
sr = 1, sc = 1, color = 2

Output:
[[2,2,2],[2,2,0],[2,0,1]]
Start from (1,1). Traverse all connected cells with same color and update them.
Repeatedly scan the entire grid to update colors based on neighbors until no more changes occur.
Iterate through the entire grid multiple times. For each cell, if it''s the target color and adjacent to an already-filled cell, update it. Stop when a full pass results in no changes.
A 3x3 grid might require multiple full passes to propagate the color from one corner to another.
In the worst case (a long snake-like path), you might only fill one new pixel per full grid scan.
Treat the grid as a graph where each pixel is a node and edges exist between 4-directional neighbors of the same color. A single traversal (DFS or BFS) visits each connected node exactly once.
1. Identify the original color at (sr, sc).
2. If original color is already the new color, return immediately to avoid infinite loops.
3. Use a queue (BFS) or recursion (DFS) to visit all adjacent pixels with the original color.
4. Change each visited pixel''s color to the new color.
Queue: [(1,1)] -> Pop (1,1), Color it 2, Push neighbors with color 1 -> Queue: [(0,1), (1,0), (2,1)]...
Time: Every pixel is processed at most once. Space: In the worst case, the stack (DFS) or queue (BFS) holds O(m*n) pixels.
Repeated grid scans
O((N*M)^2) complexity
Single traversal (DFS/BFS)
Edge Case: Original color == New color (must handle to avoid infinite loop)
4-directional connectivity', '2026-04-15T03:42:37.708Z'
);

