  'single-element-in-a-sorted-array', 'problem', 'Single Element in a Sorted Array', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/single-element-in-a-sorted-array/', 'medium', 'Array', 'Binary Search',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"You are given a sorted array consisting of only integers where every element appears exactly twice, except for one element which appears exactly once.\n\nReturn the single element that appears only once.\n\nYour solution must run in O(log n) time and O(1) space.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nA sorted integer array nums.\n\n### Output\nThe single integer that appears once.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: nums = [1,1,2,3,3,4,4,8,8]\nOutput: 2","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Before the ''2'', pairs start at even indices (0, 2). After the ''2'', pairs start at odd indices (4, 6).","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Traverse the array and compare each element with its neighbor. If an element doesn''t have a matching neighbor, it''s the single one.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Iterate through the array with a step of 2.\n2. If nums[i] is not equal to nums[i+1], then nums[i] is the single element.\n3. If the loop ends, the last element is the single one.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int singleNonDuplicate(int[] nums) {\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 1; i += 2) {\n            if (nums[i] != nums[i + 1]) return nums[i];\n        }\n        return nums[nums.length - 1];\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"We may potentially scan the entire array once.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Use Binary Search. We need to find the ''partition'' where the index pattern shifts from (even, odd) to (odd, even). If we are on an even index and the next element is the same, the single element is to the right.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Initialize low = 0, high = nums.length - 2.\n2. While low <= high:\n3. Find mid. Ensure we check the pair property.\n4. A clever trick: If we use `mid = mid ^ 1`, it toggles an even index to the next odd, and an odd index to the previous even.\n5. If nums[mid] == nums[mid ^ 1], we are in the left half, move low = mid + 1.\n6. Otherwise, move high = mid - 1.\n7. Return nums[low].","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int singleNonDuplicate(int[] nums) {\n        int low = 0, high = nums.length - 2;\n        while (low <= high) {\n            int mid = (low + high) / 2;\n            // The XOR trick checks the partner (even->next odd, odd->prev even)\n            if (nums[mid] == nums[mid ^ 1]) {\n                low = mid + 1;\n            } else {\n                high = mid - 1;\n            }\n        }\n        return nums[low];\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(log N)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"nums = [1,1,2,3,3], low=0, high=3\nMid=1: nums[1]==nums[1^1] (nums[1]==nums[0]) is 1==1. low=2.\nMid=2: nums[2]==nums[2^1] (nums[2]==nums[3]) is 2==3. false. high=1.\nLoop ends, return nums[low] which is 2."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Binary search halves the search space in each step.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Linear scan with step of 2\n- [ ] Compare nums[i] with nums[i+1]\n- [ ] O(N) time"}]}', 
  '["Binary Search on index pairs","Left of single: (Even, Odd) pairs","Right of single: (Odd, Even) pairs","Use mid ^ 1 trick to check partner","O(log N) time"]', '[]', '[]',
  'single-element-in-a-sorted-array
Single Element in a Sorted Array
Array
Binary Search
You are given a sorted array consisting of only integers where every element appears exactly twice, except for one element which appears exactly once.

Return the single element that appears only once.

Your solution must run in O(log n) time and O(1) space.
A sorted integer array nums.
The single integer that appears once.
Input: nums = [1,1,2,3,3,4,4,8,8]
Output: 2
Before the ''2'', pairs start at even indices (0, 2). After the ''2'', pairs start at odd indices (4, 6).
Traverse the array and compare each element with its neighbor. If an element doesn''t have a matching neighbor, it''s the single one.
1. Iterate through the array with a step of 2.
2. If nums[i] is not equal to nums[i+1], then nums[i] is the single element.
3. If the loop ends, the last element is the single one.
nums = [1,1,2,3,3]
i=0: nums[0]==nums[1] (1==1), continue.
i=2: nums[2]!=nums[3] (2!=3), found 2!
We may potentially scan the entire array once.
Use Binary Search. We need to find the ''partition'' where the index pattern shifts from (even, odd) to (odd, even). If we are on an even index and the next element is the same, the single element is to the right.
1. Initialize low = 0, high = nums.length - 2.
2. While low <= high:
3. Find mid. Ensure we check the pair property.
4. A clever trick: If we use `mid = mid ^ 1`, it toggles an even index to the next odd, and an odd index to the previous even.
5. If nums[mid] == nums[mid ^ 1], we are in the left half, move low = mid + 1.
6. Otherwise, move high = mid - 1.
7. Return nums[low].
nums = [1,1,2,3,3], low=0, high=3
Mid=1: nums[1]==nums[1^1] (nums[1]==nums[0]) is 1==1. low=2.
Mid=2: nums[2]==nums[2^1] (nums[2]==nums[3]) is 2==3. false. high=1.
Loop ends, return nums[low] which is 2.
Binary search halves the search space in each step.
Linear scan with step of 2
Compare nums[i] with nums[i+1]
O(N) time
Binary Search on index pairs
Left of single: (Even, Odd) pairs
Right of single: (Odd, Even) pairs
Use mid ^ 1 trick to check partner
O(log N) time', '2026-04-15T06:40:28.828Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
