  'maximum-subarray', 'problem', 'Maximum Subarray', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-subarray/', 'medium', 'Array', 'Kadane''s Algorithm',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given an integer array `nums`, find the subarray with the largest sum, and return its sum.\n\nA **subarray** is a contiguous part of an array.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn integer array nums.\n\n### Output\nThe maximum possible sum of a contiguous subarray.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: nums = [-2,1,-3,4,-1,2,1,-5,4]\nOutput: 6\nExplanation: The subarray [4,-1,2,1] has the largest sum = 6.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"The max sum is found by picking the sequence 4, -1, 2, 1. Even though -1 is negative, the overall sum increases later.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Check every possible subarray sum by using two nested loops to define the start and end of the subarray, and a third loop to calculate the sum.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Initialize maxi to a very small number.\n2. Use a loop i from 0 to n-1 (start of subarray).\n3. Use a nested loop j from i to n-1 (end of subarray).\n4. Calculate sum of elements from i to j.\n5. Update maxi = max(maxi, sum).","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int maxSubArray(int[] nums) {\n        int maxi = Integer.MIN_VALUE;\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n            int sum = 0;\n            for (int j = i; j < nums.length; j++) {\n                sum += nums[j];\n                maxi = Math.max(maxi, sum);\n            }\n        }\n        return maxi;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N^2)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"We use two nested loops to traverse the array.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Kadane''s Algorithm: As we traverse, we keep adding elements to a running sum. If the running sum becomes negative, it''s better to ''discard'' it and start fresh from the current element, because a negative prefix will only decrease our future potential sum.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Initialize sum = 0 and maxi = nums[0].\n2. Iterate through the array.\n3. Add current element to sum.\n4. Update maxi = max(maxi, sum).\n5. If sum < 0, reset sum to 0.\n6. Return maxi.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int maxSubArray(int[] nums) {\n        int maxi = Integer.MIN_VALUE;\n        int sum = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n            sum += nums[i];\n            if (sum > maxi) maxi = sum;\n            if (sum < 0) sum = 0;\n        }\n        return maxi;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"nums = [-2, 1, -3, 4]\n1. sum = -2, maxi = -2 -> sum < 0, reset sum = 0\n2. sum = 1, maxi = 1\n3. sum = 1 + (-3) = -2, maxi = 1 -> sum < 0, reset sum = 0\n4. sum = 4, maxi = 4\nFinal Answer: 4"},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"We traverse the array exactly once.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Check all subarrays\n- [ ] O(N^2) or O(N^3) time"}]}', 
  '["Kadane''s Algorithm","Keep adding to sum","If sum < 0, reset to 0","Always track global maxi","O(N) time"]', '[]', '[]',
  'maximum-subarray
Maximum Subarray
Array
Kadane''s Algorithm
Given an integer array `nums`, find the subarray with the largest sum, and return its sum.

A **subarray** is a contiguous part of an array.
An integer array nums.
The maximum possible sum of a contiguous subarray.
Input: nums = [-2,1,-3,4,-1,2,1,-5,4]
Output: 6
Explanation: The subarray [4,-1,2,1] has the largest sum = 6.
The max sum is found by picking the sequence 4, -1, 2, 1. Even though -1 is negative, the overall sum increases later.
Check every possible subarray sum by using two nested loops to define the start and end of the subarray, and a third loop to calculate the sum.
1. Initialize maxi to a very small number.
2. Use a loop i from 0 to n-1 (start of subarray).
3. Use a nested loop j from i to n-1 (end of subarray).
4. Calculate sum of elements from i to j.
5. Update maxi = max(maxi, sum).
nums = [-2, 1]
i=0, j=0: sum=-2
i=0, j=1: sum=-1
i=1, j=1: sum=1
Max is 1.
We use two nested loops to traverse the array.
Kadane''s Algorithm: As we traverse, we keep adding elements to a running sum. If the running sum becomes negative, it''s better to ''discard'' it and start fresh from the current element, because a negative prefix will only decrease our future potential sum.
1. Initialize sum = 0 and maxi = nums[0].
2. Iterate through the array.
3. Add current element to sum.
4. Update maxi = max(maxi, sum).
5. If sum < 0, reset sum to 0.
6. Return maxi.
nums = [-2, 1, -3, 4]
1. sum = -2, maxi = -2 -> sum < 0, reset sum = 0
2. sum = 1, maxi = 1
3. sum = 1 + (-3) = -2, maxi = 1 -> sum < 0, reset sum = 0
4. sum = 4, maxi = 4
Final Answer: 4
We traverse the array exactly once.
Check all subarrays
O(N^2) or O(N^3) time
Kadane''s Algorithm
Keep adding to sum
If sum < 0, reset to 0
Always track global maxi
O(N) time', '2026-04-15T06:45:46.072Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
