  'largest-number', 'problem', 'Largest Number', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/largest-number/', 'medium', 'Array', 'Custom Sorting',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given a list of non-negative integers `nums`, arrange them such that they form the largest number and return it as a string.\n\nSince the result may be very large, you need to return a string instead of an integer.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn array of non-negative integers nums.\n\n### Output\nA string representing the largest possible number.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: nums = [3,30,34,5,9]\nOutput: \"9534330\"","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"When comparing 3 and 30:\n- 3 + 30 = \"330\"\n- 30 + 3 = \"303\"\nSince 330 > 303, 3 comes before 30.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Generate all possible permutations of the numbers, concatenate them into strings, and find the maximum one.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Use recursion to find all permutations of the array.\n2. For each permutation, join the numbers into a single string.\n3. Compare all resulting strings and keep track of the largest.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"// Brute force via permutations is O(N!) and usually not practical to implement for large N.\n// It is purely theoretical for this problem."}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N!)","space":"O(N!)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"There are N! permutations for an array of size N.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Instead of checking all permutations, we define a custom comparison rule between any two numbers A and B: compare (A + B) with (B + A). If (B + A) is greater, then B should come before A. This rule is transitive, allowing us to use standard sorting algorithms.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Convert all integers in the array to strings.\n2. Sort the string array using a custom comparator: `(s1, s2) -> (s2 + s1).compareTo(s1 + s2)`.\n3. After sorting, if the largest number (first element) is \"0\", the entire result is \"0\".\n4. Join all sorted strings into one and return.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"import java.util.*;\n\nclass Solution {\n    public String largestNumber(int[] nums) {\n        String[] asStrs = new String[nums.length];\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n            asStrs[i] = String.valueOf(nums[i]);\n        }\n\n        Arrays.sort(asStrs, (a, b) -> (b + a).compareTo(a + b));\n\n        if (asStrs[0].equals(\"0\")) return \"0\";\n\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (String s : asStrs) sb.append(s);\n        return sb.toString();\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N log N)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"nums = [3, 30]\n1. Strings: \"3\", \"30\"\n2. Compare \"330\" and \"303\": \"330\" is larger.\n3. Sorted order: [\"3\", \"30\"]\n4. Result: \"330\""},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Sorting takes O(N log N) comparisons, and each comparison involves string concatenation and comparison of length K (where K is avg number of digits). Space is used to store the string versions of numbers.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Generate all permutations\n- [ ] Very slow: O(N!)"}]}', 
  '["Custom sort comparator","Compare (b+a) with (a+b)","Handle leading zero edge case","O(N log N) time"]', '[]', '[]',
  'largest-number
Largest Number
Array
Custom Sorting
Given a list of non-negative integers `nums`, arrange them such that they form the largest number and return it as a string.

Since the result may be very large, you need to return a string instead of an integer.
An array of non-negative integers nums.
A string representing the largest possible number.
Input: nums = [3,30,34,5,9]
Output: "9534330"
When comparing 3 and 30:
- 3 + 30 = "330"
- 30 + 3 = "303"
Since 330 > 303, 3 comes before 30.
Generate all possible permutations of the numbers, concatenate them into strings, and find the maximum one.
1. Use recursion to find all permutations of the array.
2. For each permutation, join the numbers into a single string.
3. Compare all resulting strings and keep track of the largest.
nums = [10, 2]
Perms: [10,2] -> "102", [2,10] -> "210"
Max: "210"
There are N! permutations for an array of size N.
Instead of checking all permutations, we define a custom comparison rule between any two numbers A and B: compare (A + B) with (B + A). If (B + A) is greater, then B should come before A. This rule is transitive, allowing us to use standard sorting algorithms.
1. Convert all integers in the array to strings.
2. Sort the string array using a custom comparator: `(s1, s2) -> (s2 + s1).compareTo(s1 + s2)`.
3. After sorting, if the largest number (first element) is "0", the entire result is "0".
4. Join all sorted strings into one and return.
nums = [3, 30]
1. Strings: "3", "30"
2. Compare "330" and "303": "330" is larger.
3. Sorted order: ["3", "30"]
4. Result: "330"
Sorting takes O(N log N) comparisons, and each comparison involves string concatenation and comparison of length K (where K is avg number of digits). Space is used to store the string versions of numbers.
Generate all permutations
Very slow: O(N!)
Custom sort comparator
Compare (b+a) with (a+b)
Handle leading zero edge case
O(N log N) time', '2026-04-15T06:48:25.824Z'
);

