  'move-zeroes', 'problem', 'Move Zeroes', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/move-zeroes/', 'medium', 'Array', 'Two Pointers',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given an integer array `nums`, move all `0`''s to the end of it while maintaining the relative order of the non-zero elements.\n\nNote that you must do this **in-place** without making a copy of the array.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn integer array `nums`.\n\n### Output\nVoid (Modify the input array in-place).","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: nums = [0,1,0,3,12]\nOutput: [1,3,12,0,0]","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Non-zero elements (1, 3, 12) are shifted to the front while preserving their order, and zeroes are filled in the remaining spots at the end.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Create a temporary array. Copy all non-zero elements into it first, then fill the rest with zeroes. Finally, copy the temp array back to the original.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Create a new array of the same size.\n2. Iterate through the original array and add non-zero elements to the new array.\n3. Fill the remaining positions in the new array with 0.\n4. Copy elements back to the original array.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public void moveZeroes(int[] nums) {\n        int n = nums.length;\n        int[] temp = new int[n];\n        int j = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            if (nums[i] != 0) {\n                temp[j++] = nums[i];\n            }\n        }\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            nums[i] = temp[i];\n        }\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"We use an additional array of size N to store the elements.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Use a ''read'' pointer and a ''write'' pointer. The write pointer only moves when we find a non-zero element. This allows us to overwrite the array from the beginning without needing extra space.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Initialize `insertPos = 0`.\n2. Iterate through the array using index `i`.\n3. Whenever `nums[i]` is non-zero, swap `nums[i]` with `nums[insertPos]` and increment `insertPos`.\n4. Swapping ensures that zeroes are naturally pushed to the back while order is preserved.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public void moveZeroes(int[] nums) {\n        int insertPos = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n            if (nums[i] != 0) {\n                // Swap current non-zero element with the element at insertPos\n                int temp = nums[insertPos];\n                nums[insertPos] = nums[i];\n                nums[i] = temp;\n                insertPos++;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"nums = [0,1,0,3,12]\n- i=0: 0, insertPos=0\n- i=1: 1, swap(0,1) -> [1,0,0,3,12], insertPos=1\n- i=2: 0, continue\n- i=3: 3, swap(1,3) -> [1,3,0,0,12], insertPos=2\n- i=4: 12, swap(2,4) -> [1,3,12,0,0], insertPos=3"},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"We only use a single loop and a few variables; no extra data structures are used.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Use a temp array\n- [ ] Copy non-zeroes first\n- [ ] Fill rest with zeroes\n- [ ] O(N) space"}]}', 
  '["Two Pointers (read and write)","Swap non-zeroes with insertPos","In-place modification","Preserves relative order","O(1) space"]', '[]', '[]',
  'move-zeroes
Move Zeroes
Array
Two Pointers
Given an integer array `nums`, move all `0`''s to the end of it while maintaining the relative order of the non-zero elements.

Note that you must do this **in-place** without making a copy of the array.
An integer array `nums`.
Void (Modify the input array in-place).
Input: nums = [0,1,0,3,12]
Output: [1,3,12,0,0]
Non-zero elements (1, 3, 12) are shifted to the front while preserving their order, and zeroes are filled in the remaining spots at the end.
Create a temporary array. Copy all non-zero elements into it first, then fill the rest with zeroes. Finally, copy the temp array back to the original.
1. Create a new array of the same size.
2. Iterate through the original array and add non-zero elements to the new array.
3. Fill the remaining positions in the new array with 0.
4. Copy elements back to the original array.
nums = [0,1,0,3]
Temp = [1,3,0,0]
Copy back to nums.
We use an additional array of size N to store the elements.
Use a ''read'' pointer and a ''write'' pointer. The write pointer only moves when we find a non-zero element. This allows us to overwrite the array from the beginning without needing extra space.
1. Initialize `insertPos = 0`.
2. Iterate through the array using index `i`.
3. Whenever `nums[i]` is non-zero, swap `nums[i]` with `nums[insertPos]` and increment `insertPos`.
4. Swapping ensures that zeroes are naturally pushed to the back while order is preserved.
nums = [0,1,0,3,12]
- i=0: 0, insertPos=0
- i=1: 1, swap(0,1) -> [1,0,0,3,12], insertPos=1
- i=2: 0, continue
- i=3: 3, swap(1,3) -> [1,3,0,0,12], insertPos=2
- i=4: 12, swap(2,4) -> [1,3,12,0,0], insertPos=3
We only use a single loop and a few variables; no extra data structures are used.
Use a temp array
Copy non-zeroes first
Fill rest with zeroes
O(N) space
Two Pointers (read and write)
Swap non-zeroes with insertPos
In-place modification
Preserves relative order
O(1) space', '2026-04-15T06:55:40.347Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'rearrange-array-elements-by-sign', 'problem', 'Rearrange Array Elements by Sign', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/rearrange-array-elements-by-sign/', 'medium', 'Array', 'Two Pointers',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"You are given a 0-indexed integer array `nums` of even length consisting of an equal number of positive and negative integers.\n\nYou should rearrange the elements of `nums` such that the modified array follows these conditions:\n1. Every consecutive pair of integers have opposite signs.\n2. For all integers with the same sign, the **relative order** in which they were present in `nums` is preserved.\n3. The rearranged array begins with a positive integer.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn integer array `nums` with equal positive and negative integers.\n\n### Output\nThe rearranged array meeting the sign and order conditions.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: nums = [3,1,-2,-5,2,-4]\nOutput: [3,-2,1,-5,2,-4]","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Positive integers: [3, 1, 2]. Negative integers: [-2, -5, -4]. We interleave them starting with positive, maintaining their original order.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Separate the positive and negative numbers into two distinct lists, then loop through both and pick one from each to fill a new result array.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Create two lists: `pos` and `neg`.\n2. Traverse `nums` and add positive numbers to `pos` and negative numbers to `neg`.\n3. Use a loop to fill the result array by alternating elements from `pos` and `neg`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int[] rearrangeArray(int[] nums) {\n        List<Integer> pos = new ArrayList<>();\n        List<Integer> neg = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int x : nums) {\n            if (x > 0) pos.add(x);\n            else neg.add(x);\n        }\n        int[] ans = new int[nums.length];\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length / 2; i++) {\n            ans[2 * i] = pos.get(i);\n            ans[2 * i + 1] = neg.get(i);\n        }\n        return ans;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"We iterate through the array twice and use extra space to store separated lists.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"We know positive integers will always occupy even indices (0, 2, 4...) and negative integers will occupy odd indices (1, 3, 5...). We can use two pointers to fill the result array in a single pass.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Initialize `posIdx = 0` and `negIdx = 1`.\n2. Create a result array `ans` of the same size as `nums`.\n3. Iterate through `nums` once.\n4. If the current number is positive, place it at `ans[posIdx]` and increment `posIdx` by 2.\n5. If the current number is negative, place it at `ans[negIdx]` and increment `negIdx` by 2.\n6. Return `ans`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int[] rearrangeArray(int[] nums) {\n        int n = nums.length;\n        int[] ans = new int[n];\n        int posIdx = 0, negIdx = 1;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            if (nums[i] > 0) {\n                ans[posIdx] = nums[i];\n                posIdx += 2;\n            } else {\n                ans[negIdx] = nums[i];\n                negIdx += 2;\n            }\n        }\n        return ans;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"nums = [3, -2, 1, -5]\n- 3 is positive: ans[0]=3, posIdx=2\n- -2 is negative: ans[1]=-2, negIdx=3\n- 1 is positive: ans[2]=1, posIdx=4\n- -5 is negative: ans[3]=-5, negIdx=5"},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"We traverse the array once. The result array takes O(N) space, which is required for the output.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Separate positives and negatives into lists\n- [ ] Fill result by alternating\n- [ ] Two passes over data"}]}', 
  '["Single pass with two pointers","posIdx starts at 0, negIdx starts at 1","Increment pointers by 2 after placement","Maintain relative order automatically"]', '[]', '[]',
  'rearrange-array-elements-by-sign
Rearrange Array Elements by Sign
Array
Two Pointers
You are given a 0-indexed integer array `nums` of even length consisting of an equal number of positive and negative integers.

You should rearrange the elements of `nums` such that the modified array follows these conditions:
1. Every consecutive pair of integers have opposite signs.
2. For all integers with the same sign, the **relative order** in which they were present in `nums` is preserved.
3. The rearranged array begins with a positive integer.
An integer array `nums` with equal positive and negative integers.
The rearranged array meeting the sign and order conditions.
Input: nums = [3,1,-2,-5,2,-4]
Output: [3,-2,1,-5,2,-4]
Positive integers: [3, 1, 2]. Negative integers: [-2, -5, -4]. We interleave them starting with positive, maintaining their original order.
Separate the positive and negative numbers into two distinct lists, then loop through both and pick one from each to fill a new result array.
