  'search-a-2d-matrix', 'problem', 'Search a 2D Matrix', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/search-a-2d-matrix/', 'medium', 'Matrix', 'Binary Search',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"You are given an `m x n` integer matrix `matrix` with the following two properties:\n1. Each row is sorted in non-decreasing order.\n2. The first integer of each row is greater than the last integer of the previous row.\n\nGiven an integer `target`, return `true` if `target` is in `matrix` or `false` otherwise.\n\nYou must write a solution in `O(log(m * n))` time complexity.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn `m x n` matrix and an integer `target`.\n\n### Output\nBoolean (true if target exists, false otherwise).","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: matrix = [[1,3,5,7],[10,11,16,20],[23,30,34,60]], target = 3\nOutput: true","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"The values flow continuously: 7 is followed by 10, 20 is followed by 23. This allows us to treat the grid as a single sorted list: [1, 3, 5, 7, 10, 11, 16, 20, 23, 30, 34, 60].","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Perform a linear scan through every row and every column until the target is found or the end of the matrix is reached.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Loop through each row from i = 0 to m-1.\n2. Loop through each column from j = 0 to n-1.\n3. If matrix[i][j] == target, return true.\n4. If loops finish, return false.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public boolean searchMatrix(int[][] matrix, int target) {\n        for (int i = 0; i < matrix.length; i++) {\n            for (int j = 0; j < matrix[0].length; j++) {\n                if (matrix[i][j] == target) return true;\n            }\n        }\n        return false;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(M * N)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"In the worst case, we visit every single element in the matrix.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Since the matrix is fully sorted, we treat it as a virtual 1D array. A 1D index `idx` can be converted to 2D coordinates using: `row = idx / n` and `col = idx % n` (where `n` is the number of columns). We then apply standard Binary Search.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Initialize low = 0 and high = (m * n) - 1.\n2. While low <= high:\n3. Find mid = low + (high - low) / 2.\n4. Get matrix value: `val = matrix[mid / n][mid % n]`.\n5. If val == target, return true.\n6. If val < target, low = mid + 1.\n7. Else, high = mid - 1.\n8. Return false if not found.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public boolean searchMatrix(int[][] matrix, int target) {\n        if (matrix == null || matrix.length == 0) return false;\n        int m = matrix.length;\n        int n = matrix[0].length;\n        int low = 0, high = m * n - 1;\n        \n        while (low <= high) {\n            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n            int midVal = matrix[mid / n][mid % n];\n            \n            if (midVal == target) return true;\n            else if (midVal < target) low = mid + 1;\n            else high = mid - 1;\n        }\n        return false;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(log(M * N))","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"matrix [3x4], target = 3\nlow=0, high=11\nmid=5 -> row=1, col=1 -> matrix[1][1] = 11\n11 > 3, so high=4\nmid=2 -> row=0, col=2 -> matrix[0][2] = 5\n5 > 3, so high=1\nmid=0 -> row=0, col=0 -> matrix[0][0] = 1\n1 < 3, so low=1\nmid=1 -> row=0, col=1 -> matrix[0][1] = 3. Found!"},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Binary search divides the search space in half each time across M*N total elements.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Linear scan (M*N)\n- [ ] O(M*N) time"}]}', 
  '["Virtual 1D Binary Search","row = mid / n, col = mid % n","O(log(M*N)) time","O(1) space"]', '[]', '[]',
  'search-a-2d-matrix
Search a 2D Matrix
Matrix
Binary Search
You are given an `m x n` integer matrix `matrix` with the following two properties:
1. Each row is sorted in non-decreasing order.
2. The first integer of each row is greater than the last integer of the previous row.

Given an integer `target`, return `true` if `target` is in `matrix` or `false` otherwise.

You must write a solution in `O(log(m * n))` time complexity.
An `m x n` matrix and an integer `target`.
Boolean (true if target exists, false otherwise).
Input: matrix = [[1,3,5,7],[10,11,16,20],[23,30,34,60]], target = 3
Output: true
The values flow continuously: 7 is followed by 10, 20 is followed by 23. This allows us to treat the grid as a single sorted list: [1, 3, 5, 7, 10, 11, 16, 20, 23, 30, 34, 60].
Perform a linear scan through every row and every column until the target is found or the end of the matrix is reached.
1. Loop through each row from i = 0 to m-1.
2. Loop through each column from j = 0 to n-1.
3. If matrix[i][j] == target, return true.
4. If loops finish, return false.
matrix = [[1, 3]], target = 3
Check (0,0): 1 != 3
Check (0,1): 3 == 3. Found!
In the worst case, we visit every single element in the matrix.
Since the matrix is fully sorted, we treat it as a virtual 1D array. A 1D index `idx` can be converted to 2D coordinates using: `row = idx / n` and `col = idx % n` (where `n` is the number of columns). We then apply standard Binary Search.
1. Initialize low = 0 and high = (m * n) - 1.
2. While low <= high:
3. Find mid = low + (high - low) / 2.
4. Get matrix value: `val = matrix[mid / n][mid % n]`.
5. If val == target, return true.
6. If val < target, low = mid + 1.
7. Else, high = mid - 1.
8. Return false if not found.
matrix [3x4], target = 3
low=0, high=11
mid=5 -> row=1, col=1 -> matrix[1][1] = 11
11 > 3, so high=4
mid=2 -> row=0, col=2 -> matrix[0][2] = 5
5 > 3, so high=1
mid=0 -> row=0, col=0 -> matrix[0][0] = 1
1 < 3, so low=1
mid=1 -> row=0, col=1 -> matrix[0][1] = 3. Found!
Binary search divides the search space in half each time across M*N total elements.
Linear scan (M*N)
O(M*N) time
Virtual 1D Binary Search
row = mid / n, col = mid % n
O(log(M*N)) time
O(1) space', '2026-04-15T07:10:58.550Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
