  'best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock', 'problem', 'Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock/', 'medium', 'Arrays', 'State Tracking / Sliding Window',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"You are given an array `prices` where `prices[i]` is the price of a given stock on the `i-th` day.\n\nYou want to maximize your profit by choosing a **single day** to buy one stock and choosing a **different day in the future** to sell that stock.\n\nReturn the maximum profit you can achieve from this transaction. If you cannot achieve any profit, return `0`.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn integer array `prices` representing daily stock prices.\n\n### Output\nAn integer representing the maximum possible profit. Return 0 if no profit is possible.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: prices = [7,1,5,3,6,4]\nOutput: 5\nExplanation: Buy on day 2 (price = 1) and sell on day 5 (price = 6), profit = 6 - 1 = 5.\nNote that buying on day 2 and selling on day 1 is not allowed because you must buy before you sell.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Plain Recursion / Nested Loops\nThe most intuitive way to solve this is to test every single possible buying day against every single possible future selling day. We calculate the profit for every valid pair and keep track of the maximum profit found.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Initialize `maxProfit = 0`.\n2. Use an outer loop `i` to represent the buy day, iterating from `0` to `n-1`.\n3. Use an inner loop `j` to represent the sell day, iterating from `i+1` to `n`.\n4. Calculate the current profit as `prices[j] - prices[i]`.\n5. Update `maxProfit` with `Math.max(maxProfit, profit)`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int maxProfit(int[] prices) {\n        int max = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < prices.length; i++) {\n            for (int j = i + 1; j < prices.length; j++) {\n                int currentProfit = prices[j] - prices[i];\n                max = Math.max(max, currentProfit);\n            }\n        }\n        return max;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N^2)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Why is this optimal?\nInstead of looking forward at every possible future day (which takes $O(N^2)$ time), we can look backwards as we traverse the array. On any given day, the absolute best profit we could possibly make by selling today is dictated purely by the **lowest historical price** we have seen so far. \n\nWe only need to track two states as we walk through time: the lowest historical buy price (`minPrice`) and the highest profit recorded so far (`maxProfit`).","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Initialize `minPrice` to `Integer.MAX_VALUE` (to ensure the first price replaces it) and `maxProfit` to `0`.\n2. Loop through each `price` in the array once.\n3. If `price` is less than `minPrice`, we have found a new historical low. Update `minPrice = price`.\n4. If `price` is greater than or equal to `minPrice`, we calculate the potential profit if we sold today: `price - minPrice`.\n5. Update `maxProfit` to track the absolute largest profit encountered.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int maxProfit(int[] prices) {\n        int minPrice = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n        int maxProfit = 0;\n        for (int price : prices) {\n            if (price < minPrice) {\n                minPrice = price;\n            } else {\n                maxProfit = Math.max(maxProfit, price - minPrice);\n            }\n        }\n        return maxProfit;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"prices = [7,1,5,3,6,4]. Day 1 (7): minPrice becomes 7. Day 2 (1): 1 < 7, minPrice becomes 1. Day 3 (5): 5 > 1, potential profit is 5 - 1 = 4. maxProfit becomes 4. Day 4 (3): profit is 3 - 1 = 2 (ignored). Day 5 (6): profit is 6 - 1 = 5. maxProfit updates to 5. Result is 5."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time Complexity: O(N) because we only need to pass through the `prices` array exactly one time. Space Complexity: O(1) because we only use two primitive integer variables (`minPrice` and `maxProfit`), allocating no additional scalable memory regardless of the input size.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Checks all valid combinations using nested loops.\n- [ ] Inefficient O(N^2) runtime causes Time Limit Exceeded (TLE) on massive datasets."}]}', 
  '["Core Logic: To maximize profit, you must sell at the current price minus the absolute lowest price seen previously.","Maintains a running `minPrice` to track historical lows seamlessly.","Runs in a single O(N) pass utilizing O(1) constant space."]', '[]', '[]',
  'best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock
Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock
Arrays
State Tracking / Sliding Window
You are given an array `prices` where `prices[i]` is the price of a given stock on the `i-th` day.

You want to maximize your profit by choosing a **single day** to buy one stock and choosing a **different day in the future** to sell that stock.

Return the maximum profit you can achieve from this transaction. If you cannot achieve any profit, return `0`.
An integer array `prices` representing daily stock prices.
An integer representing the maximum possible profit. Return 0 if no profit is possible.
Input: prices = [7,1,5,3,6,4]
Output: 5
Explanation: Buy on day 2 (price = 1) and sell on day 5 (price = 6), profit = 6 - 1 = 5.
Note that buying on day 2 and selling on day 1 is not allowed because you must buy before you sell.
### Plain Recursion / Nested Loops
The most intuitive way to solve this is to test every single possible buying day against every single possible future selling day. We calculate the profit for every valid pair and keep track of the maximum profit found.
1. Initialize `maxProfit = 0`.
2. Use an outer loop `i` to represent the buy day, iterating from `0` to `n-1`.
3. Use an inner loop `j` to represent the sell day, iterating from `i+1` to `n`.
4. Calculate the current profit as `prices[j] - prices[i]`.
5. Update `maxProfit` with `Math.max(maxProfit, profit)`.
### Why is this optimal?
Instead of looking forward at every possible future day (which takes $O(N^2)$ time), we can look backwards as we traverse the array. On any given day, the absolute best profit we could possibly make by selling today is dictated purely by the **lowest historical price** we have seen so far. 

We only need to track two states as we walk through time: the lowest historical buy price (`minPrice`) and the highest profit recorded so far (`maxProfit`).
1. Initialize `minPrice` to `Integer.MAX_VALUE` (to ensure the first price replaces it) and `maxProfit` to `0`.
2. Loop through each `price` in the array once.
3. If `price` is less than `minPrice`, we have found a new historical low. Update `minPrice = price`.
4. If `price` is greater than or equal to `minPrice`, we calculate the potential profit if we sold today: `price - minPrice`.
5. Update `maxProfit` to track the absolute largest profit encountered.
prices = [7,1,5,3,6,4]. Day 1 (7): minPrice becomes 7. Day 2 (1): 1 < 7, minPrice becomes 1. Day 3 (5): 5 > 1, potential profit is 5 - 1 = 4. maxProfit becomes 4. Day 4 (3): profit is 3 - 1 = 2 (ignored). Day 5 (6): profit is 6 - 1 = 5. maxProfit updates to 5. Result is 5.
Time Complexity: O(N) because we only need to pass through the `prices` array exactly one time. Space Complexity: O(1) because we only use two primitive integer variables (`minPrice` and `maxProfit`), allocating no additional scalable memory regardless of the input size.
Checks all valid combinations using nested loops.
Inefficient O(N^2) runtime causes Time Limit Exceeded (TLE) on massive datasets.
Core Logic: To maximize profit, you must sell at the current price minus the absolute lowest price seen previously.
Maintains a running `minPrice` to track historical lows seamlessly.
Runs in a single O(N) pass utilizing O(1) constant space.', '2026-05-24T16:29:49.331Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
