  'union-of-two-sorted-arrays', 'problem', 'Union of Two Sorted Arrays', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/union-of-two-sorted-arrays-1587115621/1', 'medium', 'Array', 'Two Pointers / Merge Process',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given two sorted arrays `a[]` and `b[]`, find the union of these two arrays. The union of two arrays can be defined as the set containing distinct elements from both arrays. The elements in the union should be in **sorted order**.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nTwo sorted integer arrays `a[]` and `b[]`.\n\n### Output\nAn ArrayList of integers representing the union of the two arrays in sorted order.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: a[] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], b[] = [1, 2, 3, 6, 7]\nOutput: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]\nExplanation: Distinct elements from both arrays are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"We combine elements from both arrays. Elements like 1, 2, and 3 appear in both but are only included once in the result to maintain ''distinct'' property.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Use a `Set` data structure (specifically a `TreeSet` to keep it sorted) to store all elements from both arrays. The set automatically handles duplicates.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Initialize a `TreeSet`.\n2. Iterate through array `a` and add all elements to the set.\n3. Iterate through array `b` and add all elements to the set.\n4. Convert the set to a list and return.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"import java.util.*;\nclass Solution {\n    public static ArrayList<Integer> findUnion(int a[], int b[]) {\n        TreeSet<Integer> set = new TreeSet<>();\n        for (int x : a) set.add(x);\n        for (int x : b) set.add(x);\n        return new ArrayList<>(set);\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O((N+M) log(N+M))","space":"O(N+M)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Adding elements to a TreeSet takes logarithmic time, and we do this for all elements in both arrays.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Since the arrays are already sorted, we can use two pointers (one for each array). Compare the elements at both pointers, pick the smaller one, and move that pointer forward. To handle duplicates, only add the element if it is different from the last element added to the result.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Initialize `i = 0`, `j = 0` and an empty list `union`.\n2. While `i < n` and `j < m`:\n   - If `a[i] <= b[j]`: Add `a[i]` to `union` (if not duplicate) and `i++`.\n   - Else: Add `b[j]` to `union` (if not duplicate) and `j++`.\n3. Add remaining elements from array `a` or `b` while checking for duplicates.\n4. Return `union`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public static ArrayList<Integer> findUnion(int a[], int b[]) {\n        ArrayList<Integer> union = new ArrayList<>();\n        int i = 0, j = 0;\n        int n = a.length, m = b.length;\n        \n        while (i < n && j < m) {\n            int val;\n            if (a[i] <= b[j]) {\n                val = a[i++];\n            } else {\n                val = b[j++];\n            }\n            if (union.isEmpty() || union.get(union.size() - 1) != val) {\n                union.add(val);\n            }\n        }\n        \n        while (i < n) {\n            int val = a[i++];\n            if (union.get(union.size() - 1) != val) union.add(val);\n        }\n        \n        while (j < m) {\n            int val = b[j++];\n            if (union.get(union.size() - 1) != val) union.add(val);\n        }\n        \n        return union;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N + M)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"a=[1,2,3], b=[2,4]\n1. i=0, j=0: 1 < 2. Add 1. i=1.\n2. i=1, j=0: 2 == 2. Add 2. i=2, j=1.\n3. i=2, j=1: 3 < 4. Add 3. i=3.\n4. Array a exhausted. j=1: Add 4. j=2.\nResult: [1, 2, 3, 4]"},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"We traverse both arrays exactly once. The space complexity is O(1) if we don''t count the space required for the output list.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Use TreeSet for uniqueness and sorting\n- [ ] O((N+M) log (N+M)) time"}]}', 
  '["Two-Pointer merge strategy","Compare a[i] and b[j]","Add smaller value and check for duplicates with previous entry","O(N+M) time, O(1) extra space"]', '[]', '[]',
  'union-of-two-sorted-arrays
Union of Two Sorted Arrays
Array
Two Pointers / Merge Process
Given two sorted arrays `a[]` and `b[]`, find the union of these two arrays. The union of two arrays can be defined as the set containing distinct elements from both arrays. The elements in the union should be in **sorted order**.
Two sorted integer arrays `a[]` and `b[]`.
An ArrayList of integers representing the union of the two arrays in sorted order.
Input: a[] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], b[] = [1, 2, 3, 6, 7]
Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]
Explanation: Distinct elements from both arrays are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7.
We combine elements from both arrays. Elements like 1, 2, and 3 appear in both but are only included once in the result to maintain ''distinct'' property.
Use a `Set` data structure (specifically a `TreeSet` to keep it sorted) to store all elements from both arrays. The set automatically handles duplicates.
1. Initialize a `TreeSet`.
2. Iterate through array `a` and add all elements to the set.
3. Iterate through array `b` and add all elements to the set.
4. Convert the set to a list and return.
a=[1,2], b=[2,3]
Set: {1, 2} -> {1, 2, 3}
Result: [1, 2, 3]
Adding elements to a TreeSet takes logarithmic time, and we do this for all elements in both arrays.
Since the arrays are already sorted, we can use two pointers (one for each array). Compare the elements at both pointers, pick the smaller one, and move that pointer forward. To handle duplicates, only add the element if it is different from the last element added to the result.
1. Initialize `i = 0`, `j = 0` and an empty list `union`.
2. While `i < n` and `j < m`:
   - If `a[i] <= b[j]`: Add `a[i]` to `union` (if not duplicate) and `i++`.
   - Else: Add `b[j]` to `union` (if not duplicate) and `j++`.
3. Add remaining elements from array `a` or `b` while checking for duplicates.
4. Return `union`.
a=[1,2,3], b=[2,4]
1. i=0, j=0: 1 < 2. Add 1. i=1.
2. i=1, j=0: 2 == 2. Add 2. i=2, j=1.
3. i=2, j=1: 3 < 4. Add 3. i=3.
4. Array a exhausted. j=1: Add 4. j=2.
Result: [1, 2, 3, 4]
We traverse both arrays exactly once. The space complexity is O(1) if we don''t count the space required for the output list.
Use TreeSet for uniqueness and sorting
O((N+M) log (N+M)) time
Two-Pointer merge strategy
Compare a[i] and b[j]
Add smaller value and check for duplicates with previous entry
O(N+M) time, O(1) extra space', '2026-04-15T07:28:28.195Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
