  'is-graph-bipartite', 'problem', 'Is Graph Bipartite?', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/is-graph-bipartite/', 'medium', 'Graph', 'BFS / DFS / Graph Coloring',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"There is an undirected graph with `n` nodes, where each node is numbered between `0` and `n - 1`. You are given a 2D array `graph`, where `graph[u]` is an array of nodes that node `u` is adjacent to.\n\nReturn `true` if and only if it is **bipartite**.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn adjacency list `graph` (2D array).\n\n### Output\nBoolean (true if bipartite, false otherwise).","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: graph = [[1,3],[0,2],[1,3],[0,2]]\nOutput: true\nExplanation: We can color nodes 0 and 2 with color 0, and nodes 1 and 3 with color 1.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"In this square-like graph (even cycle), we can alternate colors perfectly around the loop without any neighbors sharing a color.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Try every possible combination of coloring the $n$ nodes with 2 colors and check if any combination satisfies the condition.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Generate all $2^n$ possible colorings.\n2. For each coloring, check every edge $(u, v)$ to see if `color[u] == color[v]`.\n3. If any coloring works, return true.","codeBlocks":[],"metadata":{"time":"O(2^N * (V + E))","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Exponential time makes this unusable for $n > 20$.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Use BFS or DFS to color the graph. Start with an uncolored node, color it 0, and color all its neighbors 1. Then color their neighbors 0, and so on. If you ever encounter a neighbor that is already colored with the **same** color as the current node, the graph is not bipartite.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Create a `color` array initialized to -1 (uncolored).\n2. Loop through every node $i$ (to handle disconnected components).\n3. If node $i$ is uncolored, start a BFS/DFS.\n4. During traversal:\n   - For each neighbor $v$ of current node $u$:\n     - If $v$ is uncolored, give it the opposite color: `color[v] = 1 - color[u]`.\n     - If $v$ is already colored and `color[v] == color[u]`, return `false`.\n5. If all nodes are processed without conflict, return `true`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public boolean isBipartite(int[][] graph) {\n        int n = graph.length;\n        int[] color = new int[n];\n        Arrays.fill(color, -1);\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            if (color[i] == -1) {\n                if (!bfs(i, graph, color)) return false;\n            }\n        }\n        return true;\n    }\n\n    private boolean bfs(int start, int[][] graph, int[] color) {\n        Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();\n        q.add(start);\n        color[start] = 0;\n\n        while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n            int curr = q.poll();\n            for (int neighbor : graph[curr]) {\n                if (color[neighbor] == -1) {\n                    color[neighbor] = 1 - color[curr];\n                    q.add(neighbor);\n                } else if (color[neighbor] == color[curr]) {\n                    return false;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return true;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(V + E)","space":"O(V)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"Graph: 0-1, 1-2, 2-0 (Triangle)\n1. color[0]=0.\n2. Neighbor 1: color[1]=1.\n3. Neighbor 2: color[2]=1.\n4. Check node 1 neighbors: 0(color 0, ok), 2(color 1, same as 1!). Conflict found."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time: We visit every vertex and edge once. Space: Color array and Queue/Stack take O(V) space.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Try all 2^N color combinations\n- [ ] Check edges for each combination"}]}', 
  '["Bipartite == 2-Colorable == No Odd Cycles","Use BFS/DFS to assign alternating colors (0 and 1)","Conflict if color[neighbor] == color[current]","Loop through all nodes for disconnected components","O(V+E) time, O(V) space"]', '[]', '[]',
  'is-graph-bipartite
Is Graph Bipartite?
Graph
BFS / DFS / Graph Coloring
There is an undirected graph with `n` nodes, where each node is numbered between `0` and `n - 1`. You are given a 2D array `graph`, where `graph[u]` is an array of nodes that node `u` is adjacent to.

Return `true` if and only if it is **bipartite**.
An adjacency list `graph` (2D array).
Boolean (true if bipartite, false otherwise).
Input: graph = [[1,3],[0,2],[1,3],[0,2]]
Output: true
Explanation: We can color nodes 0 and 2 with color 0, and nodes 1 and 3 with color 1.
In this square-like graph (even cycle), we can alternate colors perfectly around the loop without any neighbors sharing a color.
Try every possible combination of coloring the $n$ nodes with 2 colors and check if any combination satisfies the condition.
1. Generate all $2^n$ possible colorings.
2. For each coloring, check every edge $(u, v)$ to see if `color[u] == color[v]`.
3. If any coloring works, return true.
For 3 nodes, try [R,R,R], [R,R,B], [R,B,R]... [B,B,B].
Exponential time makes this unusable for $n > 20$.
Use BFS or DFS to color the graph. Start with an uncolored node, color it 0, and color all its neighbors 1. Then color their neighbors 0, and so on. If you ever encounter a neighbor that is already colored with the **same** color as the current node, the graph is not bipartite.
1. Create a `color` array initialized to -1 (uncolored).
2. Loop through every node $i$ (to handle disconnected components).
3. If node $i$ is uncolored, start a BFS/DFS.
4. During traversal:
   - For each neighbor $v$ of current node $u$:
     - If $v$ is uncolored, give it the opposite color: `color[v] = 1 - color[u]`.
     - If $v$ is already colored and `color[v] == color[u]`, return `false`.
5. If all nodes are processed without conflict, return `true`.
Graph: 0-1, 1-2, 2-0 (Triangle)
1. color[0]=0.
2. Neighbor 1: color[1]=1.
3. Neighbor 2: color[2]=1.
4. Check node 1 neighbors: 0(color 0, ok), 2(color 1, same as 1!). Conflict found.
Time: We visit every vertex and edge once. Space: Color array and Queue/Stack take O(V) space.
Try all 2^N color combinations
Check edges for each combination
Bipartite == 2-Colorable == No Odd Cycles
Use BFS/DFS to assign alternating colors (0 and 1)
Conflict if color[neighbor] == color[current]
Loop through all nodes for disconnected components
O(V+E) time, O(V) space', '2026-04-23T15:13:56.886Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
