  'topological-sort', 'problem', 'Topological Sort', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/topological-sort/1', 'medium', 'Graph', 'Kahn''s (BFS) / Stack-based DFS',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG), find a linear ordering of vertices such that for every directed edge u -> v, u appears before v.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAdjacency list adj and vertex count V.\n\n### Output\nValid linear ordering array.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: V = 6, adj = [[], [], [3], [1], [0,1], [0,2]]\nOutput: [5, 4, 2, 3, 1, 0]","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Nodes with no incoming edges (5, 4) must come first. Node 0 depends on 4 and 5, so it appears later.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Kahn''s Algorithm (BFS)\nThink of it as ''removing dependencies''. We find nodes that don''t depend on anything (Indegree 0), process them, and then ''break'' their outgoing edges to see which nodes become independent next.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Fill an array `indegree[]` by counting incoming edges for each node.\n2. Add all nodes with `indegree == 0` to a Queue.\n3. While Queue is not empty:\n   - Poll node `u`, add to result.\n   - For each neighbor `v`, decrement `indegree[v]`.\n   - If `indegree[v]` hits 0, add to Queue.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"static int[] topoSortBFS(int V, ArrayList<ArrayList<Integer>> adj) {\n    int[] indegree = new int[V];\n    for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {\n        for (int it : adj.get(i)) indegree[it]++;\n    }\n    Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {\n        if (indegree[i] == 0) q.add(i);\n    }\n    int[] topo = new int[V];\n    int i = 0;\n    while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n        int node = q.poll();\n        topo[i++] = node;\n        for (int it : adj.get(node)) {\n            indegree[it]--;\n            if (indegree[it] == 0) q.add(it);\n        }\n    }\n    return topo;\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(V + E)","space":"O(V)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Each vertex and edge is processed once. Space is used for the indegree array and queue.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### DFS with Stack\nThis is ''backwards logic''. We dive as deep as possible into a dependency chain. When we hit a node with no outgoing edges (or all neighbors visited), we know it can safely go last. We push it to a stack, and the stack naturally reverses this ''last-to-first'' order into ''first-to-last''.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Use a `visited` array and a `Stack`.\n2. Start DFS from an unvisited node.\n3. In DFS: Visit all unvisited neighbors recursively.\n4. **Key Step:** After all neighbors are visited, push the current node to the Stack.\n5. Result is the Stack popped from top to bottom.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"private static void dfs(int node, int[] vis, Stack<Integer> st, ArrayList<ArrayList<Integer>> adj) {\n    vis[node] = 1;\n    for (int it : adj.get(node)) {\n        if (vis[it] == 0) dfs(it, vis, st, adj);\n    }\n    st.push(node);\n}\n\nstatic int[] topoSortDFS(int V, ArrayList<ArrayList<Integer>> adj) {\n    int[] vis = new int[V];\n    Stack<Integer> st = new Stack<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {\n        if (vis[i] == 0) dfs(i, vis, st, adj);\n    }\n    int[] topo = new int[V];\n    int i = 0;\n    while (!st.isEmpty()) topo[i++] = st.pop();\n    return topo;\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(V + E)","space":"O(V)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"Start DFS(5):\n- Visit 2 -> Visit 3 -> Visit 1 (No neighbors)\n- Push 1 to Stack. Backtrack to 3.\n- Push 3 to Stack. Backtrack to 2.\n- Push 2 to Stack. Backtrack to 5.\n- 5 has neighbor 0 -> Visit 0 (No neighbors left)\n- Push 0, then Push 5.\nStack Top-Down: [5, 4, 2, 3, 1, 0]"},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time is linear as each node and edge is visited once. Space is V for the stack and recursion depth.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] BFS / Kahn''s\n- [ ] Uses Indegree\n- [ ] Result size check detects cycles"}]}', 
  '["DFS + Stack","Push to stack AFTER recursion","Reverse stack for result"]', '[]', '[]',
  'topological-sort
Topological Sort
Graph
Kahn''s (BFS) / Stack-based DFS
Given a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG), find a linear ordering of vertices such that for every directed edge u -> v, u appears before v.
Adjacency list adj and vertex count V.
Valid linear ordering array.
Input: V = 6, adj = [[], [], [3], [1], [0,1], [0,2]]
Output: [5, 4, 2, 3, 1, 0]
Nodes with no incoming edges (5, 4) must come first. Node 0 depends on 4 and 5, so it appears later.
### Kahn''s Algorithm (BFS)
Think of it as ''removing dependencies''. We find nodes that don''t depend on anything (Indegree 0), process them, and then ''break'' their outgoing edges to see which nodes become independent next.
1. Fill an array `indegree[]` by counting incoming edges for each node.
2. Add all nodes with `indegree == 0` to a Queue.
3. While Queue is not empty:
   - Poll node `u`, add to result.
   - For each neighbor `v`, decrement `indegree[v]`.
   - If `indegree[v]` hits 0, add to Queue.
Initial Indegrees: {0:2, 1:2, 2:1, 3:1, 4:0, 5:0}
1. Queue: [5, 4]
2. Pop 5: Result [5], Decr 2 and 0. (2''s Indegree becomes 0)
3. Queue: [4, 2]
4. Pop 4: Result [5, 4], Decr 0 and 1.
5. Pop 2: Result [5, 4, 2], Decr 3. (3''s Indegree becomes 0)...
Each vertex and edge is processed once. Space is used for the indegree array and queue.
### DFS with Stack
This is ''backwards logic''. We dive as deep as possible into a dependency chain. When we hit a node with no outgoing edges (or all neighbors visited), we know it can safely go last. We push it to a stack, and the stack naturally reverses this ''last-to-first'' order into ''first-to-last''.
1. Use a `visited` array and a `Stack`.
2. Start DFS from an unvisited node.
3. In DFS: Visit all unvisited neighbors recursively.
4. **Key Step:** After all neighbors are visited, push the current node to the Stack.
5. Result is the Stack popped from top to bottom.
Start DFS(5):
- Visit 2 -> Visit 3 -> Visit 1 (No neighbors)
- Push 1 to Stack. Backtrack to 3.
- Push 3 to Stack. Backtrack to 2.
- Push 2 to Stack. Backtrack to 5.
- 5 has neighbor 0 -> Visit 0 (No neighbors left)
- Push 0, then Push 5.
Stack Top-Down: [5, 4, 2, 3, 1, 0]
Time is linear as each node and edge is visited once. Space is V for the stack and recursion depth.
BFS / Kahn''s
Uses Indegree
Result size check detects cycles
DFS + Stack
Push to stack AFTER recursion
Reverse stack for result', '2026-04-28T15:43:36.426Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'course-schedule-ii', 'problem', 'Course Schedule II', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/course-schedule-ii/', 'medium', 'Graph', 'Topological Sort (Kahn''s Algorithm)',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"There are a total of `numCourses` courses you have to take, labeled from `0` to `numCourses - 1`. You are given an array `prerequisites` where `prerequisites[i] = [ai, bi]` indicates that you must take course `bi` first if you want to take course `ai`.\n\nReturn the ordering of courses you should take to finish all courses. If there are many valid answers, return **any** of them. If it is impossible to finish all courses, return an **empty array**.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn integer `numCourses` (V) and a 2D array `prerequisites` (E).\n\n### Output\nAn array representing the course sequence, or `[]` if impossible.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: numCourses = 4, prerequisites = [[1,0],[2,0],[3,1],[3,2]]\nOutput: [0,2,1,3]","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Course 0 has no prerequisites. Once 0 is done, 1 and 2 become available. Once both are done, 3 can be taken.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Check every possible permutation of the courses to see if any satisfy the prerequisite rules.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Generate all V! possible orderings of the courses.\n2. For each ordering, iterate through the list of E prerequisites.\n3. For a prerequisite [a, b], find the indices of a and b in the current ordering.\n4. If index(b) < index(a) for all prerequisites, the ordering is valid.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"public int[] findOrder(int numCourses, int[][] prerequisites) {\n    int[] courses = new int[numCourses];\n    for(int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) courses[i] = i;\n    List<int[]> result = new ArrayList<>();\n    // Standard backtracking permutation logic\n    generatePermutations(courses, 0, prerequisites, result);\n    return result.isEmpty() ? new int[0] : result.get(0);\n}\n\nprivate boolean isValid(int[] order, int[][] prerequisites) {\n    Map<Integer, Integer> pos = new HashMap<>();\n    for(int i = 0; i < order.length; i++) pos.put(order[i], i);\n    for(int[] pre : prerequisites) {\n        // course pre[1] must come before pre[0]\n        if(pos.get(pre[1]) > pos.get(pre[0])) return false;\n    }\n    return true;\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(V! * (V + E))","space":"O(V)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"There are V! permutations. For each, we spend O(V) to map positions and O(E) to validate prerequisites. This grows factorially, making it unusable for V > 10.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Treat courses as nodes in a graph. Use Kahn''s Algorithm (BFS) to pick courses with zero prerequisites, then update their dependencies.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Create an adjacency list representing the graph.\n2. Create an `indegree` array where `indegree[i]` is the number of prerequisites for course `i`.\n3. Add all courses with `indegree == 0` to a Queue.\n4. While the Queue is not empty:\n    - Poll course `u`, add it to the final result array.\n    - For every neighbor `v` of `u`, decrement `indegree[v]`.\n    - If `indegree[v]` becomes 0, add `v` to the Queue.\n5. If the result array contains fewer than `numCourses`, a cycle exists.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int[] findOrder(int numCourses, int[][] prerequisites) {\n        List<Integer>[] adj = new ArrayList[numCourses];\n        int[] indegree = new int[numCourses];\n        for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) adj[i] = new ArrayList<>();\n        \n        for (int[] pre : prerequisites) {\n            adj[pre[1]].add(pre[0]);\n            indegree[pre[0]]++;\n        }\n\n        Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) {\n            if (indegree[i] == 0) q.add(i);\n        }\n\n        int[] res = new int[numCourses];\n        int count = 0;\n        while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n            int curr = q.poll();\n            res[count++] = curr;\n            for (int next : adj[curr]) {\n                if (--indegree[next] == 0) q.add(next);\n            }\n        }\n        return count == numCourses ? res : new int[0];\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(V + E)","space":"O(V + E)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time: We visit each vertex once and each edge once during graph construction and BFS. Space: The adjacency list stores all E edges, and the indegree array/Queue store up to V vertices.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Generate all permutations: O(V!)\n- [ ] Validate each against E constraints\n- [ ] Total: O(V! * (V+E))"}]}', 
  '["Kahn''s Algorithm (BFS): O(V + E)","Space: O(V + E) for adjacency list","Cycle detection included (count != V)"]', '[]', '[]',
  'course-schedule-ii
Course Schedule II
Graph
Topological Sort (Kahn''s Algorithm)
There are a total of `numCourses` courses you have to take, labeled from `0` to `numCourses - 1`. You are given an array `prerequisites` where `prerequisites[i] = [ai, bi]` indicates that you must take course `bi` first if you want to take course `ai`.

Return the ordering of courses you should take to finish all courses. If there are many valid answers, return **any** of them. If it is impossible to finish all courses, return an **empty array**.
An integer `numCourses` (V) and a 2D array `prerequisites` (E).
An array representing the course sequence, or `[]` if impossible.
Input: numCourses = 4, prerequisites = [[1,0],[2,0],[3,1],[3,2]]
Output: [0,2,1,3]
Course 0 has no prerequisites. Once 0 is done, 1 and 2 become available. Once both are done, 3 can be taken.
Check every possible permutation of the courses to see if any satisfy the prerequisite rules.
1. Generate all V! possible orderings of the courses.
2. For each ordering, iterate through the list of E prerequisites.
3. For a prerequisite [a, b], find the indices of a and b in the current ordering.
4. If index(b) < index(a) for all prerequisites, the ordering is valid.
There are V! permutations. For each, we spend O(V) to map positions and O(E) to validate prerequisites. This grows factorially, making it unusable for V > 10.
Treat courses as nodes in a graph. Use Kahn''s Algorithm (BFS) to pick courses with zero prerequisites, then update their dependencies.
1. Create an adjacency list representing the graph.
2. Create an `indegree` array where `indegree[i]` is the number of prerequisites for course `i`.
3. Add all courses with `indegree == 0` to a Queue.
4. While the Queue is not empty:
    - Poll course `u`, add it to the final result array.
    - For every neighbor `v` of `u`, decrement `indegree[v]`.
    - If `indegree[v]` becomes 0, add `v` to the Queue.
5. If the result array contains fewer than `numCourses`, a cycle exists.
Time: We visit each vertex once and each edge once during graph construction and BFS. Space: The adjacency list stores all E edges, and the indegree array/Queue store up to V vertices.
Generate all permutations: O(V!)
Validate each against E constraints
Total: O(V! * (V+E))
Kahn''s Algorithm (BFS): O(V + E)
Space: O(V + E) for adjacency list
Cycle detection included (count != V)', '2026-04-25T06:58:40.777Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'find-eventual-safe-states', 'problem', 'Find Eventual Safe States', '', 'published', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/find-eventual-safe-states/', 'medium', 'Graph', 'Reverse Topological Sort (Kahn''s Algorithm)',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"There is a directed graph of `n` nodes with each node labeled from `0` to `n - 1`. A node is a **terminal node** if there are no outgoing edges. A node is a **safe node** if every possible path starting from that node leads to a terminal node (or another safe node).\n\nReturn an array containing all the safe nodes of the graph. The answer should be sorted in **ascending order**.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nA 2D integer array `graph` where `graph[i]` is an array of nodes adjacent to node `i`.\n\n### Output\nA sorted list of integers representing safe nodes.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: graph = [[1,2],[2,3],[5],[0],[5],[],[]]\nOutput: [2,4,5,6]\nExplanation: Nodes 5 and 6 are terminal nodes. Node 2 and 4 only point to node 5, so they are safe. Nodes 0, 1, and 3 are part of a cycle and are not safe.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Nodes 5 and 6 have no outgoing edges (terminal). Node 2 points to 5 (safe). Node 4 points to 5 (safe). Nodes 0, 1, and 3 form a cycle; you can be stuck there forever, so they aren''t safe.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"For every node, use DFS to explore all possible paths. If any path enters a cycle or fails to reach a terminal node, the starting node is not safe.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. For each node i from 0 to n-1:\n2. Perform DFS to check if a cycle is reachable.\n3. Keep track of visited nodes in the current path.\n4. If a node is part of a cycle, mark it as unsafe.","codeBlocks":[],"metadata":{"time":"O(V * (V + E))","space":"O(V)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"A node is safe if it doesn''t lead to a cycle. By reversing all edges, terminal nodes (out-degree 0) become start nodes (in-degree 0). We can then use Kahn''s Algorithm to gradually remove nodes that lead only to safe nodes.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. **Reverse the Graph**: Create a new adjacency list where every edge (u -> v) becomes (v -> u).\n2. **In-Degree Array**: Calculate the in-degree of all nodes in this **reversed** graph (this is equivalent to the out-degree in the original graph).\n3. **Queue Terminal Nodes**: Add all nodes with in-degree 0 to a queue.\n4. **BFS (Kahn''s)**:\n    - Pop node `u` (it is safe).\n    - For each neighbor `v` of `u` in the reversed graph:\n        - Decrement `indegree[v]`.\n        - If `indegree[v] == 0`, it means all paths from `v` in the original graph now lead to safe nodes. Add `v` to the queue.\n5. **Sort & Return**: Sort the collected safe nodes.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public List<Integer> eventualSafeNodes(int[][] graph) {\n        int n = graph.length;\n        List<List<Integer>> reverseAdj = new ArrayList<>();\n        int[] indegree = new int[n];\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) reverseAdj.add(new ArrayList<>());\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            for (int neighbor : graph[i]) {\n                // Original: i -> neighbor | Reverse: neighbor -> i\n                reverseAdj.get(neighbor).add(i);\n                indegree[i]++;\n            }\n        }\n\n        Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            if (indegree[i] == 0) q.add(i);\n        }\n\n        boolean[] safe = new boolean[n];\n        while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n            int curr = q.poll();\n            safe[curr] = true;\n            for (int neighbor : reverseAdj.get(curr)) {\n                if (--indegree[neighbor] == 0) q.add(neighbor);\n            }\n        }\n\n        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            if (safe[i]) result.add(i);\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(V + E)","space":"O(V + E)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time: Building the reverse graph takes O(V+E), and the BFS processes each node and edge once. Sorting the result takes O(V log V), but usually V is small enough that O(V+E) dominates. Space: O(V+E) for the reversed adjacency list.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] DFS from every node to detect cycles.\n- [ ] Inefficient due to repeated path traversals."}]}', 
  '["Safe nodes = nodes not in or leading to a cycle.","Reverse graph to turn ''out-degree 0'' into ''in-degree 0''.","Use Kahn''s Algorithm (BFS) to propagate ''safeness'' backwards.","Remaining nodes with in-degree > 0 are part of cycles."]', '[]', '[]',
  'find-eventual-safe-states
Find Eventual Safe States
Graph
Reverse Topological Sort (Kahn''s Algorithm)
There is a directed graph of `n` nodes with each node labeled from `0` to `n - 1`. A node is a **terminal node** if there are no outgoing edges. A node is a **safe node** if every possible path starting from that node leads to a terminal node (or another safe node).

Return an array containing all the safe nodes of the graph. The answer should be sorted in **ascending order**.
A 2D integer array `graph` where `graph[i]` is an array of nodes adjacent to node `i`.
A sorted list of integers representing safe nodes.
Input: graph = [[1,2],[2,3],[5],[0],[5],[],[]]
Output: [2,4,5,6]
Explanation: Nodes 5 and 6 are terminal nodes. Node 2 and 4 only point to node 5, so they are safe. Nodes 0, 1, and 3 are part of a cycle and are not safe.
Nodes 5 and 6 have no outgoing edges (terminal). Node 2 points to 5 (safe). Node 4 points to 5 (safe). Nodes 0, 1, and 3 form a cycle; you can be stuck there forever, so they aren''t safe.
For every node, use DFS to explore all possible paths. If any path enters a cycle or fails to reach a terminal node, the starting node is not safe.
1. For each node i from 0 to n-1:
2. Perform DFS to check if a cycle is reachable.
3. Keep track of visited nodes in the current path.
4. If a node is part of a cycle, mark it as unsafe.
A node is safe if it doesn''t lead to a cycle. By reversing all edges, terminal nodes (out-degree 0) become start nodes (in-degree 0). We can then use Kahn''s Algorithm to gradually remove nodes that lead only to safe nodes.
1. **Reverse the Graph**: Create a new adjacency list where every edge (u -> v) becomes (v -> u).
2. **In-Degree Array**: Calculate the in-degree of all nodes in this **reversed** graph (this is equivalent to the out-degree in the original graph).
3. **Queue Terminal Nodes**: Add all nodes with in-degree 0 to a queue.
4. **BFS (Kahn''s)**:
    - Pop node `u` (it is safe).
    - For each neighbor `v` of `u` in the reversed graph:
        - Decrement `indegree[v]`.
        - If `indegree[v] == 0`, it means all paths from `v` in the original graph now lead to safe nodes. Add `v` to the queue.
5. **Sort & Return**: Sort the collected safe nodes.
Time: Building the reverse graph takes O(V+E), and the BFS processes each node and edge once. Sorting the result takes O(V log V), but usually V is small enough that O(V+E) dominates. Space: O(V+E) for the reversed adjacency list.
DFS from every node to detect cycles.
Inefficient due to repeated path traversals.
Safe nodes = nodes not in or leading to a cycle.
Reverse graph to turn ''out-degree 0'' into ''in-degree 0''.
Use Kahn''s Algorithm (BFS) to propagate ''safeness'' backwards.
Remaining nodes with in-degree > 0 are part of cycles.', '2026-04-30T19:39:49.617Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
