  'shortest-path-undirected-unit-distance', 'problem', 'Shortest Path in Undirected Graph with Unit Distance', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/shortest-path-in-undirected-graph-having-unit-distance/1', 'medium', 'Graph', 'BFS',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"You are given an undirected graph with `V` nodes and `E` edges. Each edge has a weight of `1`. Given a source node `src`, find the shortest distance from `src` to all other nodes. If a node is unreachable, the distance should be `-1`.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn adjacency list `adj`, number of nodes `V`, and a source node `src`.\n\n### Output\nAn array of integers representing the shortest distance to each node from `src`.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: V = 5, E = 5, adj = [[1,3],[0,2],[1,4],[0,4],[3,2]], src = 0\nOutput: [0, 1, 2, 1, 2]","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Explore every possible path using DFS and keep track of the minimum distance for each node.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Perform a recursive DFS starting from the source.\n2. For each path, maintain a current distance.\n3. Update the global distance array only if the new path is shorter than the previously recorded one.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"public void dfs(int node, int d, int[] dist, List<List<Integer>> adj) {\n    if (dist[node] != -1 && d >= dist[node]) return;\n    dist[node] = d;\n    for (int neighbor : adj.get(node)) {\n        dfs(neighbor, d + 1, dist, adj);\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(V!)","space":"O(V)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"In the worst case (a highly connected graph), DFS explores all possible permutations of paths, leading to factorial time complexity.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Why BFS? In a graph where all edges have a weight of 1, BFS is the most efficient shortest-path algorithm. Unlike Dijkstra, which uses a Priority Queue to handle varying weights, BFS uses a simple Queue to explore nodes layer by layer. The first time a node is reached in BFS, it is guaranteed to be via the shortest path.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Initialize a `dist` array with -1 and set `dist[src] = 0`.\n2. Add the source node to a FIFO Queue.\n3. While the queue is not empty:\n    - Pop the front node `u`.\n    - For every neighbor `v` of `u`:\n        - If `dist[v] == -1` (not visited):\n            - Set `dist[v] = dist[u] + 1`.\n            - Add `v` to the queue.\n4. Return the `dist` array.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int[] shortestPath(int[][] edges, int n, int m, int src) {\n        List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();\n        for(int i=0; i<n; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());\n        for(int[] edge : edges) {\n            adj.get(edge[0]).add(edge[1]);\n            adj.get(edge[1]).add(edge[0]);\n        }\n\n        int[] dist = new int[n];\n        Arrays.fill(dist, -1);\n        dist[src] = 0;\n\n        Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();\n        q.add(src);\n\n        while(!q.isEmpty()) {\n            int u = q.poll();\n            for(int v : adj.get(u)) {\n                if(dist[v] == -1) {\n                    dist[v] = dist[u] + 1;\n                    q.add(v);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return dist;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(V + E)","space":"O(V + E)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time: Each node and edge is processed exactly once. Space: O(V + E) for the adjacency list and O(V) for the distance array and queue.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] DFS explores all paths\n- [ ] Inefficient: O(V!)"}]}', 
  '["BFS is optimal for Unit Weights (Weight = 1)","No need for Dijkstra''s Priority Queue (avoids log V overhead)","Time: O(V + E)","Space: O(V + E)"]', '[]', '[]',
  'shortest-path-undirected-unit-distance
Shortest Path in Undirected Graph with Unit Distance
Graph
BFS
You are given an undirected graph with `V` nodes and `E` edges. Each edge has a weight of `1`. Given a source node `src`, find the shortest distance from `src` to all other nodes. If a node is unreachable, the distance should be `-1`.
An adjacency list `adj`, number of nodes `V`, and a source node `src`.
An array of integers representing the shortest distance to each node from `src`.
Input: V = 5, E = 5, adj = [[1,3],[0,2],[1,4],[0,4],[3,2]], src = 0
Output: [0, 1, 2, 1, 2]
Explore every possible path using DFS and keep track of the minimum distance for each node.
1. Perform a recursive DFS starting from the source.
2. For each path, maintain a current distance.
3. Update the global distance array only if the new path is shorter than the previously recorded one.
In the worst case (a highly connected graph), DFS explores all possible permutations of paths, leading to factorial time complexity.
Why BFS? In a graph where all edges have a weight of 1, BFS is the most efficient shortest-path algorithm. Unlike Dijkstra, which uses a Priority Queue to handle varying weights, BFS uses a simple Queue to explore nodes layer by layer. The first time a node is reached in BFS, it is guaranteed to be via the shortest path.
1. Initialize a `dist` array with -1 and set `dist[src] = 0`.
2. Add the source node to a FIFO Queue.
3. While the queue is not empty:
    - Pop the front node `u`.
    - For every neighbor `v` of `u`:
        - If `dist[v] == -1` (not visited):
            - Set `dist[v] = dist[u] + 1`.
            - Add `v` to the queue.
4. Return the `dist` array.
Time: Each node and edge is processed exactly once. Space: O(V + E) for the adjacency list and O(V) for the distance array and queue.
DFS explores all paths
Inefficient: O(V!)
BFS is optimal for Unit Weights (Weight = 1)
No need for Dijkstra''s Priority Queue (avoids log V overhead)
Time: O(V + E)
Space: O(V + E)', '2026-04-28T14:04:11.316Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'binary-search', 'problem', 'Binary Search', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-search', 'medium', 'Searching', 'Decrease and Conquer',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given an array of integers `nums` which is sorted in ascending order, and an integer `target`, write a function to search `target` in `nums`. If `target` exists, then return its index. Otherwise, return `-1`.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\n- `nums`: A sorted array of integers.\n- `target`: The value to locate.\n\n### Output\nThe index of `target` (integer) or `-1` if not found.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: nums = [-1,0,3,5,9,12], target = 9\nOutput: 4","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"9 exists in nums and its index is 4.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Check every element one by one from the start of the array until the target is found.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Iterate from $i = 0$ to $n-1$.\n2. If `nums[i] == target`, return $i$.\n3. If loop finishes, return -1.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int search(int[] nums, int target) {\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n            if (nums[i] == target) return i;\n        }\n        return -1;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"In the worst case, we might have to scan the entire array.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Since the array is sorted, we can eliminate half of the search space in every step by comparing the target with the middle element.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Initialize `low = 0` and `high = n - 1`.\n2. While `low <= high`:\n   - Find `mid = low + (high - low) / 2`.\n   - If `nums[mid] == target`, return `mid`.\n   - If `nums[mid] < target`, move `low = mid + 1`.\n   - Else, move `high = mid - 1`.\n3. Return -1 if not found.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int search(int[] nums, int target) {\n        int low = 0, high = nums.length - 1;\n        while (low <= high) {\n            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n            if (nums[mid] == target) return mid;\n            else if (nums[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;\n            else high = mid - 1;\n        }\n        return -1;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(log n)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"The search space is halved in every iteration, leading to a logarithmic time complexity.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Linear scan\n- [ ] No sorting required"}]}', 
  '["Sorted array required","Divide and conquer","Avoid overflow with low + (high-low)/2"]', '[]', '[]',
  'binary-search
Binary Search
Searching
Decrease and Conquer
Given an array of integers `nums` which is sorted in ascending order, and an integer `target`, write a function to search `target` in `nums`. If `target` exists, then return its index. Otherwise, return `-1`.
- `nums`: A sorted array of integers.
- `target`: The value to locate.
The index of `target` (integer) or `-1` if not found.
Input: nums = [-1,0,3,5,9,12], target = 9
Output: 4
9 exists in nums and its index is 4.
Check every element one by one from the start of the array until the target is found.
1. Iterate from $i = 0$ to $n-1$.
2. If `nums[i] == target`, return $i$.
3. If loop finishes, return -1.
Searching for 9 in [-1,0,3,5,9,12]: Check -1... 0... 3... 5... 9 (Found at index 4).
In the worst case, we might have to scan the entire array.
Since the array is sorted, we can eliminate half of the search space in every step by comparing the target with the middle element.
1. Initialize `low = 0` and `high = n - 1`.
2. While `low <= high`:
   - Find `mid = low + (high - low) / 2`.
   - If `nums[mid] == target`, return `mid`.
   - If `nums[mid] < target`, move `low = mid + 1`.
   - Else, move `high = mid - 1`.
3. Return -1 if not found.
{"engine":"reactflow","type":"flow","nodes":[{"id":"l","type":"pointer","data":{"label":"low"},"position":{"x":0,"y":50}},{"id":"m","type":"pointer","data":{"label":"mid","highlight":"active"},"position":{"x":100,"y":50}},{"id":"h","type":"pointer","data":{"label":"high"},"position":{"x":200,"y":50}}],"steps":[{"label":"Init","description":"low=0, high=5, mid=2"},{"label":"Compare","description":"nums[2] is 3. 9 > 3, so move low to mid + 1."}]}
The search space is halved in every iteration, leading to a logarithmic time complexity.
Linear scan
No sorting required
