  'largest-color-value-in-a-directed-graph', 'problem', 'Largest Color Value in a Directed Graph', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/largest-color-value-in-a-directed-graph', 'medium', 'Graph', 'Kahn''s Algorithm + DP',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given a directed graph and a string `colors`, find the maximum color value of any directed path. The color value is the frequency of the most frequent color on that path. If the graph contains a cycle, return -1.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nString `colors` of length n and a 2D array `edges`.\n\n### Output\nThe maximum color value or -1 if a cycle exists.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: colors = ''abac'', edges = [[0,1], [1,2], [0,2], [2,3]]\nOutput: 2","visuals":[]},{"id":"example-explanation","title":"Example Explanation","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Path 0 -> 1 -> 2 has two nodes with color ''a''. The max frequency is 2.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Kahn''s Algorithm + DP\nSince the graph must be a DAG to have a finite color value, we can use Topological Sort. We use a DP table to track the maximum frequency of each of the 26 colors ending at each node.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Initialize an adjacency list and calculate indegrees.\n2. Create a 2D DP array `cnt[n][26]`.\n3. Use a queue for nodes with 0 indegree.\n4. For each node, increment the count for its color.\n5. Push current counts to neighbors using `Math.max` for all 26 colors.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"public int largestPathValue(String colors, int[][] edges) {\n    int n = colors.length();\n    List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());\n    int[] indegree = new int[n];\n    for (int[] e : edges) {\n        adj.get(e[0]).add(e[1]);\n        indegree[e[1]]++;\n    }\n    int[][] cnt = new int[n][26];\n    Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indegree[i] == 0) q.offer(i);\n    int processed = 0, ans = 0;\n    while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n        int u = q.poll();\n        processed++;\n        int color = colors.charAt(u) - ''a'';\n        cnt[u][color]++;\n        ans = Math.max(ans, cnt[u][color]);\n        for (int v : adj.get(u)) {\n            for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) cnt[v][i] = Math.max(cnt[v][i], cnt[u][i]);\n            if (--indegree[v] == 0) q.offer(v);\n        }\n    }\n    return processed == n ? ans : -1;\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(26 * (V + E))","space":"O(26 * V)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-complexity","title":"Brute Force Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"We process each node and edge once, performing 26 operations per edge for color propagation.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Memoized DFS\nSimilar to finding the longest path in a DAG, we can use DFS with memoization. For each node and each color, we find the max value starting from that node.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. For each of the 26 colors, run a DFS to find the maximum frequency of that specific color.\n2. Use a `memo` table to store results for (node, color) to avoid redundant calculations.\n3. Detect cycles using a `pathVisited` array during the DFS.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"// DFS approach usually slower due to 26 separate passes,\n// but logically consistent with DAG DP patterns.\nclass Solution {\n    // Implementation using Memoization\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(26 * (V + E))","space":"O(26 * V)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"DFS for color ''a'' starting at node 0: Visit 1, then 2. 2 is an ''a'', so returns 1. 1 is ''b'', returns 1. 0 is ''a'', returns 1 + 1 = 2."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Identical complexity to Kahn''s, as we perform 26 DFS traversals (or one combined traversal).","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Use Kahn''s for Topological Order\n- [ ] Maintain DP table [N][26]\n- [ ] Propagate state: child = max(child, parent)"}]}', 
  '["Can also be solved with memoized DFS","Cycle detection is mandatory (processed count != N)","O(26N) space complexity"]', '[]', '[]',
  'largest-color-value-in-a-directed-graph
Largest Color Value in a Directed Graph
Graph
Kahn''s Algorithm + DP
Given a directed graph and a string `colors`, find the maximum color value of any directed path. The color value is the frequency of the most frequent color on that path. If the graph contains a cycle, return -1.
String `colors` of length n and a 2D array `edges`.
The maximum color value or -1 if a cycle exists.
Input: colors = ''abac'', edges = [[0,1], [1,2], [0,2], [2,3]]
Output: 2
Path 0 -> 1 -> 2 has two nodes with color ''a''. The max frequency is 2.
### Kahn''s Algorithm + DP
Since the graph must be a DAG to have a finite color value, we can use Topological Sort. We use a DP table to track the maximum frequency of each of the 26 colors ending at each node.
1. Initialize an adjacency list and calculate indegrees.
2. Create a 2D DP array `cnt[n][26]`.
3. Use a queue for nodes with 0 indegree.
4. For each node, increment the count for its color.
5. Push current counts to neighbors using `Math.max` for all 26 colors.
Node 0 (color ''a'') is popped. cnt[0][''a''] = 1. Neighbors 1 and 2 updated: cnt[1][''a''] = 1, cnt[2][''a''] = 1. Indegree of neighbor 1 hits 0, added to queue.
We process each node and edge once, performing 26 operations per edge for color propagation.
### Memoized DFS
Similar to finding the longest path in a DAG, we can use DFS with memoization. For each node and each color, we find the max value starting from that node.
1. For each of the 26 colors, run a DFS to find the maximum frequency of that specific color.
2. Use a `memo` table to store results for (node, color) to avoid redundant calculations.
3. Detect cycles using a `pathVisited` array during the DFS.
DFS for color ''a'' starting at node 0: Visit 1, then 2. 2 is an ''a'', so returns 1. 1 is ''b'', returns 1. 0 is ''a'', returns 1 + 1 = 2.
Identical complexity to Kahn''s, as we perform 26 DFS traversals (or one combined traversal).
Use Kahn''s for Topological Order
Maintain DP table [N][26]
Propagate state: child = max(child, parent)
Can also be solved with memoized DFS
Cycle detection is mandatory (processed count != N)
O(26N) space complexity', '2026-05-04T18:13:09.009Z'
);

