  'delete-operation-for-two-strings', 'problem', 'Delete Operation for Two Strings', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-operation-for-two-strings/', 'medium', 'Dynamic Programming', 'DP on Strings',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given two strings `word1` and `word2`, return the minimum number of steps required to make `word1` and `word2` the same.\n\nIn one step, you can delete exactly one character in either string.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nTwo strings `word1` and `word2`.\n\n### Output\nAn integer representing the minimum number of deletion steps.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: word1 = \"sea\", word2 = \"eat\"\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: You need one step to make \"sea\" to \"ea\" and another step to make \"eat\" to \"ea\".","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Plain Recursion\nWe can evaluate characters from the end of both strings. If they match, they stay (0 deletions), and we move both pointers inward. If they mismatch, we are forced to delete a character. We can either delete the current character from `word1` or from `word2`. We branch into both paths, add 1 for the deletion step, and take the minimum.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Define `solve(i, j)` returning the minimum deletions for `word1[0..i]` and `word2[0..j]`.\n2. **Base Cases**: If `i < 0`, we must delete all remaining characters in `word2` (return `j + 1`). If `j < 0`, we must delete all remaining characters in `word1` (return `i + 1`).\n3. **Decisions**:\n   - Match: If `word1[i] == word2[j]`, return `solve(i-1, j-1)`.\n   - Mismatch: Return `1 + Math.min(solve(i-1, j), solve(i, j-1))`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int minDistance(String word1, String word2) {\n        return solve(word1.length() - 1, word2.length() - 1, word1, word2);\n    }\n    private int solve(int i, int j, String s1, String s2) {\n        if (i < 0) return j + 1;\n        if (j < 0) return i + 1;\n        if (s1.charAt(i) == s2.charAt(j)) {\n            return solve(i - 1, j - 1, s1, s2);\n        }\n        return 1 + Math.min(solve(i - 1, j, s1, s2), solve(i, j - 1, s1, s2));\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(2^(N+M))","space":"O(N+M)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Why is this DP?\n1. **The \"Aha!\" Moment**: To minimize deletions, we must maximize the characters we *keep*. The longest sequence of characters that both strings can keep in the exact same order is their **Longest Common Subsequence (LCS)**.\n2. **The Formula**: Every character that is *not* part of the LCS must be deleted. Therefore, the total minimum deletions is exactly `word1.length() + word2.length() - 2 * LCS`.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"We completely bypass writing a deletion tracker and reuse our optimized LCS algorithm:\n1. **Tabulation (Space-Optimized Bottom-Up)**: We calculate the LCS using two 1D arrays (`prev` and `curr`), flattening the 2D grid logic to extract the LCS length safely in O(M) space.\n2. **Memoization (Top-Down)**: Caches the LCS `(i, j)` boundaries. We manually initialize our memoization array to `-1` using nested loops to guarantee zero external import dependencies on the platform.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int minDistance(String word1, String word2) {\n        int n = word1.length();\n        int m = word2.length();\n        int[] prev = new int[m + 1];\n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n            int[] curr = new int[m + 1];\n            for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {\n                if (word1.charAt(i - 1) == word2.charAt(j - 1)) {\n                    curr[j] = 1 + prev[j - 1];\n                } else {\n                    curr[j] = Math.max(prev[j], curr[j - 1]);\n                }\n            }\n            prev = curr;\n        }\n        return n + m - 2 * prev[m];\n    }\n    public int minDistanceMemo(String word1, String word2) {\n        int n = word1.length();\n        int m = word2.length();\n        int[][] memo = new int[n][m];\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {\n                memo[i][j] = -1;\n            }\n        }\n        int lcs = memoSolve(n - 1, m - 1, word1, word2, memo);\n        return n + m - 2 * lcs;\n    }\n    private int memoSolve(int i, int j, String s1, String s2, int[][] memo) {\n        if (i < 0 || j < 0) return 0;\n        if (memo[i][j] != -1) return memo[i][j];\n        if (s1.charAt(i) == s2.charAt(j)) {\n            return memo[i][j] = 1 + memoSolve(i - 1, j - 1, s1, s2, memo);\n        }\n        return memo[i][j] = Math.max(memoSolve(i - 1, j, s1, s2, memo), memoSolve(i, j - 1, s1, s2, memo));\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N * M)","space":"O(M)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"word1 = \"sea\", word2 = \"eat\". The Longest Common Subsequence is \"ea\" (length 2). word1 length is 3, word2 length is 3. Total deletions = 3 + 3 - (2 * 2) = 6 - 4 = 2."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time Complexity: O(N * M) since extracting the Longest Common Subsequence requires checking every character pair between the strings once. Space Complexity: Tabulation reduces the required storage from a full 2D grid down to O(M) by maintaining only the previous and current row arrays.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Recursive branches split when characters mismatch, simulating a deletion from either string.\n- [ ] When one string is exhausted, the base case returns the length of the remaining characters in the other string."}]}', 
  '["Genius Trick: Convert the problem mathematically. Total Deletions = Length1 + Length2 - (2 * LCS).","Zero-Dependency Initialization: Uses basic nested loops to fill the memo matrix with -1 to prevent platform import crashes.","Space Optimization: Uses two 1D arrays to evaluate the LCS in-place securely."]', '[]', '[]',
  'delete-operation-for-two-strings
Delete Operation for Two Strings
Dynamic Programming
DP on Strings
Given two strings `word1` and `word2`, return the minimum number of steps required to make `word1` and `word2` the same.

In one step, you can delete exactly one character in either string.
Two strings `word1` and `word2`.
An integer representing the minimum number of deletion steps.
Input: word1 = "sea", word2 = "eat"
Output: 2
Explanation: You need one step to make "sea" to "ea" and another step to make "eat" to "ea".
### Plain Recursion
We can evaluate characters from the end of both strings. If they match, they stay (0 deletions), and we move both pointers inward. If they mismatch, we are forced to delete a character. We can either delete the current character from `word1` or from `word2`. We branch into both paths, add 1 for the deletion step, and take the minimum.
1. Define `solve(i, j)` returning the minimum deletions for `word1[0..i]` and `word2[0..j]`.
2. **Base Cases**: If `i < 0`, we must delete all remaining characters in `word2` (return `j + 1`). If `j < 0`, we must delete all remaining characters in `word1` (return `i + 1`).
3. **Decisions**:
   - Match: If `word1[i] == word2[j]`, return `solve(i-1, j-1)`.
   - Mismatch: Return `1 + Math.min(solve(i-1, j), solve(i, j-1))`.
### Why is this DP?
1. **The "Aha!" Moment**: To minimize deletions, we must maximize the characters we *keep*. The longest sequence of characters that both strings can keep in the exact same order is their **Longest Common Subsequence (LCS)**.
2. **The Formula**: Every character that is *not* part of the LCS must be deleted. Therefore, the total minimum deletions is exactly `word1.length() + word2.length() - 2 * LCS`.
We completely bypass writing a deletion tracker and reuse our optimized LCS algorithm:
1. **Tabulation (Space-Optimized Bottom-Up)**: We calculate the LCS using two 1D arrays (`prev` and `curr`), flattening the 2D grid logic to extract the LCS length safely in O(M) space.
2. **Memoization (Top-Down)**: Caches the LCS `(i, j)` boundaries. We manually initialize our memoization array to `-1` using nested loops to guarantee zero external import dependencies on the platform.
word1 = "sea", word2 = "eat". The Longest Common Subsequence is "ea" (length 2). word1 length is 3, word2 length is 3. Total deletions = 3 + 3 - (2 * 2) = 6 - 4 = 2.
Time Complexity: O(N * M) since extracting the Longest Common Subsequence requires checking every character pair between the strings once. Space Complexity: Tabulation reduces the required storage from a full 2D grid down to O(M) by maintaining only the previous and current row arrays.
Recursive branches split when characters mismatch, simulating a deletion from either string.
When one string is exhausted, the base case returns the length of the remaining characters in the other string.
Genius Trick: Convert the problem mathematically. Total Deletions = Length1 + Length2 - (2 * LCS).
Zero-Dependency Initialization: Uses basic nested loops to fill the memo matrix with -1 to prevent platform import crashes.
Space Optimization: Uses two 1D arrays to evaluate the LCS in-place securely.', '2026-05-22T17:42:11.353Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
