  'edit-distance', 'problem', 'Edit Distance', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/edit-distance/', 'medium', 'Dynamic Programming', 'DP on Strings',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given two strings `word1` and `word2`, return the minimum number of operations required to convert `word1` to `word2`.\n\nYou have the following three operations permitted on a word:\n1. **Insert** a character\n2. **Delete** a character\n3. **Replace** a character","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nTwo strings `word1` and `word2`.\n\n### Output\nAn integer representing the minimum number of edit operations.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: word1 = \"horse\", word2 = \"ros\"\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: \nhorse -> rorse (replace ''h'' with ''r'')\nrorse -> rose (delete ''r'')\nrose -> ros (delete ''e'')","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Plain Recursion\nWe compare the strings starting from their ends. If the characters match, no operation is needed, so we simply shrink both strings and continue. If they mismatch, we don''t know which operation is best, so we try all three (Insert, Delete, Replace) recursively, add a cost of 1 to each, and pick the one that yields the minimum total operations.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Define `solve(i, j)` returning the min edits to convert `word1[0..i]` to `word2[0..j]`.\n2. **Base Cases**: \n   - If `i < 0` (word1 is empty), we must **insert** all remaining characters of word2. Return `j + 1`.\n   - If `j < 0` (word2 is empty), we must **delete** all remaining characters of word1. Return `i + 1`.\n3. **Decisions**:\n   - Match: If `word1[i] == word2[j]`, return `solve(i - 1, j - 1)`.\n   - Mismatch: Return `1 + Math.min(Insert, Math.min(Delete, Replace))`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int minDistance(String word1, String word2) {\n        return solve(word1.length() - 1, word2.length() - 1, word1, word2);\n    }\n    private int solve(int i, int j, String s1, String s2) {\n        if (i < 0) return j + 1;\n        if (j < 0) return i + 1;\n        if (s1.charAt(i) == s2.charAt(j)) {\n            return solve(i - 1, j - 1, s1, s2);\n        }\n        int insert = solve(i, j - 1, s1, s2);\n        int delete = solve(i - 1, j, s1, s2);\n        int replace = solve(i - 1, j - 1, s1, s2);\n        return 1 + Math.min(insert, Math.min(delete, replace));\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(3^(N + M))","space":"O(N + M)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Why is this DP?\n1. **Overlapping Subproblems**: By trying all three operations endlessly, the recursive tree generates duplicate suffix pairs `(i, j)`. Caching these evaluations turns an exponential explosion into a strict polynomial grid.\n2. **1D Array Trick**: When converting this logic to Tabulation, calculating the current state only requires looking at the current row (for Insert), the previous row directly above (for Delete), and the previous row''s diagonal (for Match/Replace). We can squash the 2D grid down to two 1D arrays (`prev` and `curr`).","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"We provide two native DP implementations bundled together:\n1. **Tabulation (Space-Optimized Bottom-Up)**: We shift indices by 1 so `0` represents an empty string. The base cases become the 0-th row and 0-th column initializations. `prev` starts as `[0, 1, 2... m]` (simulating inserting characters to match word2).\n2. **Memoization (Top-Down)**: Caches the `(i, j)` recursive coordinate results. Initialized manually to `-1` using nested loops to guarantee zero external imports.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int minDistance(String word1, String word2) {\n        int n = word1.length();\n        int m = word2.length();\n        int[] prev = new int[m + 1];\n        \n        for (int j = 0; j <= m; j++) {\n            prev[j] = j;\n        }\n        \n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n            int[] curr = new int[m + 1];\n            curr[0] = i;\n            for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {\n                if (word1.charAt(i - 1) == word2.charAt(j - 1)) {\n                    curr[j] = prev[j - 1];\n                } else {\n                    int insert = curr[j - 1];\n                    int delete = prev[j];\n                    int replace = prev[j - 1];\n                    curr[j] = 1 + Math.min(insert, Math.min(delete, replace));\n                }\n            }\n            prev = curr;\n        }\n        return prev[m];\n    }\n    \n    public int minDistanceMemo(String word1, String word2) {\n        int n = word1.length();\n        int m = word2.length();\n        int[][] memo = new int[n][m];\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {\n                memo[i][j] = -1;\n            }\n        }\n        return memoSolve(n - 1, m - 1, word1, word2, memo);\n    }\n    \n    private int memoSolve(int i, int j, String s1, String s2, int[][] memo) {\n        if (i < 0) return j + 1;\n        if (j < 0) return i + 1;\n        if (memo[i][j] != -1) return memo[i][j];\n        \n        if (s1.charAt(i) == s2.charAt(j)) {\n            return memo[i][j] = memoSolve(i - 1, j - 1, s1, s2, memo);\n        }\n        \n        int insert = memoSolve(i, j - 1, s1, s2, memo);\n        int delete = memoSolve(i - 1, j, s1, s2, memo);\n        int replace = memoSolve(i - 1, j - 1, s1, s2, memo);\n        \n        return memo[i][j] = 1 + Math.min(insert, Math.min(delete, replace));\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N * M)","space":"O(M)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"word1 = \"horse\", word2 = \"ros\". dp[i][j] represents edits. For ''e'' and ''s'' mismatch, we compute 1 + min(delete ''e'', insert ''s'', replace ''e''->''s''). The 1D arrays compute this strictly left-to-right, passing the optimal previous decisions forward until dp[n][m] yields 3."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time Complexity: O(N * M) because every cell combination between the two strings is evaluated exactly once. Space Complexity: Reduced completely to O(M) in the Tabulation algorithm since we discard older historical states and only retain the current and previous tracking rows.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Insert: Moves the ''j'' pointer backward because we artificially added a matched character to word1. (i, j - 1)\n- [ ] Delete: Moves the ''i'' pointer backward because we threw away a character from word1. (i - 1, j)\n- [ ] Replace: Moves both pointers backward as both characters are effectively dealt with. (i - 1, j - 1)"}]}', 
  '["Shift indices dynamically: By utilizing 1-based index mapping, we avoid throwing out-of-bounds exceptions for our base cases.","The first column/row base cases aren''t 0. They are `i` and `j`! If one string is empty, we must perform that exact number of deletions or insertions.","Space Optimization uses two 1D arrays: `prev` representing operations from the previous step, and `curr` acting as the active calculation tier."]', '[]', '[]',
  'edit-distance
Edit Distance
Dynamic Programming
DP on Strings
Given two strings `word1` and `word2`, return the minimum number of operations required to convert `word1` to `word2`.

You have the following three operations permitted on a word:
1. **Insert** a character
2. **Delete** a character
3. **Replace** a character
Two strings `word1` and `word2`.
An integer representing the minimum number of edit operations.
Input: word1 = "horse", word2 = "ros"
Output: 3
Explanation: 
horse -> rorse (replace ''h'' with ''r'')
rorse -> rose (delete ''r'')
rose -> ros (delete ''e'')
### Plain Recursion
We compare the strings starting from their ends. If the characters match, no operation is needed, so we simply shrink both strings and continue. If they mismatch, we don''t know which operation is best, so we try all three (Insert, Delete, Replace) recursively, add a cost of 1 to each, and pick the one that yields the minimum total operations.
1. Define `solve(i, j)` returning the min edits to convert `word1[0..i]` to `word2[0..j]`.
2. **Base Cases**: 
   - If `i < 0` (word1 is empty), we must **insert** all remaining characters of word2. Return `j + 1`.
   - If `j < 0` (word2 is empty), we must **delete** all remaining characters of word1. Return `i + 1`.
3. **Decisions**:
   - Match: If `word1[i] == word2[j]`, return `solve(i - 1, j - 1)`.
   - Mismatch: Return `1 + Math.min(Insert, Math.min(Delete, Replace))`.
### Why is this DP?
1. **Overlapping Subproblems**: By trying all three operations endlessly, the recursive tree generates duplicate suffix pairs `(i, j)`. Caching these evaluations turns an exponential explosion into a strict polynomial grid.
2. **1D Array Trick**: When converting this logic to Tabulation, calculating the current state only requires looking at the current row (for Insert), the previous row directly above (for Delete), and the previous row''s diagonal (for Match/Replace). We can squash the 2D grid down to two 1D arrays (`prev` and `curr`).
We provide two native DP implementations bundled together:
1. **Tabulation (Space-Optimized Bottom-Up)**: We shift indices by 1 so `0` represents an empty string. The base cases become the 0-th row and 0-th column initializations. `prev` starts as `[0, 1, 2... m]` (simulating inserting characters to match word2).
2. **Memoization (Top-Down)**: Caches the `(i, j)` recursive coordinate results. Initialized manually to `-1` using nested loops to guarantee zero external imports.
word1 = "horse", word2 = "ros". dp[i][j] represents edits. For ''e'' and ''s'' mismatch, we compute 1 + min(delete ''e'', insert ''s'', replace ''e''->''s''). The 1D arrays compute this strictly left-to-right, passing the optimal previous decisions forward until dp[n][m] yields 3.
Time Complexity: O(N * M) because every cell combination between the two strings is evaluated exactly once. Space Complexity: Reduced completely to O(M) in the Tabulation algorithm since we discard older historical states and only retain the current and previous tracking rows.
Insert: Moves the ''j'' pointer backward because we artificially added a matched character to word1. (i, j - 1)
Delete: Moves the ''i'' pointer backward because we threw away a character from word1. (i - 1, j)
Replace: Moves both pointers backward as both characters are effectively dealt with. (i - 1, j - 1)
Shift indices dynamically: By utilizing 1-based index mapping, we avoid throwing out-of-bounds exceptions for our base cases.
The first column/row base cases aren''t 0. They are `i` and `j`! If one string is empty, we must perform that exact number of deletions or insertions.
Space Optimization uses two 1D arrays: `prev` representing operations from the previous step, and `curr` acting as the active calculation tier.', '2026-05-23T18:56:20.787Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-ii', 'problem', 'Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock II', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-ii/', 'medium', 'Dynamic Programming', 'State Machine DP / Greedy',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"You are given an integer array `prices` where `prices[i]` is the price of a given stock on the `i-th` day.\n\nOn each day, you may decide to buy and/or sell the stock. You can only hold at most one share of the stock at any time. However, you can buy it then immediately sell it on the **same day**.\n\nFind and return the maximum profit you can achieve.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn integer array `prices`.\n\n### Output\nAn integer representing the maximum profit achievable by completing multiple transactions.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: prices = [7,1,5,3,6,4]\nOutput: 7\nExplanation: Buy on day 2 (price = 1) and sell on day 3 (price = 5), profit = 5-1 = 4.\nThen buy on day 4 (price = 3) and sell on day 5 (price = 6), profit = 6-3 = 3.\nTotal profit is 4 + 3 = 7.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Custom Tracking (Greedy Base)\nThis approach operates on highly efficient logic by explicitly tracking the local minimums (`mini`). Whenever the stock price goes up, we immediately capture the local `cost` profit, and then reset the `mini` tracker to `Integer.MAX_VALUE` to effectively \"close\" the transaction and force the algorithm to search for the next buying dip.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Initialize `profit` to `0` and `mini` to the first day''s price.\n2. Loop through the array starting from day 1.\n3. Calculate the potential profit (`cost`) as `prices[i] - mini`.\n4. If `cost > 0`, add it to `profit` and safely reset `mini` to `Integer.MAX_VALUE` so it picks up a new buying price on subsequent iterations.\n5. Continuously update `mini` using `Math.min(mini, prices[i])` to always hold the lowest possible legal buy price for the next peak.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int maxProfit(int[] prices) {\n        int n = prices.length;\n        int profit = 0;\n        int mini = prices[0];\n        int cost = 0;\n\n        for(int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n            cost = prices[i] - mini;\n            if(cost > 0) {\n                profit += cost;\n                mini = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n            }\n            mini = Math.min(mini, prices[i]);\n        }\n\n        return profit;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Simplified Greedy & State Machine DP\nWhile the custom tracking approach is already optimal in $O(N)$ time, we can simplify the Greedy math slightly: if `prices[i] > prices[i-1]`, just add the difference! More importantly, we must introduce **State Machine DP** here. While Greedy is faster for this specific problem, the DP state-tracking framework is strictly required to solve harder variations like *Stock III (max 2 transactions)* or *Stock with Cooldown*.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"We provide both implementations bundled securely together:\n1. **Simplified Greedy (Ultra-Fast)**: Iterate starting from day 1. If `prices[i] > prices[i-1]`, simply add `prices[i] - prices[i-1]` to the total profit.\n2. **Space-Optimized DP**: Evaluate days backward. Use two variables `aheadBuy` and `aheadSell` initialized to 0. Update the current legal choices tracking `currBuy` and `currSell`, passing them backward until day 0 yields the maximum `aheadBuy` profit.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int maxProfit(int[] prices) {\n        int profit = 0;\n        for (int i = 1; i < prices.length; i++) {\n            if (prices[i] > prices[i - 1]) {\n                profit += prices[i] - prices[i - 1];\n            }\n        }\n        return profit;\n    }\n    public int maxProfitDP(int[] prices) {\n        int n = prices.length;\n        int aheadBuy = 0;\n        int aheadSell = 0;\n        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n            int currBuy = Math.max(aheadBuy, -prices[i] + aheadSell);\n            int currSell = Math.max(aheadSell, prices[i] + aheadBuy);\n            aheadBuy = currBuy;\n            aheadSell = currSell;\n        }\n        return aheadBuy;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(1)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"Greedy Dry Run: prices = [1, 5, 3, 6]. Day 1: 5 > 1. Profit += 4. Day 2: 3 < 5. Ignore. Day 3: 6 > 3. Profit += 3. Total profit = 7. We captured every upward climb effortlessly."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time Complexity: O(N) because both the Greedy and DP approaches evaluate the `prices` array exactly once. Space Complexity: Reduced perfectly to O(1) because we only use a few primitive integer tracking variables, abandoning recursive stacks and matrices completely.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Tracks local minimums manually.\n- [ ] Captures upward delta and resets the minimum to Infinity to force a new purchase window."}]}', 
  '["Greedy Mathematical Truth: Maximizing sum(peaks - valleys) is identical to capturing the delta of every single sequential upward step.","DP State tracking completely drops recursion arrays, maintaining only the two explicit states from the ''ahead'' day.","Both solutions operate flawlessly in constant O(1) auxiliary space."]', '[]', '[]',
  'best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-ii
Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock II
Dynamic Programming
State Machine DP / Greedy
You are given an integer array `prices` where `prices[i]` is the price of a given stock on the `i-th` day.

On each day, you may decide to buy and/or sell the stock. You can only hold at most one share of the stock at any time. However, you can buy it then immediately sell it on the **same day**.

Find and return the maximum profit you can achieve.
An integer array `prices`.
An integer representing the maximum profit achievable by completing multiple transactions.
Input: prices = [7,1,5,3,6,4]
Output: 7
Explanation: Buy on day 2 (price = 1) and sell on day 3 (price = 5), profit = 5-1 = 4.
Then buy on day 4 (price = 3) and sell on day 5 (price = 6), profit = 6-3 = 3.
Total profit is 4 + 3 = 7.
### Custom Tracking (Greedy Base)
This approach operates on highly efficient logic by explicitly tracking the local minimums (`mini`). Whenever the stock price goes up, we immediately capture the local `cost` profit, and then reset the `mini` tracker to `Integer.MAX_VALUE` to effectively "close" the transaction and force the algorithm to search for the next buying dip.
1. Initialize `profit` to `0` and `mini` to the first day''s price.
2. Loop through the array starting from day 1.
3. Calculate the potential profit (`cost`) as `prices[i] - mini`.
4. If `cost > 0`, add it to `profit` and safely reset `mini` to `Integer.MAX_VALUE` so it picks up a new buying price on subsequent iterations.
5. Continuously update `mini` using `Math.min(mini, prices[i])` to always hold the lowest possible legal buy price for the next peak.
### Simplified Greedy & State Machine DP
While the custom tracking approach is already optimal in $O(N)$ time, we can simplify the Greedy math slightly: if `prices[i] > prices[i-1]`, just add the difference! More importantly, we must introduce **State Machine DP** here. While Greedy is faster for this specific problem, the DP state-tracking framework is strictly required to solve harder variations like *Stock III (max 2 transactions)* or *Stock with Cooldown*.
We provide both implementations bundled securely together:
1. **Simplified Greedy (Ultra-Fast)**: Iterate starting from day 1. If `prices[i] > prices[i-1]`, simply add `prices[i] - prices[i-1]` to the total profit.
2. **Space-Optimized DP**: Evaluate days backward. Use two variables `aheadBuy` and `aheadSell` initialized to 0. Update the current legal choices tracking `currBuy` and `currSell`, passing them backward until day 0 yields the maximum `aheadBuy` profit.
Greedy Dry Run: prices = [1, 5, 3, 6]. Day 1: 5 > 1. Profit += 4. Day 2: 3 < 5. Ignore. Day 3: 6 > 3. Profit += 3. Total profit = 7. We captured every upward climb effortlessly.
Time Complexity: O(N) because both the Greedy and DP approaches evaluate the `prices` array exactly once. Space Complexity: Reduced perfectly to O(1) because we only use a few primitive integer tracking variables, abandoning recursive stacks and matrices completely.
Tracks local minimums manually.
Captures upward delta and resets the minimum to Infinity to force a new purchase window.
Greedy Mathematical Truth: Maximizing sum(peaks - valleys) is identical to capturing the delta of every single sequential upward step.
DP State tracking completely drops recursion arrays, maintaining only the two explicit states from the ''ahead'' day.
Both solutions operate flawlessly in constant O(1) auxiliary space.', '2026-05-24T16:41:21.596Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-iv', 'problem', 'Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock IV', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-iv/', 'medium', 'Dynamic Programming', 'State Machine DP (3D State)',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"You are given an integer array `prices` where `prices[i]` is the price of a given stock on the `i-th` day, and an integer `k`.\n\nFind the maximum profit you can achieve. You may complete at most `k` transactions: i.e. you may buy at most `k` times and sell at most `k` times.\n\nNote: You may not engage in multiple transactions simultaneously (i.e., you must sell the stock before you buy again).","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn integer `k` and an integer array `prices`.\n\n### Output\nAn integer representing the maximum profit achievable with at most `k` transactions.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: k = 2, prices = [3,2,6,5,0,3]\nOutput: 7\nExplanation: Buy on day 2 (price = 2) and sell on day 3 (price = 6), profit = 6-2 = 4. Then buy on day 5 (price = 0) and sell on day 6 (price = 3), profit = 3-0 = 3. Total profit is 4 + 3 = 7.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Plain Recursion (3D State Tracking)\nThis is exactly the same State Machine logic as Stock II, but with one critical new constraint: a transaction limit `k`. To enforce this limit, we introduce a third tracking variable (`cap`). A full transaction consists of a Buy and a Sell. We choose to decrease our `cap` by 1 exactly when we execute a *Sell* operation. If our `cap` hits 0, we can no longer make any trades.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Initialize `solve(ind, buy, cap)` starting at day `0` with `buy = 1` and `cap = k`.\n2. **Base Cases**: If `ind == prices.length` (market closed) OR `cap == 0` (out of transactions), return `0`.\n3. **Decisions**:\n   - If `buy == 1`: Maximize between Buying (switches state to 0, `cap` stays same) and Skipping.\n   - If `buy == 0`: Maximize between Selling (switches state to 1, **decreases `cap` by 1**) and Skipping.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int maxProfit(int k, int[] prices) {\n        return solve(0, 1, k, prices);\n    }\n    private int solve(int ind, int buy, int cap, int[] prices) {\n        if (ind == prices.length || cap == 0) return 0;\n        \n        if (buy == 1) {\n            int take = -prices[ind] + solve(ind + 1, 0, cap, prices);\n            int skip = solve(ind + 1, 1, cap, prices);\n            return Math.max(take, skip);\n        } else {\n            int sell = prices[ind] + solve(ind + 1, 1, cap - 1, prices);\n            int skip = solve(ind + 1, 0, cap, prices);\n            return Math.max(sell, skip);\n        }\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(2^N)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Why is this DP?\n1. **Overlapping Subproblems**: The recursive tree evaluates identical states of `(Day, CanBuy, Cap)` repeatedly. Caching this 3D coordinate stops the exponential branching.\n2. **State Flattening**: Evaluating the current day only ever requires the results from the day immediately *ahead* of it (`ind + 1`). This means we can drop the `N` dimension completely! Instead of a massive 3D matrix `[N][2][k+1]`, we only need two 2D grids `[2][k+1]` representing the `ahead` day and the `curr` day.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"We provide both implementations bundled securely together:\n1. **Tabulation (Space-Optimized 2D Grid)**: We evaluate backward from the last day. We track states in two small 2D arrays: `ahead` and `curr`. Both are size `2` by `k + 1`. We iterate through days, buy states, and capacities, updating `curr` using `ahead`, and then overwriting `ahead` for the next backward step.\n2. **Memoization (Top-Down 3D Matrix)**: Caches the `(ind, buy, cap)` recursive boundaries. We initialize the 3D matrix to `-1` natively using triple nested loops to prevent any platform import crashes.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int maxProfit(int k, int[] prices) {\n        int n = prices.length;\n        if (n == 0) return 0;\n        \n        int[][] ahead = new int[2][k + 1];\n        int[][] curr = new int[2][k + 1];\n        \n        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n            for (int buy = 0; buy <= 1; buy++) {\n                for (int cap = 1; cap <= k; cap++) {\n                    if (buy == 1) {\n                        curr[buy][cap] = Math.max(-prices[i] + ahead[0][cap], ahead[1][cap]);\n                    } else {\n                        curr[buy][cap] = Math.max(prices[i] + ahead[1][cap - 1], ahead[0][cap]);\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n            for (int b = 0; b <= 1; b++) {\n                for (int c = 0; c <= k; c++) {\n                    ahead[b][c] = curr[b][c];\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return ahead[1][k];\n    }\n\n    public int maxProfitMemo(int k, int[] prices) {\n        int n = prices.length;\n        int[][][] memo = new int[n][2][k + 1];\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            for (int j = 0; j < 2; j++) {\n                for (int c = 0; c <= k; c++) {\n                    memo[i][j][c] = -1;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return memoSolve(0, 1, k, prices, memo);\n    }\n\n    private int memoSolve(int ind, int buy, int cap, int[] prices, int[][][] memo) {\n        if (ind == prices.length || cap == 0) return 0;\n        if (memo[ind][buy][cap] != -1) return memo[ind][buy][cap];\n        \n        if (buy == 1) {\n            return memo[ind][buy][cap] = Math.max(-prices[ind] + memoSolve(ind + 1, 0, cap, prices, memo), \n                                                  memoSolve(ind + 1, 1, cap, prices, memo));\n        } else {\n            return memo[ind][buy][cap] = Math.max(prices[ind] + memoSolve(ind + 1, 1, cap - 1, prices, memo), \n                                                  memoSolve(ind + 1, 0, cap, prices, memo));\n        }\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N * k)","space":"O(k)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"k = 1, prices = [3, 2, 6]. DP runs backward. Day 3 (6): If holding stock (buy=0), sell profit = 6, cap drops to 0. Day 2 (2): If not holding (buy=1), buy profit = -2 + ahead sell profit (6) = 4. Day 1 (3): buy profit = -3 + 6 = 3. Max is strictly tracked. Result is 4."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time Complexity: O(N * k) because we evaluate each day across `2` buy states and `k` capacity limits. Space Complexity: The Tabulation approach radically reduces memory overhead from O(N * k) down to strictly O(k) by overriding historical day arrays and keeping only the immediate `ahead` grid.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Adds a 3rd dimension ''cap'' to the Stock II logic to track remaining transactions.\n- [ ] Crucial Rule: Decrease the capacity ONLY when a full cycle is complete (either on Buy or on Sell, but be consistent!)."}]}', 
  '["Zero-Dependency Initialization: Uses triple nested loops to fill the 3D memo matrix with -1 to prevent platform import crashes.","Space Optimization cuts out the ''Day'' dimension natively, tracking only a 2x(K+1) grid mapping the current and previous calculation steps.","Avoids out of bounds by explicitly starting the Tabulation capacity loop at cap = 1."]', '[]', '[]',
  'best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-iv
Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock IV
Dynamic Programming
State Machine DP (3D State)
You are given an integer array `prices` where `prices[i]` is the price of a given stock on the `i-th` day, and an integer `k`.

Find the maximum profit you can achieve. You may complete at most `k` transactions: i.e. you may buy at most `k` times and sell at most `k` times.

Note: You may not engage in multiple transactions simultaneously (i.e., you must sell the stock before you buy again).
An integer `k` and an integer array `prices`.
An integer representing the maximum profit achievable with at most `k` transactions.
Input: k = 2, prices = [3,2,6,5,0,3]
Output: 7
Explanation: Buy on day 2 (price = 2) and sell on day 3 (price = 6), profit = 6-2 = 4. Then buy on day 5 (price = 0) and sell on day 6 (price = 3), profit = 3-0 = 3. Total profit is 4 + 3 = 7.
### Plain Recursion (3D State Tracking)
This is exactly the same State Machine logic as Stock II, but with one critical new constraint: a transaction limit `k`. To enforce this limit, we introduce a third tracking variable (`cap`). A full transaction consists of a Buy and a Sell. We choose to decrease our `cap` by 1 exactly when we execute a *Sell* operation. If our `cap` hits 0, we can no longer make any trades.
1. Initialize `solve(ind, buy, cap)` starting at day `0` with `buy = 1` and `cap = k`.
2. **Base Cases**: If `ind == prices.length` (market closed) OR `cap == 0` (out of transactions), return `0`.
3. **Decisions**:
   - If `buy == 1`: Maximize between Buying (switches state to 0, `cap` stays same) and Skipping.
   - If `buy == 0`: Maximize between Selling (switches state to 1, **decreases `cap` by 1**) and Skipping.
### Why is this DP?
1. **Overlapping Subproblems**: The recursive tree evaluates identical states of `(Day, CanBuy, Cap)` repeatedly. Caching this 3D coordinate stops the exponential branching.
2. **State Flattening**: Evaluating the current day only ever requires the results from the day immediately *ahead* of it (`ind + 1`). This means we can drop the `N` dimension completely! Instead of a massive 3D matrix `[N][2][k+1]`, we only need two 2D grids `[2][k+1]` representing the `ahead` day and the `curr` day.
We provide both implementations bundled securely together:
1. **Tabulation (Space-Optimized 2D Grid)**: We evaluate backward from the last day. We track states in two small 2D arrays: `ahead` and `curr`. Both are size `2` by `k + 1`. We iterate through days, buy states, and capacities, updating `curr` using `ahead`, and then overwriting `ahead` for the next backward step.
2. **Memoization (Top-Down 3D Matrix)**: Caches the `(ind, buy, cap)` recursive boundaries. We initialize the 3D matrix to `-1` natively using triple nested loops to prevent any platform import crashes.
k = 1, prices = [3, 2, 6]. DP runs backward. Day 3 (6): If holding stock (buy=0), sell profit = 6, cap drops to 0. Day 2 (2): If not holding (buy=1), buy profit = -2 + ahead sell profit (6) = 4. Day 1 (3): buy profit = -3 + 6 = 3. Max is strictly tracked. Result is 4.
Time Complexity: O(N * k) because we evaluate each day across `2` buy states and `k` capacity limits. Space Complexity: The Tabulation approach radically reduces memory overhead from O(N * k) down to strictly O(k) by overriding historical day arrays and keeping only the immediate `ahead` grid.
Adds a 3rd dimension ''cap'' to the Stock II logic to track remaining transactions.
Crucial Rule: Decrease the capacity ONLY when a full cycle is complete (either on Buy or on Sell, but be consistent!).
Zero-Dependency Initialization: Uses triple nested loops to fill the 3D memo matrix with -1 to prevent platform import crashes.
Space Optimization cuts out the ''Day'' dimension natively, tracking only a 2x(K+1) grid mapping the current and previous calculation steps.
Avoids out of bounds by explicitly starting the Tabulation capacity loop at cap = 1.', '2026-05-25T17:35:28.467Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-with-cooldown', 'problem', 'Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock with Cooldown', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-with-cooldown/', 'medium', 'Dynamic Programming', 'State Machine DP',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"You are given an array `prices` where `prices[i]` is the price of a given stock on the `i-th` day.\n\nFind the maximum profit you can achieve. You may complete as many transactions as you like (i.e., buy one and sell one share of the stock multiple times) with the following restrictions:\n* After you sell your stock, you cannot buy stock on the next day (i.e., **cooldown one day**).\n\nNote: You may not engage in multiple transactions simultaneously (i.e., you must sell the stock before you buy again).","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn integer array `prices` representing daily stock prices.\n\n### Output\nAn integer representing the maximum profit achievable.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: prices = [1,2,3,0,2]\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: Transactions = [buy, sell, cooldown, buy, sell]. \nBuy on day 1 (price=1), sell on day 2 (price=2) -> Profit: 1. \nDay 3 is cooldown.\nBuy on day 4 (price=0), sell on day 5 (price=2) -> Profit: 2. \nTotal Profit: 3.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Plain Recursion (State Tracking with Jump)\nThis operates almost identically to the unlimited transaction model (Stock II), but we introduce a strict penalty for selling. If we decide to sell on `Day i`, the rules state we cannot do anything on `Day i+1`. Therefore, when we execute a Sell branch, our recursive pointer must immediately jump to `Day i+2`.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Initialize `solve(ind, buy)` starting at day `0` with `buy = 1`.\n2. **Base Cases**: Since selling can push our index past the end of the array via a `+2` jump, our base condition must be `if (ind >= prices.length)`, return `0`.\n3. **Decisions**:\n   - If `buy == 1`: Maximize between Buying (switches state to 0, moves to `ind + 1`) and Skipping (moves to `ind + 1`).\n   - If `buy == 0`: Maximize between Selling (switches state to 1, **moves to `ind + 2`**) and Skipping (moves to `ind + 1`).","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int maxProfit(int[] prices) {\n        return solve(0, 1, prices);\n    }\n    private int solve(int ind, int buy, int[] prices) {\n        if (ind >= prices.length) return 0;\n        \n        if (buy == 1) {\n            int take = -prices[ind] + solve(ind + 1, 0, prices);\n            int skip = solve(ind + 1, 1, prices);\n            return Math.max(take, skip);\n        } else {\n            int sell = prices[ind] + solve(ind + 2, 1, prices); // Cooldown Jump!\n            int skip = solve(ind + 1, 0, prices);\n            return Math.max(sell, skip);\n        }\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(2^N)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Why is this DP?\n1. **Overlapping Subproblems**: The recursive branches (especially the skips and cooldown jumps) will evaluate the exact same `(Day, CanBuy)` combinations repeatedly. Caching them prevents exponential pathing.\n2. **Array Sizing Trick (N+2)**: When we transition from Memoization to Tabulation, we process the days backward. Because the `Sell` state looks ahead to `Day i+2`, we will trigger Out-Of-Bounds exceptions on the final two days if we use a standard `N` sized table. By allocating our DP matrix with size `N + 2`, we simulate an infinitely closed market past the final day, automatically handling all base case bounds effortlessly.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"We provide both efficient implementations bundled securely together:\n1. **Tabulation (Bottom-Up N+2 Grid)**: Initialize a DP table of size `[N + 2][2]` filled natively with 0s. Loop backward from `N - 1` down to `0`. If `buy == 1`, we reference `dp[i+1]`. If `buy == 0` (selling), we reference `dp[i+2]` directly. Return `dp[0][1]`.\n2. **Memoization (Top-Down)**: Caches the `(ind, buy)` outcomes. We initialize the matrix to `-1` natively using double loops to guarantee zero external platform dependencies.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int maxProfit(int[] prices) {\n        int n = prices.length;\n        // Create N+2 table to safely handle the ind+2 cooldown jump\n        int[][] dp = new int[n + 2][2];\n        \n        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n            // Case: We are allowed to buy\n            dp[i][1] = Math.max(-prices[i] + dp[i + 1][0], dp[i + 1][1]);\n            \n            // Case: We are allowed to sell (triggers cooldown jump)\n            dp[i][0] = Math.max(prices[i] + dp[i + 2][1], dp[i + 1][0]);\n        }\n        \n        return dp[0][1];\n    }\n\n    public int maxProfitMemo(int[] prices) {\n        int n = prices.length;\n        int[][] memo = new int[n][2];\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            memo[i][0] = -1;\n            memo[i][1] = -1;\n        }\n        return memoSolve(0, 1, prices, memo);\n    }\n\n    private int memoSolve(int ind, int buy, int[] prices, int[][] memo) {\n        if (ind >= prices.length) return 0;\n        if (memo[ind][buy] != -1) return memo[ind][buy];\n        \n        if (buy == 1) {\n            return memo[ind][buy] = Math.max(-prices[ind] + memoSolve(ind + 1, 0, prices, memo), \n                                             memoSolve(ind + 1, 1, prices, memo));\n        } else {\n            return memo[ind][buy] = Math.max(prices[ind] + memoSolve(ind + 2, 1, prices, memo), \n                                             memoSolve(ind + 1, 0, prices, memo));\n        }\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"prices = [1, 2, 3, 0, 2]. dp table size is 7x2. Iterating backwards: Day 4 (price 2): Buy maxes at 0, Sell maxes at 2 (dp[4+2]=0). Day 3 (price 0): Buy maxes at 2 (-0 + dp[4][0]=2), Sell maxes at 0. Eventually at Day 0, the dp[0][1] evaluates the max of (skip day 0) or (buy day 0, resolving into the 3 profit path). Result is 3."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time Complexity: O(N) because the array is traversed backward exactly one time in Tabulation, evaluating state transitions instantly. Space Complexity: O(N) as we maintain a 2D matrix of size (N+2) x 2. (Note: This can technically be optimized to O(1) by explicitly tracking just the variables for Day i+1 and Day i+2, but maintaining the N+2 array is structurally safer and vastly easier to read for interviews).","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Identical to Stock II, but the Sell branch changes from `solve(ind + 1)` to `solve(ind + 2)`.\n- [ ] Because of the +2 jump, the base case MUST use `>= prices.length` to catch bounds overshoots."}]}', 
  '["The N+2 Array Trick: By sizing the Tabulation grid to `prices.length + 2`, we can write `dp[i + 2]` flawlessly without needing messy bounds-checking logic inside our main loop.","Zero-Dependency Initialization: Uses basic loops to fill the Memo matrix with -1 to prevent platform import crashes.","Maintains simple constant-time lookups into future state variables."]', '[]', '[]',
  'best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-with-cooldown
Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock with Cooldown
Dynamic Programming
State Machine DP
You are given an array `prices` where `prices[i]` is the price of a given stock on the `i-th` day.

Find the maximum profit you can achieve. You may complete as many transactions as you like (i.e., buy one and sell one share of the stock multiple times) with the following restrictions:
* After you sell your stock, you cannot buy stock on the next day (i.e., **cooldown one day**).

Note: You may not engage in multiple transactions simultaneously (i.e., you must sell the stock before you buy again).
An integer array `prices` representing daily stock prices.
An integer representing the maximum profit achievable.
Input: prices = [1,2,3,0,2]
Output: 3
Explanation: Transactions = [buy, sell, cooldown, buy, sell]. 
Buy on day 1 (price=1), sell on day 2 (price=2) -> Profit: 1. 
Day 3 is cooldown.
Buy on day 4 (price=0), sell on day 5 (price=2) -> Profit: 2. 
Total Profit: 3.
### Plain Recursion (State Tracking with Jump)
This operates almost identically to the unlimited transaction model (Stock II), but we introduce a strict penalty for selling. If we decide to sell on `Day i`, the rules state we cannot do anything on `Day i+1`. Therefore, when we execute a Sell branch, our recursive pointer must immediately jump to `Day i+2`.
1. Initialize `solve(ind, buy)` starting at day `0` with `buy = 1`.
2. **Base Cases**: Since selling can push our index past the end of the array via a `+2` jump, our base condition must be `if (ind >= prices.length)`, return `0`.
3. **Decisions**:
   - If `buy == 1`: Maximize between Buying (switches state to 0, moves to `ind + 1`) and Skipping (moves to `ind + 1`).
   - If `buy == 0`: Maximize between Selling (switches state to 1, **moves to `ind + 2`**) and Skipping (moves to `ind + 1`).
### Why is this DP?
1. **Overlapping Subproblems**: The recursive branches (especially the skips and cooldown jumps) will evaluate the exact same `(Day, CanBuy)` combinations repeatedly. Caching them prevents exponential pathing.
2. **Array Sizing Trick (N+2)**: When we transition from Memoization to Tabulation, we process the days backward. Because the `Sell` state looks ahead to `Day i+2`, we will trigger Out-Of-Bounds exceptions on the final two days if we use a standard `N` sized table. By allocating our DP matrix with size `N + 2`, we simulate an infinitely closed market past the final day, automatically handling all base case bounds effortlessly.
We provide both efficient implementations bundled securely together:
1. **Tabulation (Bottom-Up N+2 Grid)**: Initialize a DP table of size `[N + 2][2]` filled natively with 0s. Loop backward from `N - 1` down to `0`. If `buy == 1`, we reference `dp[i+1]`. If `buy == 0` (selling), we reference `dp[i+2]` directly. Return `dp[0][1]`.
2. **Memoization (Top-Down)**: Caches the `(ind, buy)` outcomes. We initialize the matrix to `-1` natively using double loops to guarantee zero external platform dependencies.
prices = [1, 2, 3, 0, 2]. dp table size is 7x2. Iterating backwards: Day 4 (price 2): Buy maxes at 0, Sell maxes at 2 (dp[4+2]=0). Day 3 (price 0): Buy maxes at 2 (-0 + dp[4][0]=2), Sell maxes at 0. Eventually at Day 0, the dp[0][1] evaluates the max of (skip day 0) or (buy day 0, resolving into the 3 profit path). Result is 3.
Time Complexity: O(N) because the array is traversed backward exactly one time in Tabulation, evaluating state transitions instantly. Space Complexity: O(N) as we maintain a 2D matrix of size (N+2) x 2. (Note: This can technically be optimized to O(1) by explicitly tracking just the variables for Day i+1 and Day i+2, but maintaining the N+2 array is structurally safer and vastly easier to read for interviews).
Identical to Stock II, but the Sell branch changes from `solve(ind + 1)` to `solve(ind + 2)`.
Because of the +2 jump, the base case MUST use `>= prices.length` to catch bounds overshoots.
The N+2 Array Trick: By sizing the Tabulation grid to `prices.length + 2`, we can write `dp[i + 2]` flawlessly without needing messy bounds-checking logic inside our main loop.
Zero-Dependency Initialization: Uses basic loops to fill the Memo matrix with -1 to prevent platform import crashes.
Maintains simple constant-time lookups into future state variables.', '2026-05-25T18:01:15.765Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'printing-longest-increasing-subsequence', 'problem', 'Printing Longest Increasing Subsequence', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/printing-longest-increasing-subsequence/1', 'medium', 'Dynamic Programming', 'DP on Subsequences (Path Reconstruction)',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given an integer array `arr`, return the strictly increasing subsequence of maximum length. If there are multiple longest increasing subsequences, you can return any of them.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn integer array `arr` and its length `n`.\n\n### Output\nAn array/list containing the elements of the longest increasing subsequence.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: n = 8, arr = [10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18]\nOutput: [2, 3, 7, 101]\nExplanation: The longest increasing subsequence is [2,3,7,101], therefore we return those exact elements.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Plain Recursion (Generating Lists)\nWe can explore every possible subsequence recursively. Instead of returning an integer representing the length, our recursive function returns an actual list of integers. We branch into \"take\" and \"skip\" paths, recursively build their respective lists, and return whichever list ended up being longer.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Define `solve(ind, prev_index)` which returns the `ArrayList` of the LIS starting from `ind`.\n2. **Base Case**: If `ind == arr.length`, return an empty list.\n3. **Decisions**:\n   - Skip: Recursively call for the next index without taking the current element.\n   - Take: If `prev_index == -1` OR `arr[ind] > arr[prev_index]`, create a new list, add the current element, and append the result of the recursive call.\n4. Return whichever list (`take` or `skip`) has the greater `.size()`.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public ArrayList<Integer> longestIncreasingSubsequence(int n, int arr[]) {\n        return solve(0, -1, arr);\n    }\n    private ArrayList<Integer> solve(int ind, int prev_index, int[] arr) {\n        if (ind == arr.length) return new ArrayList<>();\n        \n        ArrayList<Integer> skip = solve(ind + 1, prev_index, arr);\n        ArrayList<Integer> take = new ArrayList<>();\n        \n        if (prev_index == -1 || arr[ind] > arr[prev_index]) {\n            take.add(arr[ind]);\n            take.addAll(solve(ind + 1, ind, arr));\n        }\n        \n        return take.size() > skip.size() ? take : skip;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(2^N)","space":"O(N^2)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Why is this DP + Path Reconstruction?\n1. **The Hash Array Trick**: We run the exact same $O(N^2)$ 1D Tabulation DP to find the length of the LIS. However, we introduce a new array called `hash` (or parent). Whenever we update `dp[i]` because we found a valid `prev` element that increases our sequence, we log that relationship: `hash[i] = prev`.\n2. **Backtracking the Path**: Once the DP table is fully populated, we find the absolute maximum value in the `dp` array and its index (`lastIndex`). We then use the `hash` array like breadcrumbs, jumping from `hash[lastIndex]` backwards until we hit an element that is its own parent (`hash[i] == i`). Because we traced this path backwards, we simply reverse our final list to get the chronological subsequence.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. **Tabulation Setup**: Initialize `dp` and `hash` arrays of size `n`. Fill `dp` with 1, and set `hash[i] = i` for all indices (every element is initially a sequence of length 1 ending at itself).\n2. **Compute**: Loop through the array. For each `i`, loop `prev` from 0 to `i-1`. If `arr[prev] < arr[i]` AND `1 + dp[prev] > dp[i]`, update `dp[i]` and explicitly update `hash[i] = prev`.\n3. **Find the End**: Keep track of the `maxi` LIS length and the `lastIndex` where it occurred.\n4. **Reconstruct & Reverse**: Follow the `hash` array backwards from `lastIndex` adding elements to a temporary list. Once the while loop breaks, reverse the list and return it.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public ArrayList<Integer> longestIncreasingSubsequence(int n, int arr[]) {\n        int[] dp = new int[n];\n        int[] hash = new int[n];\n        int maxLIS = 1;\n        int lastIndex = 0;\n        \n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            hash[i] = i;\n            dp[i] = 1;\n            for (int prev = 0; prev < i; prev++) {\n                if (arr[prev] < arr[i] && 1 + dp[prev] > dp[i]) {\n                    dp[i] = 1 + dp[prev];\n                    hash[i] = prev;\n                }\n            }\n            if (dp[i] > maxLIS) {\n                maxLIS = dp[i];\n                lastIndex = i;\n            }\n        }\n        \n        ArrayList<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        result.add(arr[lastIndex]);\n        \n        while (hash[lastIndex] != lastIndex) {\n            lastIndex = hash[lastIndex];\n            result.add(arr[lastIndex]);\n        }\n        \n        // Zero-Dependency manual reverse to guarantee platform compatibility\n        int left = 0, right = result.size() - 1;\n        while (left < right) {\n            int temp = result.get(left);\n            result.set(left, result.get(right));\n            result.set(right, temp);\n            left++;\n            right--;\n        }\n        \n        return result;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N^2)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"arr = [5, 4, 11, 1, 16, 8]. The DP array evaluates max lengths. The hash array remembers the steps. When looking at 16 (index 4), it sees 11 (index 2) forms a longer chain, so hash[4] = 2. It also traces 11 back to 4 or 5. Backtracking from 16 follows hash[4] -> 2 -> hash[2] -> 0. Elements retrieved: 16, 11, 5. Reversed: 5, 11, 16."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time Complexity: O(N^2) as the primary engine is the standard double-loop LIS Tabulation. The backtracking while-loop takes at most O(N) time. Space Complexity: Strictly O(N) because we allocate two 1D primitive integer arrays (`dp` and `hash`) alongside the resulting `ArrayList`.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Recursive return types are upgraded from ''int'' to ''ArrayList''.\n- [ ] Generates massive memory overhead as a new sequence list is instantiated and copied at every single recursive branch."}]}', 
  '["The Hash Parent Strategy: By remembering *which* index provided the maximum subproblem value, we map a trace-route through our DP array.","End-Point Tracking: We must track `lastIndex` dynamically inside the outer loop, because the Longest Subsequence does not necessarily end at the very last element of the array.","Zero-Dependency Reverse: Using a manual two-pointer swap on the final ArrayList guarantees the code compiles on strict platforms without needing `java.util.Collections`."]', '[]', '[]',
  'printing-longest-increasing-subsequence
Printing Longest Increasing Subsequence
Dynamic Programming
DP on Subsequences (Path Reconstruction)
Given an integer array `arr`, return the strictly increasing subsequence of maximum length. If there are multiple longest increasing subsequences, you can return any of them.
An integer array `arr` and its length `n`.
An array/list containing the elements of the longest increasing subsequence.
Input: n = 8, arr = [10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18]
Output: [2, 3, 7, 101]
Explanation: The longest increasing subsequence is [2,3,7,101], therefore we return those exact elements.
### Plain Recursion (Generating Lists)
We can explore every possible subsequence recursively. Instead of returning an integer representing the length, our recursive function returns an actual list of integers. We branch into "take" and "skip" paths, recursively build their respective lists, and return whichever list ended up being longer.
1. Define `solve(ind, prev_index)` which returns the `ArrayList` of the LIS starting from `ind`.
2. **Base Case**: If `ind == arr.length`, return an empty list.
3. **Decisions**:
   - Skip: Recursively call for the next index without taking the current element.
   - Take: If `prev_index == -1` OR `arr[ind] > arr[prev_index]`, create a new list, add the current element, and append the result of the recursive call.
4. Return whichever list (`take` or `skip`) has the greater `.size()`.
### Why is this DP + Path Reconstruction?
1. **The Hash Array Trick**: We run the exact same $O(N^2)$ 1D Tabulation DP to find the length of the LIS. However, we introduce a new array called `hash` (or parent). Whenever we update `dp[i]` because we found a valid `prev` element that increases our sequence, we log that relationship: `hash[i] = prev`.
2. **Backtracking the Path**: Once the DP table is fully populated, we find the absolute maximum value in the `dp` array and its index (`lastIndex`). We then use the `hash` array like breadcrumbs, jumping from `hash[lastIndex]` backwards until we hit an element that is its own parent (`hash[i] == i`). Because we traced this path backwards, we simply reverse our final list to get the chronological subsequence.
1. **Tabulation Setup**: Initialize `dp` and `hash` arrays of size `n`. Fill `dp` with 1, and set `hash[i] = i` for all indices (every element is initially a sequence of length 1 ending at itself).
2. **Compute**: Loop through the array. For each `i`, loop `prev` from 0 to `i-1`. If `arr[prev] < arr[i]` AND `1 + dp[prev] > dp[i]`, update `dp[i]` and explicitly update `hash[i] = prev`.
3. **Find the End**: Keep track of the `maxi` LIS length and the `lastIndex` where it occurred.
4. **Reconstruct & Reverse**: Follow the `hash` array backwards from `lastIndex` adding elements to a temporary list. Once the while loop breaks, reverse the list and return it.
arr = [5, 4, 11, 1, 16, 8]. The DP array evaluates max lengths. The hash array remembers the steps. When looking at 16 (index 4), it sees 11 (index 2) forms a longer chain, so hash[4] = 2. It also traces 11 back to 4 or 5. Backtracking from 16 follows hash[4] -> 2 -> hash[2] -> 0. Elements retrieved: 16, 11, 5. Reversed: 5, 11, 16.
Time Complexity: O(N^2) as the primary engine is the standard double-loop LIS Tabulation. The backtracking while-loop takes at most O(N) time. Space Complexity: Strictly O(N) because we allocate two 1D primitive integer arrays (`dp` and `hash`) alongside the resulting `ArrayList`.
Recursive return types are upgraded from ''int'' to ''ArrayList''.
Generates massive memory overhead as a new sequence list is instantiated and copied at every single recursive branch.
The Hash Parent Strategy: By remembering *which* index provided the maximum subproblem value, we map a trace-route through our DP array.
End-Point Tracking: We must track `lastIndex` dynamically inside the outer loop, because the Longest Subsequence does not necessarily end at the very last element of the array.
Zero-Dependency Reverse: Using a manual two-pointer swap on the final ArrayList guarantees the code compiles on strict platforms without needing `java.util.Collections`.', '2026-05-27T17:20:45.376Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
  source, source_url, difficulty, topic, pattern,
  tags_json, content_json, revision_json, mistakes_json, visuals_json,
  search_text, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'longest-string-chain', 'problem', 'Longest String Chain', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-string-chain/', 'medium', 'Dynamic Programming', 'DP on Subsequences (LIS Variation)',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"You are given an array of `words` where each word consists of lowercase English letters.\n\n`wordA` is a predecessor of `wordB` if and only if we can insert exactly one letter anywhere in `wordA` without changing the order of the other characters to make it equal to `wordB`.\n\nA word chain is a sequence of words `[word_1, word_2, ..., word_k]` with `k >= 1`, where `word_1` is a predecessor of `word_2`, `word_2` is a predecessor of `word_3`, and so on. A single word is trivially a word chain with `k == 1`.\n\nReturn the length of the longest possible word chain with words chosen from the given list of words in any order.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn array of strings `words`.\n\n### Output\nAn integer representing the maximum length of a valid string chain.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: words = [\"a\",\"b\",\"ba\",\"bca\",\"bda\",\"bdca\"]\nOutput: 4\nExplanation: One of the longest word chains is [\"a\",\"ba\",\"bda\",\"bdca\"].","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Plain Recursion\nSince the problem states words can be chosen in *any order*, we first sort the array based on string length to impose a strict forward-moving sequence. From there, we explore every single branching path. We track the `prev_index`, and if the current word is a valid predecessor of the previous word, we can choose to ''take'' it. We branch into all valid take/skip configurations.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. **Sort the array** by string length so smaller words strictly come before larger words.\n2. Define `solve(ind, prev_index)` calculating the chain length starting from `ind`.\n3. **Base Case**: If `ind == words.length`, return `0`.\n4. **Decisions**:\n   - Skip: `solve(ind + 1, prev_index)`.\n   - Take: If `prev_index == -1` OR `checkPossible(words[ind], words[prev_index])` returns true, we can take the current word: `1 + solve(ind + 1, ind)`.\n5. Return the max of take and skip.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int longestStrChain(String[] words) {\n        Arrays.sort(words, (a, b) -> a.length() - b.length());\n        return solve(0, -1, words);\n    }\n    private int solve(int ind, int prev_index, String[] words) {\n        if (ind == words.length) return 0;\n        \n        int skip = solve(ind + 1, prev_index, words);\n        int take = 0;\n        \n        if (prev_index == -1 || checkPossible(words[ind], words[prev_index])) {\n            take = 1 + solve(ind + 1, ind, words);\n        }\n        \n        return Math.max(take, skip);\n    }\n    private boolean checkPossible(String s1, String s2) {\n        if (s1.length() != s2.length() + 1) return false;\n        int first = 0, second = 0;\n        while (first < s1.length()) {\n            if (second < s2.length() && s1.charAt(first) == s2.charAt(second)) {\n                first++;\n                second++;\n            } else {\n                first++;\n            }\n        }\n        return first == s1.length() && second == s2.length();\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N * logN + 2^N * L)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Why is this DP?\n1. **LIS Disguise**: Once the array is sorted by length, this problem evaluates structurally identically to the Longest Increasing Subsequence. We are looking for the longest chain of elements ending at `i`.\n2. **The Two-Pointer Helper**: To check if string `s2` is a predecessor of `s1`, `s1` must be exactly one character longer. We use a two-pointer approach to iterate through both strings. If characters match, we advance both. If they mismatch, we only advance the pointer on the larger string (`s1`). If we successfully reach the end of the smaller string, it is a valid predecessor!","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. **Sort `words`** by string length.\n2. Create a 1D `dp` array of size `N`, initialized to 1.\n3. Loop `i` through the words. For each `i`, look backward at `prev` from `0` to `i-1`.\n4. If `checkPossible(words[i], words[prev])` is true, we update `dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], 1 + dp[prev])`.\n5. Keep a running `maxLIS` variable to track the highest value generated in the `dp` array and return it.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int longestStrChain(String[] words) {\n        Arrays.sort(words, (a, b) -> a.length() - b.length());\n        int n = words.length;\n        int[] dp = new int[n];\n        int maxLIS = 1;\n        \n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            dp[i] = 1;\n            for (int prev = 0; prev < i; prev++) {\n                if (checkPossible(words[i], words[prev])) {\n                    dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], 1 + dp[prev]);\n                }\n            }\n            maxLIS = Math.max(maxLIS, dp[i]);\n        }\n        \n        return maxLIS;\n    }\n\n    public int longestStrChainMemo(String[] words) {\n        Arrays.sort(words, (a, b) -> a.length() - b.length());\n        int n = words.length;\n        int[][] memo = new int[n][n + 1];\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) {\n                memo[i][j] = -1;\n            }\n        }\n        return memoSolve(0, -1, words, memo);\n    }\n\n    private int memoSolve(int ind, int prev_index, String[] words, int[][] memo) {\n        if (ind == words.length) return 0;\n        if (memo[ind][prev_index + 1] != -1) return memo[ind][prev_index + 1];\n        \n        int skip = memoSolve(ind + 1, prev_index, words, memo);\n        int take = 0;\n        \n        if (prev_index == -1 || checkPossible(words[ind], words[prev_index])) {\n            take = 1 + memoSolve(ind + 1, ind, words, memo);\n        }\n        \n        return memo[ind][prev_index + 1] = Math.max(take, skip);\n    }\n\n    private boolean checkPossible(String s1, String s2) {\n        if (s1.length() != s2.length() + 1) return false;\n        int first = 0, second = 0;\n        while (first < s1.length()) {\n            if (second < s2.length() && s1.charAt(first) == s2.charAt(second)) {\n                first++;\n                second++;\n            } else {\n                first++;\n            }\n        }\n        return first == s1.length() && second == s2.length();\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N^2 * L)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"words = [\"a\",\"b\",\"ba\",\"bca\"]. Sorted by length: [\"a\", \"b\", \"ba\", \"bca\"].\n''a'' (dp=1)\n''b'' (dp=1)\n''ba'': ''a'' is predecessor -> dp=2. ''b'' is predecessor -> dp=2. \n''bca'': ''ba'' is predecessor -> dp=3.\nMax chain length found is 3."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time Complexity: Sorting takes O(N log N * L). The nested LIS loop evaluates N^2 pairs, and each string comparison takes O(L) time where L is the maximum length of a word. Total time is O(N^2 * L). Space Complexity: O(N) since we allocate a 1D tracking array representing the max chain ending at each word.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Unbounded order requires sorting the strings by length first to enforce a sequential pipeline.\n- [ ] Generates exponential paths analyzing every take/skip configuration."}]}', 
  '["LIS Copy-Paste: The entire core logic perfectly mirrors Longest Increasing Subsequence, merely replacing `nums[i] > nums[prev]` with `checkPossible(words[i], words[prev])`.","Two-Pointer Validation: The `checkPossible` helper moves the first pointer when a character mismatch occurs, successfully validating if exactly 1 character separates the two strings.","Zero-Dependency Memoization: Native nested loops insert `-1` into the evaluation matrix to bypass platform compilation errors."]', '[]', '[]',
  'longest-string-chain
Longest String Chain
Dynamic Programming
DP on Subsequences (LIS Variation)
You are given an array of `words` where each word consists of lowercase English letters.

`wordA` is a predecessor of `wordB` if and only if we can insert exactly one letter anywhere in `wordA` without changing the order of the other characters to make it equal to `wordB`.

A word chain is a sequence of words `[word_1, word_2, ..., word_k]` with `k >= 1`, where `word_1` is a predecessor of `word_2`, `word_2` is a predecessor of `word_3`, and so on. A single word is trivially a word chain with `k == 1`.

Return the length of the longest possible word chain with words chosen from the given list of words in any order.
An array of strings `words`.
An integer representing the maximum length of a valid string chain.
Input: words = ["a","b","ba","bca","bda","bdca"]
Output: 4
Explanation: One of the longest word chains is ["a","ba","bda","bdca"].
### Plain Recursion
Since the problem states words can be chosen in *any order*, we first sort the array based on string length to impose a strict forward-moving sequence. From there, we explore every single branching path. We track the `prev_index`, and if the current word is a valid predecessor of the previous word, we can choose to ''take'' it. We branch into all valid take/skip configurations.
1. **Sort the array** by string length so smaller words strictly come before larger words.
2. Define `solve(ind, prev_index)` calculating the chain length starting from `ind`.
3. **Base Case**: If `ind == words.length`, return `0`.
4. **Decisions**:
   - Skip: `solve(ind + 1, prev_index)`.
   - Take: If `prev_index == -1` OR `checkPossible(words[ind], words[prev_index])` returns true, we can take the current word: `1 + solve(ind + 1, ind)`.
5. Return the max of take and skip.
### Why is this DP?
1. **LIS Disguise**: Once the array is sorted by length, this problem evaluates structurally identically to the Longest Increasing Subsequence. We are looking for the longest chain of elements ending at `i`.
2. **The Two-Pointer Helper**: To check if string `s2` is a predecessor of `s1`, `s1` must be exactly one character longer. We use a two-pointer approach to iterate through both strings. If characters match, we advance both. If they mismatch, we only advance the pointer on the larger string (`s1`). If we successfully reach the end of the smaller string, it is a valid predecessor!
1. **Sort `words`** by string length.
2. Create a 1D `dp` array of size `N`, initialized to 1.
3. Loop `i` through the words. For each `i`, look backward at `prev` from `0` to `i-1`.
4. If `checkPossible(words[i], words[prev])` is true, we update `dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], 1 + dp[prev])`.
5. Keep a running `maxLIS` variable to track the highest value generated in the `dp` array and return it.
words = ["a","b","ba","bca"]. Sorted by length: ["a", "b", "ba", "bca"].
''a'' (dp=1)
''b'' (dp=1)
''ba'': ''a'' is predecessor -> dp=2. ''b'' is predecessor -> dp=2. 
''bca'': ''ba'' is predecessor -> dp=3.
Max chain length found is 3.
Time Complexity: Sorting takes O(N log N * L). The nested LIS loop evaluates N^2 pairs, and each string comparison takes O(L) time where L is the maximum length of a word. Total time is O(N^2 * L). Space Complexity: O(N) since we allocate a 1D tracking array representing the max chain ending at each word.
Unbounded order requires sorting the strings by length first to enforce a sequential pipeline.
Generates exponential paths analyzing every take/skip configuration.
LIS Copy-Paste: The entire core logic perfectly mirrors Longest Increasing Subsequence, merely replacing `nums[i] > nums[prev]` with `checkPossible(words[i], words[prev])`.
Two-Pointer Validation: The `checkPossible` helper moves the first pointer when a character mismatch occurs, successfully validating if exactly 1 character separates the two strings.
Zero-Dependency Memoization: Native nested loops insert `-1` into the evaluation matrix to bypass platform compilation errors.', '2026-05-27T18:41:07.760Z'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_items (
  slug, type, title, summary, status, visibility,
