  'palindrome-partitioning-ii', 'problem', 'Palindrome Partitioning II', '', 'revision-ready', 'public',
  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/palindrome-partitioning-ii/', 'medium', 'Dynamic Programming', 'Front Partitioning (MCM Variation)',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given a string `s`, partition `s` such that every substring of the partition is a palindrome.\n\nReturn the minimum cuts needed for a palindrome partitioning of `s`.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nA string `s` consisting of lowercase English letters.\n\n### Output\nAn integer representing the minimum number of cuts required.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: s = \"aab\"\nOutput: 1\nExplanation: The palindrome partitioning [\"aa\",\"b\"] could be produced using 1 cut.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Plain Recursion (Front Partitioning)\nWe start at the beginning of the string. We try every possible prefix substring by moving a pointer `j` from `i` to the end of the string. If the substring from `i` to `j` is a palindrome, we can safely make a cut right after `j` and recursively ask the function to find the minimum cuts for the remaining suffix `s[j+1 ... n-1]`. We try all valid palindromic prefixes and return the absolute minimum.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Define `solve(i)` which returns the minimum partitions needed for the substring starting at index `i`.\n2. **Base Case**: If `i == s.length()`, we have exhausted the string, return `0`.\n3. **Decisions**: Loop `j` from `i` to `s.length() - 1`.\n4. If `s[i...j]` is a palindrome, calculate `cost = 1 + solve(j + 1)`.\n5. Keep track of the minimum cost across all loops and return it.\n6. **Final Adjustment**: A string split into 2 palindromes has 1 cut, but our logic adds a cut after every valid segment (evaluating to 2). We simply return `solve(0) - 1` to offset the imaginary trailing cut at the end of the string.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int minCut(String s) {\n        return solve(0, s) - 1;\n    }\n    private int solve(int i, String s) {\n        if (i == s.length()) return 0;\n        \n        int minCuts = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n        for (int j = i; j < s.length(); j++) {\n            if (isPalindrome(s, i, j)) {\n                int cost = 1 + solve(j + 1, s);\n                minCuts = Math.min(minCuts, cost);\n            }\n        }\n        return minCuts;\n    }\n    private boolean isPalindrome(String s, int left, int right) {\n        while (left < right) {\n            if (s.charAt(left++) != s.charAt(right--)) return false;\n        }\n        return true;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(2^N)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Why is this DP?\n1. **Overlapping Subproblems**: The recursive tree frequently re-evaluates the same suffix substrings `s[j+1 ... n-1]`. We can cache the minimum cuts for every starting index `i` to stop the exponential branching.\n2. **Front Partitioning Strategy**: Instead of Matrix Chain Multiplication''s complex 2D intervals `(i, j)`, we only need a 1D state `(i)`. Because we linearly consume the string from left to right, we strictly ask: \"What is the minimum cost to partition the suffix starting at `i`?\"","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"We provide both standard DP implementations bundled together:\n1. **Tabulation (1D Array)**: We initialize a `dp` array of size `N + 1`. We work backwards from `N - 1` down to `0`. For each `i`, we look forward using `j`. If `s[i...j]` is a palindrome, we update `dp[i]` with the minimum of its current value and `1 + dp[j + 1]`. Finally, return `dp[0] - 1`.\n2. **Memoization (Top-Down)**: Caches the `solve(i)` results into a 1D `memo` array initialized natively to `-1` to ensure zero platform dependency issues.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int minCut(String s) {\n        int n = s.length();\n        int[] dp = new int[n + 1];\n        \n        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n            int minCuts = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n                if (isPalindrome(s, i, j)) {\n                    int cost = 1 + dp[j + 1];\n                    minCuts = Math.min(minCuts, cost);\n                }\n            }\n            dp[i] = minCuts;\n        }\n        \n        return dp[0] - 1;\n    }\n\n    public int minCutMemo(String s) {\n        int n = s.length();\n        int[] memo = new int[n];\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            memo[i] = -1;\n        }\n        return memoSolve(0, s, memo) - 1;\n    }\n\n    private int memoSolve(int i, String s, int[] memo) {\n        if (i == s.length()) return 0;\n        if (memo[i] != -1) return memo[i];\n        \n        int minCuts = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n        for (int j = i; j < s.length(); j++) {\n            if (isPalindrome(s, i, j)) {\n                int cost = 1 + memoSolve(j + 1, s, memo);\n                minCuts = Math.min(minCuts, cost);\n            }\n        }\n        return memo[i] = minCuts;\n    }\n\n    private boolean isPalindrome(String s, int left, int right) {\n        while (left < right) {\n            if (s.charAt(left++) != s.charAt(right--)) return false;\n        }\n        return true;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(N^3)","space":"O(N)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"s = \"aab\". We evaluate backwards.\nIndex 2 (''b''): Is palindrome. Cost = 1 + dp[3] (0) = 1. dp[2] = 1.\nIndex 1 (''a''): ''a'' is palindrome, cost 1 + dp[2] (1) = 2. ''ab'' is not. dp[1] = 2.\nIndex 0 (''a''): ''a'' is palindrome, cost 1 + dp[1] (2) = 3. ''aa'' is palindrome, cost 1 + dp[2] (1) = 2. ''aab'' is not. dp[0] = 2.\nReturn dp[0] - 1 = 2 - 1 = 1."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time Complexity: O(N^3) because we have a double loop traversing the string evaluating every substring O(N^2), and inside the loop, the `isPalindrome` helper takes O(N) to check validity. *(Note: This can be optimized to strictly O(N^2) by precomputing palindromes in a 2D boolean grid, but the O(N^3) Front Partition framework is the core pattern.)* Space Complexity: strictly O(N) for our 1D DP tracking array.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Front Partition logic moves pointer ''j'' forward to find valid chunks, recursively parsing whatever remains.\n- [ ] Must subtract 1 from the final result because the logic inherently adds a cut at the very end of the final segment."}]}', 
  '["1D MCM Flattening: Unlike bursting balloons or standard Matrix Chain Multiplication, front partitioning only requires 1 parameter (the starting index), compressing tracking space down to O(N).","Base Case Offset: A completely valid palindrome will execute `1 + dp[n]`. Since `dp[n]` is 0, the total cost evaluates to 1, causing the need to `return dp[0] - 1` globally.","Zero-Dependency memo array initialization utilizes a basic loop targeting `-1`."]', '[]', '[]',
  'palindrome-partitioning-ii
Palindrome Partitioning II
Dynamic Programming
Front Partitioning (MCM Variation)
Given a string `s`, partition `s` such that every substring of the partition is a palindrome.

Return the minimum cuts needed for a palindrome partitioning of `s`.
A string `s` consisting of lowercase English letters.
An integer representing the minimum number of cuts required.
Input: s = "aab"
Output: 1
Explanation: The palindrome partitioning ["aa","b"] could be produced using 1 cut.
### Plain Recursion (Front Partitioning)
We start at the beginning of the string. We try every possible prefix substring by moving a pointer `j` from `i` to the end of the string. If the substring from `i` to `j` is a palindrome, we can safely make a cut right after `j` and recursively ask the function to find the minimum cuts for the remaining suffix `s[j+1 ... n-1]`. We try all valid palindromic prefixes and return the absolute minimum.
1. Define `solve(i)` which returns the minimum partitions needed for the substring starting at index `i`.
2. **Base Case**: If `i == s.length()`, we have exhausted the string, return `0`.
3. **Decisions**: Loop `j` from `i` to `s.length() - 1`.
4. If `s[i...j]` is a palindrome, calculate `cost = 1 + solve(j + 1)`.
5. Keep track of the minimum cost across all loops and return it.
6. **Final Adjustment**: A string split into 2 palindromes has 1 cut, but our logic adds a cut after every valid segment (evaluating to 2). We simply return `solve(0) - 1` to offset the imaginary trailing cut at the end of the string.
### Why is this DP?
1. **Overlapping Subproblems**: The recursive tree frequently re-evaluates the same suffix substrings `s[j+1 ... n-1]`. We can cache the minimum cuts for every starting index `i` to stop the exponential branching.
2. **Front Partitioning Strategy**: Instead of Matrix Chain Multiplication''s complex 2D intervals `(i, j)`, we only need a 1D state `(i)`. Because we linearly consume the string from left to right, we strictly ask: "What is the minimum cost to partition the suffix starting at `i`?"
We provide both standard DP implementations bundled together:
1. **Tabulation (1D Array)**: We initialize a `dp` array of size `N + 1`. We work backwards from `N - 1` down to `0`. For each `i`, we look forward using `j`. If `s[i...j]` is a palindrome, we update `dp[i]` with the minimum of its current value and `1 + dp[j + 1]`. Finally, return `dp[0] - 1`.
2. **Memoization (Top-Down)**: Caches the `solve(i)` results into a 1D `memo` array initialized natively to `-1` to ensure zero platform dependency issues.
s = "aab". We evaluate backwards.
Index 2 (''b''): Is palindrome. Cost = 1 + dp[3] (0) = 1. dp[2] = 1.
Index 1 (''a''): ''a'' is palindrome, cost 1 + dp[2] (1) = 2. ''ab'' is not. dp[1] = 2.
Index 0 (''a''): ''a'' is palindrome, cost 1 + dp[1] (2) = 3. ''aa'' is palindrome, cost 1 + dp[2] (1) = 2. ''aab'' is not. dp[0] = 2.
Return dp[0] - 1 = 2 - 1 = 1.
Time Complexity: O(N^3) because we have a double loop traversing the string evaluating every substring O(N^2), and inside the loop, the `isPalindrome` helper takes O(N) to check validity. *(Note: This can be optimized to strictly O(N^2) by precomputing palindromes in a 2D boolean grid, but the O(N^3) Front Partition framework is the core pattern.)* Space Complexity: strictly O(N) for our 1D DP tracking array.
Front Partition logic moves pointer ''j'' forward to find valid chunks, recursively parsing whatever remains.
Must subtract 1 from the final result because the logic inherently adds a cut at the very end of the final segment.
1D MCM Flattening: Unlike bursting balloons or standard Matrix Chain Multiplication, front partitioning only requires 1 parameter (the starting index), compressing tracking space down to O(N).
Base Case Offset: A completely valid palindrome will execute `1 + dp[n]`. Since `dp[n]` is 0, the total cost evaluates to 1, causing the need to `return dp[0] - 1` globally.
Zero-Dependency memo array initialization utilizes a basic loop targeting `-1`.', '2026-05-30T07:34:05.739Z'
);

