  'leetcode', 'https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-cost-to-cut-a-stick/', 'medium', 'Dynamic Programming', 'Matrix Chain Multiplication (MCM) / Interval DP',
  '[]', '{"format":"sections","sections":[{"id":"statement","title":"Problem Statement","kind":"problem-statement","bodyMd":"Given a wooden stick of length `n` units. The stick is labelled from `0` to `n`.\n\nGiven an integer array `cuts` where `cuts[i]` denotes a position you should perform a cut at.\n\nYou should perform the cuts in order, you can change the order of the cuts as you wish.\n\nThe cost of one cut is the length of the stick to be cut, the total cost is the sum of costs of all cuts. When you cut a stick, it will be split into two smaller sticks (i.e. the sum of lengths of the two smaller sticks is equal to the length of the stick before cut).\n\nReturn the minimum total cost of the cuts.","visuals":[]},{"id":"input-output","title":"Input / Output","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Input\nAn integer `n` representing the initial stick length, and an integer array `cuts` representing the positions to make cuts.\n\n### Output\nAn integer representing the minimum total cost to complete all cuts.","visuals":[]},{"id":"example","title":"Example","kind":"example","bodyMd":"Input: n = 7, cuts = [1,3,4,5]\nOutput: 16\nExplanation: Using cuts order [3, 5, 1, 4] yields:\n- Cut at 3: stick of length 7 is cut into [0..3] and [3..7]. Cost = 7.\n- Cut at 5: stick of length 4 ([3..7]) is cut into [3..5] and [5..7]. Cost = 4.\n- Cut at 1: stick of length 3 ([0..3]) is cut into [0..1] and [1..3]. Cost = 3.\n- Cut at 4: stick of length 2 ([3..5]) is cut into [3..4] and [4..5]. Cost = 2.\nTotal cost = 7 + 4 + 3 + 2 = 16.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-intuition","title":"Brute Force Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Plain Recursion (Interval Partitioning)\nTo solve this, we can think of each cut as breaking a larger interval down into independent left and right sub-intervals. If we sort the cuts array and pad it with the original stick boundaries (`0` and `n`), any stick segment can be described strictly by its boundaries in this padded array. To find the optimal first cut for any given segment, we test all available cut points inside that segment, calculate the cost of making that cut (which is the current segment''s length), and recursively solve for the resulting left and right segments.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-approach","title":"Brute Force Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"1. Pad the `cuts` array by adding `0` at the beginning and `n` at the end, then sort it to ensure cuts are sequential.\n2. Define `solve(i, j)` representing the minimum cost to perform all available cuts from index `i` to `j` in our padded array.\n3. **Base Case**: If `i > j`, no cuts are left to make within this segment, so return `0`.\n4. **Decisions**: Loop an index `k` from `i` to `j` representing our choice for the current cut.\n5. The cost of making cut `k` is the length of the current segment: `c[j + 1] - c[i - 1]`.\n6. Add the recursive costs of the left chunk `solve(i, k - 1)` and right chunk `solve(k + 1, j)`, tracking the minimum sum found.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Brute Force Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int minCost(int n, int[] cuts) {\n        int m = cuts.length;\n        int[] c = new int[m + 2];\n        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {\n            c[i + 1] = cuts[i];\n        }\n        c[0] = 0;\n        c[m + 1] = n;\n        java.util.Arrays.sort(c);\n        \n        return solve(1, m, c);\n    }\n    \n    private int solve(int i, int j, int[] c) {\n        if (i > j) return 0;\n        \n        int minCost = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n        for (int k = i; k <= j; k++) {\n            int cost = (c[j + 1] - c[i - 1]) \n                       + solve(i, k - 1, c) \n                       + solve(k + 1, j, c);\n            minCost = Math.min(minCost, cost);\n        }\n        return minCost;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(2^M)","space":"O(M)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-intuition","title":"Optimal Intuition","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"### Why is this DP?\n1. **Overlapping Subproblems**: When evaluating different sequences of cuts, we frequently encounter identical sub-segments (e.g., finding the best way to cut the segment between index `i` and index `j` is evaluated multiple times across different execution branches). Caching these interval combinations stops exponential branching.\n2. **Matrix Chain Multiplication (MCM) Structure**: The problem naturally fits the Interval DP format. We can process sub-segments bottom-up by increasing the interval length, ensuring that whenever we evaluate an interval `(i, j)`, all its smaller component sub-segments `(i, k-1)` and `(k+1, j)` have already been perfectly optimized.","visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-approach","title":"Optimal Approach","kind":"solution","bodyMd":"We provide both powerful DP implementations bundled together:\n1. **Tabulation (Bottom-Up 2D Grid)**: We use a 2D `dp` array where `dp[i][j]` stores the min cost for cuts from `i` to `j`. We loop the left boundary `i` backwards from `m` down to 1, and the right boundary `j` forward from `i` up to `m`. This order guarantees smaller sub-intervals are computed before larger ones. A nested loop `k` evaluates the optimal cut point within `(i, j)`.\n2. **Memoization (Top-Down)**: Caches the `(i, j)` boundaries within a 2D `memo` matrix. We natively initialize the cells to `-1` using basic nested loops to ensure zero external platform import crashes.","codeBlocks":[{"language":"java","title":"Optimal Java","code":"class Solution {\n    public int minCost(int n, int[] cuts) {\n        int m = cuts.length;\n        int[] c = new int[m + 2];\n        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {\n            c[i + 1] = cuts[i];\n        }\n        c[0] = 0;\n        c[m + 1] = n;\n        java.util.Arrays.sort(c);\n        \n        int[][] dp = new int[m + 2][m + 2];\n        \n        // Bottom-Up Tabulation loops\n        for (int i = m; i >= 1; i--) {\n            for (int j = i; j <= m; j++) {\n                int minCost = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n                for (int k = i; k <= j; k++) {\n                    int cost = (c[j + 1] - c[i - 1]) + dp[i][k - 1] + dp[k + 1][j];\n                    minCost = Math.min(minCost, cost);\n                }\n                dp[i][j] = minCost;\n            } \n        }\n        \n        return dp[1][m];\n    }\n    \n    public int minCostMemo(int n, int[] cuts) {\n        int m = cuts.length;\n        int[] c = new int[m + 2];\n        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {\n            c[i + 1] = cuts[i];\n        }\n        c[0] = 0;\n        c[m + 1] = n;\n        java.util.Arrays.sort(c);\n        \n        int[][] memo = new int[m + 2][m + 2];\n        for (int i = 0; i <= m + 1; i++) {\n            for (int j = 0; j <= m + 1; j++) {\n                memo[i][j] = -1;\n            }\n        }\n        \n        return memoSolve(1, m, c, memo);\n    } \n    \n    private int memoSolve(int i, int j, int[] c, int[][] memo) {\n        if (i > j) return 0;\n        if (memo[i][j] != -1) return memo[i][j];\n        \n        int minCost = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n        for (int k = i; k <= j; k++) {\n            int cost = (c[j + 1] - c[i - 1]) \n                       + memoSolve(i, k - 1, c, memo) \n                       + memoSolve(k + 1, j, c, memo);\n            minCost = Math.min(minCost, cost);\n        }\n        return memo[i][j] = minCost;\n    }\n}"}],"metadata":{"time":"O(M^3)","space":"O(M^2)"},"visuals":[]},{"id":"optimal-dry-run","title":"Optimal Dry Run","kind":"dry-run","bodyMd":"n = 7, padded cuts c = [0, 1, 3, 4, 5, 7]. For interval from index 1 (val 1) to index 4 (val 5), the parent stick boundaries are c[0]=0 and c[5]=7, meaning length is 7 - 0 = 7. We test partitioning at each k (1, 3, 4, 5) and add the pre-computed costs of their sub-segments from our DP table, minimizing the total cost."},{"id":"optimal-complexity","title":"Optimal Complexity","kind":"markdown","bodyMd":"Time Complexity: O(M^3) where M is the number of cuts. There are O(M^2) distinct interval states, and computing each state requires an internal loop running up to M times to locate the optimal cut point. Space Complexity: O(M^2) to allocate the 2D matrix tracking our interval state configurations.","visuals":[]},{"id":"brute-quick-revision","title":"Brute Force Quick Revision","kind":"checklist","bodyMd":"- [ ] Sort and pad the cuts array with 0 and n to cleanly track stick segment boundaries.\n- [ ] Recursively splits intervals at each valid cut, adding the length of the current parent stick segment as the step cost."}]}', 
  '["Classic Matrix Chain Multiplication (MCM) pattern applied directly to physical stick segments.","Tabulation loop configuration (`i` down from `m`, `j` up from `i`) guarantees smaller dependent intervals are evaluated before larger containing blocks.","Zero-Dependency Initialization handles formatting natively via basic loops, bypassing unexpected environment platform issues."]', '[]', '[]',
  'minimum-cost-to-cut-a-stick
Minimum Cost to Cut a Stick
Dynamic Programming
Matrix Chain Multiplication (MCM) / Interval DP
Given a wooden stick of length `n` units. The stick is labelled from `0` to `n`.

Given an integer array `cuts` where `cuts[i]` denotes a position you should perform a cut at.

You should perform the cuts in order, you can change the order of the cuts as you wish.

The cost of one cut is the length of the stick to be cut, the total cost is the sum of costs of all cuts. When you cut a stick, it will be split into two smaller sticks (i.e. the sum of lengths of the two smaller sticks is equal to the length of the stick before cut).

Return the minimum total cost of the cuts.
An integer `n` representing the initial stick length, and an integer array `cuts` representing the positions to make cuts.
An integer representing the minimum total cost to complete all cuts.
Input: n = 7, cuts = [1,3,4,5]
Output: 16
Explanation: Using cuts order [3, 5, 1, 4] yields:
- Cut at 3: stick of length 7 is cut into [0..3] and [3..7]. Cost = 7.
- Cut at 5: stick of length 4 ([3..7]) is cut into [3..5] and [5..7]. Cost = 4.
- Cut at 1: stick of length 3 ([0..3]) is cut into [0..1] and [1..3]. Cost = 3.
- Cut at 4: stick of length 2 ([3..5]) is cut into [3..4] and [4..5]. Cost = 2.
Total cost = 7 + 4 + 3 + 2 = 16.
### Plain Recursion (Interval Partitioning)
To solve this, we can think of each cut as breaking a larger interval down into independent left and right sub-intervals. If we sort the cuts array and pad it with the original stick boundaries (`0` and `n`), any stick segment can be described strictly by its boundaries in this padded array. To find the optimal first cut for any given segment, we test all available cut points inside that segment, calculate the cost of making that cut (which is the current segment''s length), and recursively solve for the resulting left and right segments.
1. Pad the `cuts` array by adding `0` at the beginning and `n` at the end, then sort it to ensure cuts are sequential.
2. Define `solve(i, j)` representing the minimum cost to perform all available cuts from index `i` to `j` in our padded array.
3. **Base Case**: If `i > j`, no cuts are left to make within this segment, so return `0`.
4. **Decisions**: Loop an index `k` from `i` to `j` representing our choice for the current cut.
5. The cost of making cut `k` is the length of the current segment: `c[j + 1] - c[i - 1]`.
6. Add the recursive costs of the left chunk `solve(i, k - 1)` and right chunk `solve(k + 1, j)`, tracking the minimum sum found.
### Why is this DP?
1. **Overlapping Subproblems**: When evaluating different sequences of cuts, we frequently encounter identical sub-segments (e.g., finding the best way to cut the segment between index `i` and index `j` is evaluated multiple times across different execution branches). Caching these interval combinations stops exponential branching.
2. **Matrix Chain Multiplication (MCM) Structure**: The problem naturally fits the Interval DP format. We can process sub-segments bottom-up by increasing the interval length, ensuring that whenever we evaluate an interval `(i, j)`, all its smaller component sub-segments `(i, k-1)` and `(k+1, j)` have already been perfectly optimized.
We provide both powerful DP implementations bundled together:
1. **Tabulation (Bottom-Up 2D Grid)**: We use a 2D `dp` array where `dp[i][j]` stores the min cost for cuts from `i` to `j`. We loop the left boundary `i` backwards from `m` down to 1, and the right boundary `j` forward from `i` up to `m`. This order guarantees smaller sub-intervals are computed before larger ones. A nested loop `k` evaluates the optimal cut point within `(i, j)`.
2. **Memoization (Top-Down)**: Caches the `(i, j)` boundaries within a 2D `memo` matrix. We natively initialize the cells to `-1` using basic nested loops to ensure zero external platform import crashes.
n = 7, padded cuts c = [0, 1, 3, 4, 5, 7]. For interval from index 1 (val 1) to index 4 (val 5), the parent stick boundaries are c[0]=0 and c[5]=7, meaning length is 7 - 0 = 7. We test partitioning at each k (1, 3, 4, 5) and add the pre-computed costs of their sub-segments from our DP table, minimizing the total cost.
Time Complexity: O(M^3) where M is the number of cuts. There are O(M^2) distinct interval states, and computing each state requires an internal loop running up to M times to locate the optimal cut point. Space Complexity: O(M^2) to allocate the 2D matrix tracking our interval state configurations.
Sort and pad the cuts array with 0 and n to cleanly track stick segment boundaries.
Recursively splits intervals at each valid cut, adding the length of the current parent stick segment as the step cost.
Classic Matrix Chain Multiplication (MCM) pattern applied directly to physical stick segments.
Tabulation loop configuration (`i` down from `m`, `j` up from `i`) guarantees smaller dependent intervals are evaluated before larger containing blocks.
Zero-Dependency Initialization handles formatting natively via basic loops, bypassing unexpected environment platform issues.', '2026-05-30T08:59:23.235Z'
);

